/* generated by tools/i18n_build.py — 20 + 21 chapters. Edit tools/i18n/*.json, not this file. */
if (window.I18N) I18N.pack("VOLS",{"motion":{"chapters":[{"id":"mo0","title":"Main rules","html":"<ol><li><strong>If it is better redrawn, redraw it.<\/strong> A pose/shape the rig cannot reach cleanly is redrawn (pose-space or a new drawing), not fixed by moving points.<\/li><li><strong>Check EVERY frame, not the key poses.<\/strong> gapcheck (holes + loose pieces) on every frame, a sweep per joint, a strip at 1/8 cycle.<\/li><li><strong>A regression sheet with every delivery<\/strong> (vector-character chapter 11.2): one fix must not silently break another part.<\/li><li><strong>Measure the motion<\/strong>: joint angle jumps per frame, leg reach (max 99%), foot slide (0.000 H), spring resonance.<\/li><li><strong>Be honest<\/strong>: the feel of motion (choppy, stiff) shows most in video; write down what cannot be confirmed yet.<\/li><li><strong>Motion numbers from data + character size<\/strong>: take gait numbers from a source (write the source and page), then convert them with formulas to the character's size. Assumptions are written as assumptions (for example a subject height not given in the source). When the user asks to \"start from 0\", do not use old project numbers as defaults.<\/li><li><strong>A shifted still drawing = sliding.<\/strong> At a camera angle that has no rig yet, the character looks like it slides and its shadow is dead (Review Results, test character animatic v1). Label it clearly in the animatic as a placeholder, and always compute the character shadow from the 3D pose (chapter 5.3).<\/li><li><strong>Judge in the scene frame, stop when it gets worse.<\/strong> Rig/drawing changes are judged in the scene frame (final camera distance, critical angle) side by side with the accepted version, at every step. When working on your own (\"fix everything without confirmation\"), work item by item and check the scene frame after each item; as soon as one frame looks worse, stop, report, and send a comparison. (Review Results, rig test project v4: fixes piled up, checked only on the angle sheet, the orbit frame was \"wrecked\".)<\/li><li><strong>Reply in the user's language.<\/strong> For Indonesian users, write fully in Bahasa Indonesia; when you use the user-facing templates of motion-graphics-pro (T1 brief, gate messages, reports), take the ID variants in <code>motion-graphics-pro/references/template.id.md<\/code>.<\/li><\/ol>"},{"id":"mo1","title":"Required workflow (before making anything)","html":"<p>Every learning source in chapter 1 (videos, taste notes) is a REFERENCE, not a style to copy exactly. Take the principles (taste, motion, timing, transitions), then combine them to fit the brief. Do not copy the characters, logos or signature compositions of the reference works.<\/p>\n<ol><li>Ask for the brief first (AskUserQuestion + the questions also written in chat): goal/main message, audience, platform and aspect ratio, duration, required brand/palette/fonts, available assets (logo, characters, footage, photos), text/narration/voice over, music/beat, deadline, examples liked and disliked.<\/li><li>Recommend 1 style that best fits the brief + the reasons.<\/li><li>Give a side-by-side comparison of 2-3 styles in a table. Columns: style, source reference, palette, shape/character, motion and timing, transitions, typography, strengths, weaknesses, best for. Then explain the pros and cons of each option one by one.<\/li><li>After the user chooses, confirm the style elements one by one (OK or not): colours (hex), typography, drawing/character style, motion (fps, on twos/fours, easing, intensity), transitions, camera, effects (grain, blur, glow), pacing and duration structure, audio, output format. Do not guess unconfirmed elements.<\/li><li>Only then produce in stages: style frame (still) → strip/animatic → full render. Send each stage for Review Results.<\/li><li>When the user is away (unattended) and the script is complete (style, location, shots, timing written down), the script = the brief. Write down the decisions taken at the start, then work without waiting.<\/li><li>A brief answer that can be read two ways (for example \"a comparison of all three\", \"start from 0 without defaults\", \"further away for every take\") is written out with both readings and asked about. Do not guess. When the answer is a visual number (camera distance, character size), send a comparison sheet (for example 8/10/12/14 H with the character height in percent), then ask.<\/li><li>\"Style comparison\" means visual examples (a style frame + a short clip per style), not only a table.<\/li><li>One-take camera video:<ul><li>write the beats (character action, camera move);<\/li><li>map the existing assets vs those still to make (drawing angles, rig, background);<\/li><li>offer technique options (number of drawing angles for the orbit, multi-angle rig method);<\/li><li>ask the orbit direction per beat with a table of the angles passed + the number of rigs per option (angles relative to the character: 0 front, 90R right side, 180 back, 90L);<\/li><li>agree on multi-session stages: background research, 2-3 style frames, animatic, angle drawings + rig, render. Large productions start in a new session with a handover pack.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>One-take animatic (Review Results, test project):<ul><li>propose a timing table per beat (time, duration, character action, camera, speed) and get approval first;<\/li><li>render at 24 fps without motion blur, labels in the frame: beat, time, camera angle, drawing/rig used, action status (not rigged yet), timeline;<\/li><li>send the MP4 + a sheet of 12-15 key times; write down the visible weaknesses (drawings jumping between angles, actions not rigged yet).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>File delivery: PNGs are sent as renders, MP4s and zips as attachments. When the user asks \"where is it?\", resend each file as a separate card with the file name in the caption, then repeat the question.<\/li><\/ol>"},{"id":"mo2","title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li>Adrianne Walujo (Adobe APAC): separate named layers; CC Bend It for leaves; loopOut(); Wave Warp for water; night variant = swap colours, keep the layers.<\/li><li>Dody Priyatmono: anchor at the joint (Pan Behind); leg rotation 0-12-24 f; Easy Ease; Puppet Pin on knees/elbows; parent null + squash; Time Remap loopOut(\"cycle\"); background moving the opposite way; Gaussian Blur for depth.<\/li><li>Jess (Envato) and Audrey (School of Motion): round joints, rotation anchor at the centre of the joint circle (R), parenting (face &gt; head &gt; neck &gt; torso; pupil &gt; eye white; fingers &gt; palm &gt; forearm &gt; upper arm &gt; torso), big motion first, then detail, then secondary. Hands and mouths = swap sets.<\/li><li>Ben Marriott: blocking hold keys → easing → secondary; Graph Editor anticipation; Posterize Time 12 fps (on twos); Turbulent Displace boil (random seed time*4); legs as shape paths + a knee point; retime with Alt-drag; rough pass of lines + circles; noise 8% + wiggle 1.5 px 6x/second. Burnt Toast: action 20 f, path keys every frame, 3-shape shading, 1-frame smear.<\/li><li>AE Puppet Pin: animate only the pins that need it; loopOut(\"pingpong\"); different part durations and reversed direction (Time-Reverse) to avoid unison.<\/li><li>Swaying wave: mask the free part + Wave Warp, pin the attached edge.<\/li><li>SonduckFilm (Cinematic Motion Graphic Stories): 2D layers lit in 3D space, wind wave warp, particles, 360 orbit through a null, DOF + vignette + noise.<\/li><li>edwin rowin (Jammin' Out): rig from the hips without IK, on twos/fours, head turn with arc + hold, motion never stops completely, edge roughness by distance.<\/li><li>Nijat Ibrahimli (Lip Sync): 12 mouth shapes, time remap hold.<\/li><li>Taste references batch 1: IKEA Together (allkustom), GATE (azma), define. (aflow), \"us\" / Tarot / am not cute anymor. (y6richo), Cupid (wanezv), 결정 (Rafi Rizky).<\/li><li>Taste references batch 2: Re:bake and I'M LOST. (seven), Bohdan Martovskyi Showreel 2025, Cloudwalk Showreel 2023, Glam (Dan Stein), Myth Showreel 2021.<\/li><li>Standard principles (written reference animation-principles): numbers in chapter 2.<\/li><li>Internal motion study (approved): puppet mesh + IK rig for waving, a silat side kick, jumping, walking, sitting; rig from traced artwork (DeformSpace) for idle, waving, side-view walk, turnaround (chapters 3.1, 4, 4.1).<\/li><li>Vector background research (test project, principles): Clip Studio \"Flat Design Illustration 6 Styles\" and \"How to paint: Trees\"; BINUS DKV \"Backgrounds that Breathe\"; Creative Bloq \"How to depict forest light\" and \"Inject great depth into flat colours\"; Painting Lessons with Marla \"Color in Light and Shadow\"; Zoung Art \"Painting in the style of Sleeping Beauty\" (Eyvind Earle); Animation Obsessive \"What Kazuo Oga thinks about\" (chapter 5.1).<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo3","title":"Taste","html":"<ul><li>A palette of 3-6 colours per piece. Pattern: 1 main hue + 1 dark + 1 accent (lime/navy; red/black/white), or pastel off-white #F3F3F3 + 2-3 blues.<\/li><li>A locked 3-colour sequence: light background, dark silhouette, 1 warm accent only for rims and details (about 5-10% of the area, estimate). Myth example: #7FB9CB / #0D2330 / #E8694A.<\/li><li>A saturated cool subject (#2B3BD6) on a warm 2-stop vertical gradient (#F2875E to #EBA56E); shadows towards #0A0A1E.<\/li><li>8-9 colours only when everything is flat with a uniform dark outline of 1.5-2 px at 1080p (estimate), strokes not scaled.<\/li><li>Each scene has its own solid background colour, a soft radial glow behind the subject, and a floor one step darker.<\/li><li>Depth through colour: the distance lighter and less saturated (towards the sky colour), the foreground dark and blurred.<\/li><li>Finish with light grain/noise or rough edges of 1-3 px; texture clipped inside each shape, not over the whole frame; dry-brush edge = path point jitter of 1-2 px. Light sources get a blurred copy on top (glow).<\/li><li>Ending: tint the whole frame (for example lilac #A6B4F0), show the title, hold for about 3 s.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo4","title":"Composition and typography","html":"<ul><li>One subject in the middle with wide empty space; cinematic: the subject on the right third, vertical elements (tree trunks) as rhythm, a dark foreground cutting the bottom edge. Aspect ratio 16:9, 2.67:1 or 4:3.<\/li><li>A 4:5 poster crop inside the 16:9 frame with cream margins (#F4F0EA); full-bleed during action, back to the poster for the title.<\/li><li>Symmetry for hero shots: one-point perspective or a 4/8-fold kaleidoscope.<\/li><li>Typography: narration in a small lowercase geometric sans; keywords in an ultra-condensed display face; titles bold extended caps; captions thin with very wide tracking (+300-400, estimate); collage style = a different font per word; credits small with wide tracking.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo5","title":"Motion principles (the 12 principles) with numbers","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Principle<\/th><th>Rule in code<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Squash &amp; stretch<\/td><td>Scale X x Y roughly constant: x = 100 + (100 - y) x 0.5; blink = eye squashed into a line<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Anticipation<\/td><td>wind-up 0-10 f, transition 10-12 f, main action 12-30 f, settle 30-40 f<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Slow in/out<\/td><td>ease in/out at every key pose; steep curve = fast<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Arc<\/td><td>limb tips move on arcs (FK rotation), not straight lines; a head turn dips slightly in the middle; objects on a jump path rotate along the path (atan2)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Follow-through/overlap<\/td><td>children lag 2-5 f or valueAtTime(time - 0.05); hair lags the head; tail segments lag 2-3 f per segment (estimate)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Secondary<\/td><td>separate layer, time offset, supports rather than competes<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Timing<\/td><td>2-4 f sharp, 6-8 f fast, 12-15 f normal, 20-30 f heavy, 40 f+ dramatic; 24 fps cinematic, 30 fps smooth; a whole action can be 20 f, the biggest move within about 3 f, then ease to the peak<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Exaggeration<\/td><td>20-50% above realistic; may break physics<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Smear<\/td><td>on the fastest frame, the moving tip is stretched along the motion for 1 frame<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Overshoot<\/td><td>amp 15, freq 3, decay 5: value + amp sin(t freq 2 pi)/exp(t decay) after the last key<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Bounce<\/td><td>amp 80, freq 3, decay 8<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Staging<\/td><td>one focus; blur/dim the secondary; DOF locked on the subject + vignette<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Appeal<\/td><td>silhouette readable in every pose; the last frame is never fully still (eyes opening slightly, scale growing slightly)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Advanced principles v2.4 (details, API and QC numbers in <code>references/advanced-principles.md<\/code>; used during spline and QC):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Principle<\/th><th>Use<\/th><th>API<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Physical springs<\/td><td>Replace overshoot/bounce numbers with named presets: <code>bouncy<\/code> (ζ 0.45, 21% overshoot), <code>snappy<\/code> (ζ 1, no overshoot), <code>heavy<\/code> (ζ 1.5)<\/td><td>ease <code>\"spring.bouncy\"<\/code>; <code>mgp.motion.spring(name)<\/code>; <code>MGP.core.springPhys<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Inertia<\/td><td>Damped bounce from the arrival speed when an object stops<\/td><td><code>mgp.motion.inertia(vars, options)<\/code>; <code>MGP.core.inertia<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Chained delay<\/td><td>Follow-through/overlap per index (hair, tails, rows)<\/td><td><code>MGP.core.delay<\/code> / <code>delayChain<\/code>; stagger<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Constant-volume squash<\/td><td>sx·sy = 1 (the old chapter 2 formula errs by ±20%)<\/td><td><code>MGP.core.stretch<\/code>, <code>stretchDirection<\/code>; check with <code>mgp curves<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Constant stroke<\/td><td>Lines do not thicken when the character is scaled<\/td><td><code>strokeFixed<\/code>; SVG <code>vector-effect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Rubberhose<\/td><td>Bendy arms/legs with a fixed length<\/td><td><code>MGP.core.rubber<\/code>; <code>character-styles<\/code> <code>limbs: \"rubber\"<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2.5D head, FACS<\/td><td>Head turns from 5 poses; expressions from Action Units<\/td><td><code>MGP.face<\/code> (<code>poseHead<\/code>, <code>EMOTION<\/code>, <code>auAt<\/code>)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>SLERP<\/td><td>3D camera/joint rotation without gimbal<\/td><td><code>MGP.core.slerp<\/code>; <code>sceneTanker<\/code> <code>cameraInterp: \"slerp\"<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Rigid origami<\/td><td>Rigid hinge folding, Maekawa/Kawasaki validator; default origami character style <code>origamiStyle: \"rigid\"<\/code> (<code>\"old\"<\/code> = v2.3)<\/td><td><code>origamiFold.validatePattern<\/code>, <code>paperFoldTransition<\/code> <code>rigid: true<\/code>, <code>MGP.foldPattern<\/code> (Miura, Yoshimura, Kresling)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fields<\/td><td>Cloner + effector + falloff field (MoGraph)<\/td><td><code>MGP.field<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Curve QC<\/td><td>Value/speed graphs; flags linear, pop, abrupt, volume<\/td><td><code>mgp curves &lt;composition.html&gt;<\/code><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"mo6","title":"Rotation and rig","html":"<ul><li>The rotation anchor sits exactly at the centre of the joint circle; test by rotating before animating; no detail in the joint area. Standing objects: anchor at the base. Rigid objects rotate as a whole, not by moving path points.<\/li><li>Chained parenting (see chapter 1); from the hips: hair &gt; head &gt; neck &gt; collar &gt; torso &gt; belly &gt; hips &gt; main null; a foot that never lifts goes straight to the null. A null/root moves the whole body; squash on the null.<\/li><li>Puppet pins/mesh for bending parts (cloth, hands, torso) so they stay one shape. In code: the finished artwork is cut once, sampled into polygons, and LBS-skinned to the bones (see vector-character chapter 5 (<code>vector-character/references/rig.md<\/code>)).<\/li><li>Joints that \"fill in\" during motion (shoulder, armpit, elbow, hip, knee) are redrawn per pose and change CONTINUOUSLY with the angle, without if/else switches (vector-character chapter 8 (<code>vector-character/references/rig.md<\/code>)). Before rendering, make a sweep video per joint (for example an arm 0-120-0 degrees).<\/li><li>Pose = a dict of local bone angles (degrees, + = clockwise on screen) + 'root' (body shift) + '&lt;bone&gt;_off' (pivot shift, for example a raised shoulder). Pose blend = lerp per key; IK targets (ikL/ikR, wN/wF) hold their value instead of lerping to 0.<\/li><li>Short pieces (about 3 s): shape-path animation from pose to pose without an IK rig is fine. Boneless limbs (noodle): a curved centre line + a thick round-cap stroke.<\/li><li>Blocking: every key of one pose at the same time with the same easing (hold keys); overlap is added afterwards.<\/li><li>Alternating motion: key 0, 12 f (opposite angle), 24 f = key 0, then loop.<\/li><li>Avoid unison: a different duration and phase for each part.<\/li><li>Head turn: strong ease, dipping slightly in the middle (arc), the hips shift a little too, hair lags, then a hold of about 0.5 s.<\/li><li>Hands move after the body/camera has started, and pause briefly in the air before the action.<\/li><li>Characters on twos (12 of 24 fps); decorations on fours. Short stylised pieces can be full 12 fps: pose at floor(t x 12)/12.<\/li><li>Hair is split per strand with staggered keys; wind sways left and right every 8 or 12 frames. Hair can stretch into a ribbon that becomes the portal to the next scene.<\/li><li>Lip sync: 12 mouth shapes, hold (no interpolation) per sound, at least 2 frames per shape (time remap).<\/li><li>Turnaround: fixed drawings per view (front, 3/4, side, back, mirrored side and 3/4, front), an anticipation dip before the turn + a small settle at each drawing change.<\/li><li>Raised arms (especially loose clothing): a flat tube, elbow = a rigid piece + a joint circle, forearm straight (vector-character chapter 0 (<code>vector-character/references/mistakes.md<\/code>), 8).<\/li><li>Multi-angle rig for an orbit: one 3D skeleton from the gait engine projected to the camera angle (vector-character chapter 16 (<code>vector-character/references/multi-angle.md<\/code>)). Motion (gait, arms, springs) is computed once in the body frame; the camera angle only changes the projection, so the feet stay planted at every angle.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>3.1 Secondary motion (springs)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Hair, scarves, skirts, tails, ropes = a chain of damped springs: theta'' = -w^2 theta - 2 z w theta' + gain (a_x / L) 57.3 - parent angular acceleration + wind (value noise) - drag (while walking).<\/li><li>The spring frequency must NOT be close to the motion frequency (walk: hip roll 0.9 Hz, bob 1.8 Hz). Resonance at 1.0 Hz makes hair swing too much; use 1.35-1.55 Hz, zeta 0.32-0.35.<\/li><li>Simulate with a 1.5 s pre-roll so the springs have settled by frame 0.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo7","title":"Walk cycle","html":"<ul><li>Cycle T = 1.0-1.1 s. Phase p = (t/T) mod 1, far leg p + 0.5. Poses: contact 0, down 0.08, passing 0.33, up 0.42.<\/li><li>The foot stays still against the ground while planted: v_ground = 2 x step / T. The footing background must move at exactly v_ground, otherwise the feet slide.<\/li><li>Foot roll: heel pivot (-14 to -20 to 0 degrees, ease out), flat, ball pivot (0 to 26-32 degrees, ease in). The pivot really sits at the heel/ball (the ankle is computed from the rotation around that point).<\/li><li>CONTINUITY (the cause of a \"choppy\" walk): the swing MUST start exactly at the toe-off position and end exactly at the next heel-strike position (the same stance function is used for the swing ends). The toe angle is blended smoothly, without switching modes. Check: the ankle speed change between frames has no spikes.<\/li><li>Hips: lowest at contact, highest at passing (bob = 0.065 + 0.03 cos(4 pi p) H). The hip-ankle distance is at most 98-99% of the leg length; 100% = the knee locks and then releases (a break).<\/li><li>The leg is almost STRAIGHT at passing. Hips too low = knees bent throughout the cycle = a crouching walk that reads as \"choppy\" (Review Results).<\/li><li>Sample approved relaxed walk parameters: bob = 0.055 + 0.033 cos(4 pi p) H, step 1.10 H, T 1.06 s, heel strike in the first 22% of stance, max reach 99.1%.<\/li><li>Wide trousers/long skirts: the cloth does not bend sharply at the knee (wide blend); in a side-view walk the silhouette of wide trousers reads as heavy, check it in the video.<\/li><li>2-bone IK: alpha = acos((L1^2 + d^2 - L2^2)/(2 L1 d)), knee = H + L1(cos(theta - alpha), sin(theta - alpha)), d max 0.999(L1+L2).<\/li><li>Arms opposite to the legs: upper = +-22-28 degrees; the elbow bends when the arm is in front; the head lags 0.20 (nod 1-2 degrees). Chest leans 4 degrees.<\/li><li>The swinging foot moves about 2-3x the body speed (about 0.3 H per frame at 24 fps) → motion blur is REQUIRED, otherwise it strobes.<\/li><li>Secondary springs (antennae, hair): u'' = -w^2 u - 2 zeta w u' - a_lateral, w = 2 pi x 2.3 Hz, zeta = 0.14.<\/li><li>Run cycle (Ben Marriott): on twos, 7 drawings, left leg = right leg offset by 1 frame; reduce the head bounce when it is too much.<\/li><li>Front-view run (Cloudwalk): cycle about 0.8 s (estimate); the character stays in the middle, the world comes towards the camera (scale = 1/z), hip bob A|sin|. Match cut: hard cut on an identical pose every 0.7-1 s (estimate), only the palette and particles change (seasons).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>4.1 Other motions (unit H = head height, 24 fps, approved)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>A planted foot is solved with IK EVERY frame (the ankle target stays on the ground), so the foot does not slide while the body moves.<\/li><li>Walk (side view): cycle 1.10 s, step 1.35 H, stance 50%: heel strike in the first 16%, flat, toe-off in the last 30%; swing: the foot lifts 0.30 H sin(pi u)^1.3, continuous ends (chapter 4); motion blur 4 sub-frames.<\/li><li>Wave (front view): raise the arm 0.7 s (forearm lags 0.15 s), 4 swings in 1.9 s (forearm +-16 degrees, the hand follows with a phase difference of 0.6 rad), lower 0.8 s; the hand switches to the open set (palm facing forwards) as the arm rises; the shoulder lifts slightly.<\/li><li>Silat side kick (front view): horse stance 0.8 s (hips down 0.30 H, feet apart +-0.86 H), hold 0.3 s, chamber (knee raised, thigh -78, shin +95 degrees) 0.35 s, kick 0.2 s ease out (thigh -76, shin 0, torso leaning 16 degrees away), hold 0.5 s, chamber, back to the horse stance, stand. The kicking leg leaves the IK only while it is raised. Fists clenched, guard stance (front arm guarding the face, back elbow down).<\/li><li>Jump (side view): anticipation crouch 0.55 s (hips down 0.95 H, torso leaning, arms back), push-off 0.17 s ease in (arms swing up), rise 0.29 s ease out, fall 0.29 s ease in, landing and damping 0.25 s (knees bend), return 0.75 s. In the air the knees bend following sin(pi u).<\/li><li>Sit (side view): the crate/chair BEHIND the standing position, its surface slightly below the knee (5.86 H, about 1.7 H above the floor). The feet stay in place; lean + hips back and down 0.6 s, down onto the crate 0.35 s, upright 0.35 s, hold 1.0 s, lean again, then stand 1.1 s. The hip height is COMPUTED from the contact of the buttocks with the surface (an iterative constraint, never penetrating), thighs almost flat, shins upright, hands IK to the knees (smooth blend). The old version was rejected: the buttocks at the crate edge and \"inside the bench\" because the hip height was guessed.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>4.2 Object and shape animation<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Start from two extreme poses; anticipation, then the action; stagger with a fixed time offset (domino).<\/li><li>Assembly: parts start from a random offset (scale 0.6-0.9), stagger 2-4 f, done in about 1.5 s.<\/li><li>Shapes change in sequence while growing: circle &gt; ring &gt; diamond &gt; hexagon &gt; rectangle; shatter, then re-form. Shape morph: resample both paths to N points, then interpolate.<\/li><li>One-take morph chain (Myth): 1-2 s per step, locked palette (dune &gt; wave &gt; tentacle &gt; tree &gt; horse &gt; train &gt; wall).<\/li><li>Fake 3D coin flip: sx = cos theta, a side band d|sin theta| in the rim colour, the back side mirrored when cos &lt; 0.<\/li><li>Fake extrusion: an offset copy of 8-20 px (estimate), side quads in between, a specular top edge.<\/li><li>Fake spinning globe: dots move with sin(lon + omega t) and shrink towards the edge.<\/li><li>Vines: grow with a trim path, leaves pop along the stem. Hanging objects swing, then settle (small amplitude). A striped ball rolling: slide the stripes inside a circle clip.<\/li><li>Floating objects bob on a slow sine; the floor shadow shrinks as they rise. A radial burst for impacts. Handoff: an object leaves the hand and the camera follows it.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>4.3 Realistic walk cycle from gait data (Review Results, test project, strip accepted)<\/h4>\n<p>Reference data (adult woman):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter<\/th><th>Value<\/th><th>Source<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Speed, step<\/td><td>1.31 m/s, 66 cm (stride 132 cm)<\/td><td>Murray 1970 (OUHSC stride analysis)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Cadence<\/td><td>60 x v / step = 119 steps/min; cycle T = 120 / cadence = 1.01 s<\/td><td>Derived<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Stance / swing / double support<\/td><td>60% / 40% / 2 x 10%<\/td><td>Missouri PM&amp;R Normal Gait pp. 2, 4, 5<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Knee<\/td><td>5-10 degrees during loading; max 65 degrees in early swing<\/td><td>Missouri pp. 14, 18<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Ankle<\/td><td>0 degrees at heel strike; plantarflexion 20-30 degrees at toe-off<\/td><td>Missouri pp. 13, 17<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Centre of mass rise and fall<\/td><td>about 5 cm, 2 oscillations per cycle (male data)<\/td><td>OUHSC kinematics<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Shoulder / elbow<\/td><td>range 20-25 degrees / 15-30 degrees (29.7 +- 10.2)<\/td><td>Gait &amp; Posture 2019; CiSE 2023<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Step / body height<\/td><td>0.46<\/td><td>Barreira 2010<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Conversion to a stylised character (big head, short legs):<\/p>\n<ul><li>Scale the step to the crotch height, not the body height: step = (step/height) / 0.50 x the character's crotch height.<\/li><li>Test character example: (0.40-0.46) / 0.50 x 2.36 H = 1.9-2.2 H (0.40 = Murray's 66 cm assuming a height of 1.64 m, because the subject height is not given in the source), while the v3 contact pose was only 0.89 H (43%). The user chose a realistic 2.0 H step, and the contact pose was rebuilt by the gait engine.<\/li><li>Round the cycle to a whole number of frames (1.01 s becomes 24 f) so the loop fits.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>Gait engine (unit H, hips at x 0):<\/p>\n<ul><li>Planted foot: the heel on the ground moves at -v relative to the hips, x = H0 - stride x stance x u.<ul><li>Heel rocker: heel pivot, angle -phi_hs to 0.<\/li><li>Flat.<\/li><li>Toe rocker: pivot at the TIP of the sole, not at the ball. A rigid shoe rotated at the ball pushes the toe of the shoe through the ground.<\/li><li>Every pivot is on the ground, so the foot does not slide.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Planted position: H0 is chosen so the ankle sits right under the hips in the middle of the flat phase.<\/li><li>phi_hs is solved so the ankle is neutral (0 degrees) at heel strike.<\/li><li>Hips:<ul><li>Computed from the leading stance leg with a target knee bend (loading, then midstance).<\/li><li>Then smoothed with a Fourier series of even harmonics only (half-cycle period, because the two legs are symmetric).<\/li><li>Then shifted down just enough that the stance leg reach is &lt;= 99% in every phase.<\/li><li>The final heel-rise angle in terminal stance (phi_ps) is solved so the hip height joins up when the stance leg changes.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Pre-swing: foot angle = Hermite from phi_ps (initial slope equal to the previous phase) to phi_to. phi_to is solved so the plantarflexion = 25 degrees at toe-off. When the reach overshoots, the angle is raised.<\/li><li>Swing:<ul><li>Ankle = Hermite from the toe-off state to heel strike, with tangents = the stance speed at both ends (C1 continuous).<\/li><li>Plus a lift L x sin(pi x w^0.65)^2: peak in early swing, zero derivative at the ends.<\/li><li>Foot angle = 2-segment Hermite: flat at 55% of the swing, then to -phi_hs.<\/li><li>The ankle is pulled towards the hips when the reach &gt; 99% (late swing).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Required report (<code>gait.py report<\/code>): hip bob, max reach, knee per phase and max swing, toe-off plantarflexion, swing clearance, ground penetration, ankle speed jumps per frame.<\/li><li>Data vs rule conflict: the reach rule &lt;= 99% makes the stance knee at least about 16 degrees (data: 5 degrees). Tell the user about this conflict. The user chose to keep the 99% rule.<\/li><li>Test character result: bob 0.147 H, reach 99.0%, swing knee 66 degrees, toe-off plantar 24 degrees, clearance 0.062 H, penetration 0.000 H.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>Rig redrawn per frame (not a mesh pull):<\/p>\n<ul><li>Pose = hips (bob), legs (shoe cfg: pivot, position, angle; ankle; IK knee), arms (shoulder angle + elbow bend), head nod.<\/li><li>Frames:<ul><li>hips P = bob shift;<\/li><li>upper body U = lean + bob;<\/li><li>head = U rotated by the nod at the chin.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>The side-view shape functions (leg stations, arm tubes, shoes) are called again from the pose. Layers and colours are the same as the still drawing.<\/li><li>Arms:<ul><li>opposite to the leg on the same side: -cos(2 pi (p - lag)), lag 0.03;<\/li><li>the arm carrying a load gets a smaller swing (an assumption, tell the user);<\/li><li>the fist rotates 0.8 x the forearm angle.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Secondary motion:<ul><li>Hair tips = a vertical spring driven by the head acceleration, plus a constant drag backwards. The shape is warped only below the ears, with weight ((y - y0)/(y1 - y0))^2.<\/li><li>Bag = a pendulum at the fist's hanging point: psi'' = -(a_x cos psi + (g - a_y) sin psi)/l - damping. The fist acceleration is computed RELATIVE to the hips. Using a world position that jumps at the cycle boundary causes an impulse every cycle and the bag spins 90 degrees.<\/li><li>Hem = a spring driven by the near thigh angle.<\/li><li>Spring frequency 1.40-1.60 Hz (gait 1 and 2 Hz), damping 0.30-0.40. Pre-roll 5 cycles, then keep one periodic cycle.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Gapcheck every frame: small gaps that appear when the bag strap/edge passes the leg edge are legitimate geometry gaps (covered by motion blur). Tell the user, do not hide them.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>Running data (early research in the test project, not used by a rig yet; check the primary text before use):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter<\/th><th>Value<\/th><th>Source<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Toe-off<\/td><td>about 40% of the cycle (walking 60%); double float at the start and end of the swing<\/td><td>Novacheck 1998 (Gait &amp; Posture), via Physiopedia Running Biomechanics<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Swing<\/td><td>given as 62% (inconsistent with toe-off at 40%; check the original text)<\/td><td>Physiopedia<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Knee<\/td><td>initial contact about 40, loading 60, before toe-off about 40, max 125 degrees mid swing<\/td><td>Novacheck 1998 via Physiopedia<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hips<\/td><td>flexion about 50 at contact, hyperextension 10 after toe-off, flexion 55 at the end of the swing<\/td><td>Novacheck 1998 via Physiopedia<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Ankle<\/td><td>dorsiflexion 10 at contact, rising to 25; max plantarflexion 25 in early swing<\/td><td>Novacheck 1998 via Physiopedia<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Cadence<\/td><td>2.68 m/s 169.2; 2.98 m/s 170.5; 3.16 m/s 172.3; 3.35 m/s 174.3 steps/min; self-selected 3.20 m/s, 168<\/td><td>IJSPT, effect of running speed on cadence, Table 1 (30 runners, 11 women)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Step length<\/td><td>v / (cadence/60): 0.95 m at 2.68 m/s (the source says \"stride 0.98 m\", actually one step)<\/td><td>Derived<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Vertical oscillation<\/td><td>6-9 cm (secondary source, needs a primary source)<\/td><td>RunnersConnect<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"mo8","title":"Living backgrounds","html":"<ul><li>Parallax: sky 0, clouds 0.04x + drift, far mountains 0.08x (blur 2.2), near mountains 0.14x, hills 0.32x, trees 0.55x, footing 1.0x, road 1.15x, foreground grass 1.5x (blur 4.5).<\/li><li>Atmospheric perspective: the further away, the closer to the sky colour; the palette stays in one colour family. 2D layers get light in 3D space for cinematic depth.<\/li><li>Bend (CC Bend It): dx = sway x H x (h/H)^2, a different phase per object. Wave Warp only on free parts (skirt, hair, robe, fire): a mask with a straight edge at the root, the wave pinned at that edge, a small shift before the wave to close the seam; fire waves upwards.<\/li><li>Leaf particles float up (slightly negative gravity) and sideways. Birds = a trail of dots. A tree crown breaks into spreading dots.<\/li><li>Wiggle = 2-octave value noise, not a pure sine. Loop = a whole number of cycles, first frame = last frame.<\/li><li>Twinkling stars: scale up and down; random twinkle = fractal noise as a luma matte.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>5.1 Detailed backgrounds (Review Results, test project)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>A plain/simple background (flat layers, blob trees, no cast shadows) was REJECTED: \"make it cool and detailed, shadows, etc.\".<\/li><li>Before designing a background: research vector background references (principles, not copies), then make 2-3 style frames to choose from + an orbit sheet at several angles + a short clip per style.<\/li><li>Principles from the research:<ul><li>flat-style layer order: flat shape → shadow per shape (clipped) → multiply gradient per shape → limited texture;<\/li><li>depth: linear perspective, parallax, less detail and contrast in the distance, the distance much paler and cooler;<\/li><li>trees = clumps, the light-dark border not round, sky holes + branches visible in the holes, crown shadow on the trunk;<\/li><li>dappled light: leaves = gobo, shadows with holes, shadows never black;<\/li><li>saturation in the shadows never higher than in the light;<\/li><li>large dark shapes first, small light details last; the background serves the story (take the essence).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>A natural background palette (for example green mountains), matched to the character palette so it blends in instead of looking pasted on. Write a hex table + its link to the character colours, and show swatches on the comparison sheet.<\/li><li>Avoid background colours equal to the clothing where they overlap. Example: sage trousers in front of a hill, so the hill was shifted to olive green; a cream road under white shoes + sage trousers.<\/li><li>9:16 composition: character sole at about 80% of the frame height, character about 50-60% of the frame height (or as the chosen camera distance requires), road under the feet.<\/li><li>The mountain view must be visible: clear tall trees from the view wedge of the main shot (open meadow up to the distant tree line); framing trees only in the top corners.<\/li><li>Three style directions compared in the test project: A flat cel, 3 tones, high sun (chosen: safest for the orbit and blends best with the flat character), B gradient + grain + glow + warm grade, low sun (needs relighting of the character per angle), C brush edges + leaf strokes + paper texture.<\/li><li>Background mistakes found so far:<ul><li>straight triangular mountains = geometric; use periodic fBm + key peaks with rounded tops (soft-abs) and uneven shoulders; volcanoes with concave slopes k^1.75 + a shallow crater; S-curved radial gullies with random length and width;<\/li><li>the shadow side of a mountain is found on a smoothed ridge (large peaks only);<\/li><li>chevron leaf marks read as birds; use small jagged leaf clumps;<\/li><li>a dark band on the trunk under the crown reads as a hard band; use a gradient;<\/li><li>sky holes in distant trees read as white specks; only for crowns &gt; 150 px;<\/li><li>trees at mid distance cover the mountains (a 10 m tree at 60 m is taller than a 1 km mountain at 10 km);<\/li><li>a sun that makes large crown shadows cover the road in front of the character; pick an azimuth so the shadows fall behind the objects, or put the sun behind the scene for long shadows towards the camera;<\/li><li>a soft-light texture with a mean other than 0.5 makes the frame grey; paper texture must be neutral (mean 0.5).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Grain/texture only on the background behind the character; a flat character stays clean.<\/li><li>Principles from two stock vector backgrounds (village + winter forest, measured from the SVG: 0 strokes, 0 opacity/blend, no grain; test project 05_Riset):<ul><li>a gradient per shape, almost always vertical, length = shape height, dL* end to end 8-14; smoothness comes from gradients, not texture (fits the flat cel style when dL* &lt;= 14);<\/li><li>the foot of each layer fades into the haze colour (blue-teal valley) as a layer separator without lines; snow: a light lip on the top edge of each mound;<\/li><li>hue shifts in vegetation gradients (green at the ends to maroon/purple in the middle) and warm light / cool shade on mountains (for example h105 vs h321) give depth without darkening;<\/li><li>mountain = a shaded base mass + angular light flakes on the sun side + thin blue flakes on the edge facing the sky;<\/li><li>value range per distance: far L* 72-96 C* 5-14 (almost sky colour), middle 60-87, near 22-64, framing trees darkest at about L* 22;<\/li><li>tree trunks in 2 tones + a warm rim band 20-30% of the width on the light side (about +26 L*); pines = tiers of drooping branches, distant rows = small triangles on a gradient band;<\/li><li>water = a gradient (sand to blue) + white glint ellipses in horizontal rows that shrink into the distance, the shore with a dark lip; ice = a gradient + thin light crack lines.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>5.2 3D space background for a one-take orbit (Review Results, test project, approved)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Pinhole camera: f about 1500 px for 1080 x 1920, yaw from north clockwise, pitch down +; near plane 1.0 H with polygon clipping (Sutherland-Hodgman) before projection.<\/li><li>Camera height so the character's sole sits at y_s (for example 80% of the frame) at horizontal distance d: h = d (sin t + k cos t) / (cos t - k sin t), k = (y_s - cy) / f, t = pitch. Test character example: d 14 H, pitch 4 degrees, h 6.53 H.<\/li><li>Ground, road (with staggered brick joints and slab tone variation), pond, grass patches, fallen petals = polygons on the ground plane, projected per point. Straight 3D lines stay straight; circles are sampled at 48-96 points.<\/li><li>Mountains, hills, distant hedges = cylinder rings around the park centre (radius 300-2600 H), a height profile per azimuth, only the run of visible points (z &gt; 0.35 R) made into one polygon. Parallax is automatically correct.<\/li><li>Trees, lamps, shrubs, flowers, grass, rocks = billboard sprites facing the camera, base at the world position, scale f/depth, sorted far to near together with the character. Distant trees grouped on rings with gaps. LOD: small sprites use fewer clumps without detail.<\/li><li>The sun is fixed in the world; the sprite light direction on screen = (sun . right, sun . up) of the camera, so the lit side of a tree moves correctly during the orbit.<\/li><li>Haze per distance through colour mixing (not a layer): t = haze_max (1 - exp(-(d - 15) / D)).<\/li><li>Cast shadows are computed in the world: crown = an ellipse along the sun (axis r / sin e), trunk = a quad, dappled light holes = small erased ellipses. All shadows are drawn OPAQUE on one layer, then composited once (multiply + alpha; <code>brush<\/code>: <code>cv.save_layer(brush.Paint(alpha=a, blend=\"multiply\"))<\/code> ... <code>cv.restore()<\/code>, <code>shadow3d.shadow_layer<\/code>): overlaps do not darken twice. DO NOT union hundreds of polygons with PathOps (23 s per frame); a layer = 2 s per frame.<\/li><li>Camera path vs objects: check the distance from the camera to tree trunks/lamps/shrubs in every frame of the take (<code>clearance<\/code>). Objects the camera passes through are removed after the layout is made (keepout), with the random draws still consumed so the rest of the layout does not change and old style frames can still be regression-tested.<\/li><li>Test the 3D space before the rig: an orbit sheet at 4 angles (90R, 180, 90L, 0) with the existing character drawings.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>5.3 Character shadows in a 3D scene (Review Results, test project)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>A character shadow must NOT be a fixed capsule shape (\"the shadow doesn't change based on the motion\"). Compute it from the 3D pose: body = capsules/spheres (legs, torso, neck, head, arms, bag, shoes) from the gait engine, each point P projected as G = P - sun x (P_y / sun_y), a sphere becomes an ellipse with axis r / sin e along the sun. The shadow steps along and is correct from every camera angle.<\/li><li>Draw the shadow in ground coordinates through a ground → screen homography: for camera axes a (right, up, fwd), the camera coordinates of a ground point (x, 0, z) = a_x x + a_z z - C . a; homography rows = (f a0 + cx a2), (cy a2 - f a1), (a2) against (x, z, 1). Round-cap strokes in ground space give the correct width.<\/li><li>Contact occlusion (AO) under a planted shoe.<\/li><li>The character darkens too in the shade of trees/lamps: map the character billboard pixels to the ground along the sun (screen → sprite local → ground → screen, one 3x3 matrix), sample the shadow mask (white = lit, shadow = 1 - a + a x colour), then blend with <code>modulate<\/code> on the character layer (<code>brush.Paint(blend=\"modulate\")<\/code>, <code>shadow3d.draw_shaded<\/code>). Shadows of low objects (shrubs, flowers) are not used.<\/li><li>Actions not rigged yet use the standing pose for the shadow; write this down as a weakness.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo9","title":"Kinetic typography","html":"<ul><li>Per-letter entrance with a mask reveal from the baseline: stagger 0.03 s, duration 0.6 s, ease out back 1.4; opacity done at 45% of the duration.<\/li><li>Exit faster: 0.3 s ease in cubic, stagger 0.015 s. Supporting sentence per word: stagger 0.05-0.08 s, shift 26 px, ease out expo.<\/li><li>One word per beat: the new word enters blurred, pops in scale 0.6 &gt; 1.08 &gt; 1.0, then sharpens. A dot bouncing over the words (lyric style). Typewriter with a faint offset copy (shadow).<\/li><li>Letter swap: letters swing on an arc with 40-60 degrees of rotation (estimate). Parts of a glyph can become objects (a colon becomes two doughnuts falling at different times).<\/li><li>Logo as a fake text editor: text selected, replaced, typed, a cursor click, then a stroke drawn around it, about 7 s.<\/li><li>Large mesh-warp title: y += A sin(2 pi x / L) + skew; swap black and white during the warp. Text inside footage, scattering letters.<\/li><li>Labels: tracking 2 → 10 px. Accent lines: grow with ease out expo, retract with ease in expo. One big thing moves at a time; text holds fully still for at least 1 s.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo10","title":"Transitions and camera","html":"<ul><li>Layered circle wipe: 3 circles offset 0.12-0.16 s, ease in-out cubic; the new scene zooms 1.10 → 1.0.<\/li><li>Panel wipe: the second panel follows 0.12 s later; the old scene is pushed 170 px.<\/li><li>Callout: dot pop (ease out back 2.2) → trim-path line → box 0.75 → 1 overshoot → text wipe; the target follows the rig position.<\/li><li>Open with a blurred extreme close-up, then zoom out to reveal the scene (about 1.4 s, ease out). Gradient reveal from black. Tilt down a wall to reveal the subject. Fly into an object until it turns abstract, then pull back.<\/li><li>Push the camera into a door/hole/keyhole to change scene.<\/li><li>The scene shrinks into a card, then scrolls away or is set down by a hand; a perspective frame pulled off screen; scenes on the sides of a turning cube; a 3-panel split screen.<\/li><li>A diagonal wipe with a blurred edge, a paper tear, a hard cut on the beat; a dark foreground passing for 2-4 f (estimate) to hide a cut.<\/li><li>Endless spiral tunnel: nested polygons at scale k^i (k about 0.8), rotation i x dtheta (10-20 degrees, estimate), zoom k^(-frac(vt)).<\/li><li>360-degree orbit: the camera parented to a null that turns once; flat 2D assets always face the camera; motion blur on.<\/li><li>One-take orbit with a 2D character (Review Results, test project):<ul><li>the background is built as a simple 3D space (chapter 5.2);<\/li><li>the take timeline = a separate module with no drawing dependencies (character position, facing, action, walk phase, camera azimuth, distance), shared by the render and the keepout;<\/li><li>character drawing angle v = camera azimuth from the character - character facing (0 front, 90 = 90R, 180 back, 270 = 90L);<\/li><li>character: a multi-angle rig from one 3D skeleton (vector-character chapter 16 (<code>vector-character/references/multi-angle.md<\/code>)), continuous angles; the old version with 16 still drawings swapped and shifted read as sliding;<\/li><li>the character's light direction is recomputed per angle from the world sun (review decision, test project);<\/li><li>the camera distance is chosen from a comparison sheet (test project: 14 H for every beat, character 31% of the frame height);<\/li><li>drawing swaps (if any remain) are hidden by motion blur or a dip.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Fitting the multi-angle rig into the one-take animatic (tested in the rig v4 test project, not yet accepted as a result):<ul><li>one function <code>pose_for(state)<\/code> returns the rig joints for the render AND the shadow (3D primitives from the same joints), so the shadow always matches the pose, also during blends;<\/li><li>actions not rigged yet use the standing pose (legs almost straight at 99%, feet flat, arms hanging), the camera angle stays continuous, the label \"not rigged yet\" stays in the frame;<\/li><li>action changes (stand → walk, walk → stop, turn → run) are blended over 0.3 s (staged joint lerp), not swapped abruptly;<\/li><li>a placeholder run with the walk rig: phase = distance travelled / (2 x step), so the feet stay planted; label \"walk rig sped up\";<\/li><li>character light from the world sun through the body frame (vector-character chapter 4 (<code>vector-character/references/anatomy.md<\/code>)); the character sheet keeps the reference light;<\/li><li>orthographic billboards in a perspective scene: measure the foot slip (px) per beat BEFORE the full render. Test character project: side 1 px, front/back orbit 57 px. A perspective correction on the legs only removed the slip but lengthened the legs (rejected); a correction must be consistent for the whole figure (vector-character chapter 16.1 (<code>vector-character/references/multi-angle.md<\/code>)).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo11","title":"Effects","html":"<ul><li>Edge roughness gets stronger as the character comes closer to the camera.<\/li><li>Travelling shine: a light mask sliding over a copy of the line. 4-point sparkle pop of about 6-8 f. Thin white elliptical trail lines around a spinning object.<\/li><li>Neon: a thin stroke + glow, drawn on progressively (trim path); moving neon text = glow + 4-6 fading copies behind the direction of motion.<\/li><li>Jagged starbursts / torn edges for the collage style; iridescent gradients + grain. DOF blur by depth.<\/li><li>Light rays (warm style): quads from the sun's screen position (may be outside the frame), alpha gradient 0.16 to 0, screen blend (<code>brush.Paint(blend=\"screen\", shader=brush.linear_gradient(...))<\/code>; JS canvas: <code>globalCompositeOperation = \"screen\"<\/code>); the sky glow still reaches the frame when the sun is outside it (radius plus the distance outside).<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo12","title":"Pacing","html":"<ul><li>Music loop 13-37 s, a cut every 1-4 s on the beat; narrative scenes 2-6 s.<\/li><li>15 s structure: hook 2 s, action 6 s, settle, title hold about 3 s, credits. 34 s brand film: logo 3 s, 8-9 beats of about 3-4 s (estimate).<\/li><li>Showreel: cuts of 0.5-2 s on the beat, lyric words on the vocals, a logo wall at the end.<\/li><li>Fast action of 2-3 frames, then a long hold. End card in the middle of a plain background for 2-5 s.<\/li><li>Sample approved 18 s take (test character): wave 3.4 s, turn 1.0 s, walk followed 2.0 s, walk + 270-degree orbit 6.0 s (45 degrees/s), turn + start running 1.6 s, run + 180-degree orbit 4.0 s.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo13","title":"Render","html":"<ul><li>Python: frames are drawn with <code>brush<\/code> (the skia engine when skia-python is installed, otherwise numpy + Pillow; choose with <code>MGP_BRUSH_ENGINE<\/code>) → PNG (<code>render.py frames<\/code>) → ffmpeg H.264 CRF 18, preset veryfast, yuv420p, +faststart. 1920 x 1080, 24 fps. HTML/canvas compositions (<code>lib/<\/code> modules, chapter 13): mgp seeks <code>window.__seek(t)<\/code> per frame, then encodes with ffmpeg.<\/li><li>Pixels are read with <code>Surface.array()<\/code> (always RGBA uint8 on both engines). Old code that calls skia directly must still use <code>toarray(colorType=skia.kRGBA_8888_ColorType)<\/code> (the default is BGRA → swapped colours).<\/li><li>Grain: a fixed seed for video (random grain per frame makes the file 16x larger and flickers).<\/li><li>Motion blur: the average of n sub-frames within a 180-degree shutter (0.5 frame): walk/jump 4, martial arts 3, wave 2, still 1. An on-twos option (12 fps posterize) for a hand-made feel.<\/li><li>Test a still/strip first; run the full render in the background (nohup), split over several parallel processes per frame range (<code>video - &lt;dir&gt; &lt;start&gt; &lt;stop&gt;<\/code>), then <code>encode<\/code>. When only one clip changes, re-render only that clip's frame range. 2-core workspace: 2 processes; a 3D scene about 2.7 s per frame (18 s animatic = about 20 minutes, final with 4 sub-frames about 40 minutes with 2 processes).<\/li><li>Measure before optimising: timing per stage (sky, panorama, ground, shadows, sprites); on the skia engine the Python profiler does not count time inside skia functions; on the pure engine most time goes to numpy rasterisation (for drafts lower <code>MGP_BRUSH_SUPERSAMPLE<\/code>, for example to 2, then set it back to 4 for the final).<\/li><li>Pathfinder through <code>brush.union<\/code> / <code>brush.intersect<\/code> / <code>brush.subtract<\/code>, not <code>skia.Op<\/code> directly. On the skia engine Op can raise a RuntimeError on complex paths/large coordinates: wrap it in try/except, try <code>brush.simplify<\/code>, fall back to a clip. The pure engine evaluates booleans per sample while drawing (no errors, no seams); <code>bounds()<\/code> of the result is an approximate box.<\/li><li>Claude Code on Windows (8 cores) vs Cowork (Linux, 2 cores): parallel render with <code>python tools/par.py N a b command... {a} {b}<\/code> (replaces .sh scripts with /home/claude paths). Measured in the test project: 3D scene animatic 0.75 s per effective frame with 8 processes (Cowork about 2.7 s per frame per process, 2 processes); a multi-angle rig adds about 2-3 s per frame per process. Files go directly into the project folder, no zip between sessions (zip only when continuing in Cowork).<\/li><li>Zero-pixel regression per machine: text labels and anti-aliasing differ between operating systems (max 2/255 in a scene); make new references per machine (<code>review/ref_win/<\/code>).<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo14","title":"Quality check (required before delivery)","html":"<ol><li>gapcheck EVERY frame (holes + loose pieces) and a regression sheet against the previous delivery. 1. No foot sliding (3 consecutive frames). 2. A strip per 1/8 cycle + a contact sheet of every clip. 3. Measure the numbers: wrist jumps per frame, max leg reach (&lt;= 99%), penetration (0.000 H). 4. Loops without jumps. 5. Readable text: text colour contrasts with the background, holds long enough. 6. Palette, fonts, motion and transitions as confirmed in chapter 0. 7. A sweep per joint without shape jumps (armpit, shoulder, knee). 8. Check the whole figure part by part with zoom, not only the part being fixed. 9. Motion not in unison; the last frame not dead. 10. Write down the weaknesses still visible when delivering. 11. Walk cycle: gait report (chapter 4.3) + walkcheck on every frame + a list of legitimate gaps. 12. Package test: zip, extract to a new folder, re-render, compare pixels with the delivery. 13. 3D scene: camera path clearance (0 tall objects &lt; 1.5 H from the camera), deterministic render (regression reference frames with 0 pixel difference), character shadow follows the pose. 14. Multi-angle rig: gapcheck every frame at every angle + a 360-degree turn video. 15. Rig in the scene: frames at critical angles (0, 90, 180, 270 + the hardest) side by side with the accepted version, proportions measured in H in the frame (rule 00.8). 16. Foot slip in a perspective scene max 1 px per stance phase (<code>tools/selip.py<\/code>), without changing proportions.<\/li><li>Motion curves after the spline pass (<code>mgp curves<\/code>): no accidental linear motion, breaks only at impacts/contacts/cuts, squash with constant volume (reference numbers in <code>references/advanced-principles.md<\/code>).<\/li><\/ol>"},{"id":"mo15","title":"Support files per project","html":"<ul><li>Reusable general tools are in this skill's <code>scripts/<\/code> (chapter 11). What stays in the project: character sizes, character drawings, background layout, style/palette, take timeline.<\/li><li>Keep in the project folder (not in the skill): motion clip scripts + reel render (<code>sheet<\/code>, <code>video - &lt;dir&gt; &lt;start&gt; &lt;stop&gt;<\/code>, <code>encode<\/code>), front/side rig, joint sweeps, gapcheck, regression sheet, reference taste notes, and the character spec (sizes, colours, motion parameters specific to that character).<\/li><li>A style can be swapped without touching the rig (separate paint module, vector-character chapter 5 (<code>vector-character/references/rig.md<\/code>)); re-render the clips after a style change and re-run gapcheck.<\/li><li>Walk cycle from data: the gait engine separate from the drawing rig (<code>gait.py report<\/code>), the drawing rig redrawn per frame (<code>walk_rig.py strip|frame<\/code>), and <code>walkcheck<\/code> per frame.<\/li><li>One-take 3D scene (test project example): ruang.py (camera, projection, layout, keepout), gaya.py (palette + sprites + sky/panorama/ground/shadows + <code>render<\/code>), take.py (timeline + camera), bayang.py (3D pose shadows + shade on the character), animatic.py (<code>frames<\/code>, <code>sheet<\/code>, <code>report<\/code>, <code>clearance<\/code>), rig16.py (multi-angle rig), tools/rigcheck.py.<\/li><li>Check tools that proved useful (test project): <code>tools/regresi.py<\/code> (pixel comparison + difference image), <code>tools/par.py<\/code> (parallel render), <code>tools/selip.py<\/code> (foot slip in the scene), <code>tools/sudut16.py<\/code> and <code>tools/detail16.py<\/code> (16 angles + hand/shoe zoom), <code>tools/tascek.py<\/code> (variant comparison per angle), <code>tools/stripsheet.py<\/code> (stacked strips).<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo16","title":"Bundled scripts (`scripts/`)","html":"<p>General tools from the test projects, cleaned up so they do not depend on a particular character. All motion, character and background numbers still come from the brief/project.<\/p>\n<p>How to use them in a new project:<\/p>\n<ol><li>Copy this skill's <code>scripts/<\/code> folder into the project folder (for example <code>tools/mg/<\/code>). The copy in the skill is not changed; tool fixes are noted and brought into the skill through a skill update.<\/li><li>In the project module: <code>sys.path.insert(0, \"tools/mg\")<\/code>, then <code>import brush<\/code>, <code>from gait_engine import Gait<\/code>, <code>import space3d as R<\/code>, <code>import shadow3d as B<\/code>. Canvas = <code>brush.Canvas<\/code> (<code>brush.Surface(w, h).canvas<\/code>), shape = <code>brush.Path<\/code>, colour = <code>brush.color(hex)<\/code>.<\/li><li>Fill in your own character sizes: <code>Gait(body=dict(thigh=.., shin=.., sole_y=.., hip_y=.., shoulder=.., upper=.., fore=.., shoe=[..]), step=.., T=..)<\/code>, <code>shadow3d.PRIM<\/code>. The defaults in the scripts = the sample character in the 7.5-head canon on 5.78 H (<code>gait_engine.BODY<\/code>, <code>shadow3d.PRIM<\/code>, <code>space3d.HERO<\/code>; check the landmarks with <code>gait_engine.canon()<\/code>), not defaults to reuse.<\/li><li>Install once: <code>pip install -r scripts/requirements.txt<\/code> (numpy + Pillow only). <code>pip install skia-python<\/code> is optional, for speed; OpenCV is not needed. <code>MGP_BRUSH_ENGINE=pure|skia<\/code> forces an engine, <code>MGP_BRUSH_SUPERSAMPLE<\/code> sets the pure-engine supersampling (default 4). Run each script's demo once in the working folder to confirm the environment (numpy, Pillow, ffmpeg). Details: <code>scripts/README.md<\/code>.<\/li><li><code>brush.py<\/code>, <code>image_ops.py<\/code>, <code>cavity.py<\/code> and <code>NOTICE<\/code> are copies from one source shared with the vector-character skill; do not edit the copies (sync flow in <code>scripts/README.md<\/code>).<\/li><\/ol>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Script<\/th><th>Contents<\/th><th>Check/demo<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>gait_engine.py<\/td><td>Walk cycle engine from gait data (chapter 4.3): <code>Gait(body, **G)<\/code>, <code>pose(p)<\/code>, <code>arm(p, bag)<\/code>, <code>hip_y(p)<\/code>, <code>foot(p)<\/code>, <code>shoe_map<\/code>, <code>ik<\/code>; <code>BODY<\/code> (7.5-head canon), <code>canon(gt)<\/code> (landmarks in heads); <code>report<\/code>; skeleton strip<\/td><td><code>python -I gait_engine.py report [param=value]<\/code>, <code>strip out.png 8<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>spring.py<\/td><td>Periodic secondary motion (chapter 3.1): <code>spring<\/code>, <code>spring_to<\/code>, <code>pendulum<\/code>, <code>accel<\/code>, <code>Table.at(p)<\/code>; pre-roll 5 cycles, keep 1 cycle<\/td><td><code>python -I spring.py demo out.png<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>space3d.py<\/td><td>3D background space (chapter 5.2): <code>Cam<\/code>, <code>Cam.orbit<\/code>, <code>cam_height<\/code>, <code>sun_dir<\/code>, <code>light2d<\/code>, <code>sprite<\/code>, <code>ground_poly<\/code>, <code>strip_xz<\/code>, <code>ellipse_xz<\/code>, <code>ring_path<\/code>, <code>mt_profile<\/code>, <code>haze_t<\/code>, <code>keepout<\/code>, <code>clearance<\/code>, <code>HERO<\/code><\/td><td><code>python -I space3d.py demo out.png<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>shadow3d.py<\/td><td>Character shadow from the 3D pose + shade (chapter 5.3): <code>walk_prims(gt, p, load_arm, extra)<\/code>, <code>ground_matrix<\/code>, <code>draw_shadow<\/code>, <code>draw_contact<\/code>, <code>shadow_layer<\/code>, <code>shade_matrix<\/code>, <code>draw_shaded<\/code>, <code>mask_color<\/code>; <code>PRIM<\/code> (7.5-head canon)<\/td><td><code>python -I shadow3d.py demo out.png<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>render.py<\/td><td><code>frames<\/code> (sub-frame motion blur, shutter 0.5), <code>parallel<\/code> (split ranges, 2 processes), <code>encode<\/code> (H.264 CRF 18 veryfast), <code>hstack<\/code> (labelled comparison), <code>sheet<\/code> (contact sheet); fn(t) returns a <code>brush.Surface<\/code> (or a <code>brush.Image<\/code>, an RGBA array; <code>skia.Surface<\/code> is accepted too)<\/td><td><code>python -I render.py frames module:fn dir 0 24<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>cavity.py<\/td><td>Gapcheck <code>still<\/code> (cavities + loose pieces + 15% silhouette) and <code>frames<\/code> (every animation frame); the same as vector-character (one source)<\/td><td><code>python -I cavity.py frames module:fn 24<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>brush.py<\/td><td>Vector drawing layer: <code>Surface<\/code>, <code>Canvas<\/code>, <code>Path<\/code> (+ <code>from_svg<\/code>), <code>Paint<\/code> (<code>blend<\/code> multiply, modulate, screen, ...), <code>linear_gradient/radial_gradient<\/code>, <code>Matrix<\/code> (perspective), <code>Font<\/code>, <code>Image<\/code>, pathfinder <code>union/intersect/subtract/exclude<\/code>, <code>measure<\/code>, <code>draw_svg<\/code>; skia or numpy engine<\/td><td>used by every script; examples in <code>scripts/README.md<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>image_ops.py<\/td><td>numpy + Pillow image tools replacing OpenCV: <code>read/write<\/code>, <code>cvt_color<\/code>, <code>resize<\/code>, <code>gaussian_blur<\/code>, <code>warp_affine<\/code>, <code>connected_components<\/code>, <code>outer_contours<\/code>, <code>convex_hull<\/code>, <code>line<\/code>, <code>rectangle<\/code><\/td><td>used by <code>cavity.py<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>requirements.txt, README.md<\/td><td>Required packages (numpy, Pillow) + optional (skia-python); command list and engine choice<\/td><td><code>pip install -r scripts/requirements.txt<\/code><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Regression test against the test character pipeline (2026-10-30):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Script<\/th><th>Result<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>gait_engine.py<\/td><td><code>report<\/code> output identical; 240-phase pose (hips, knees, ankles, foot angle, arms) difference 0.0<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>spring.py<\/td><td>Hair, bag, hem vs the project simulation: max difference 0, 9.7e-17 rad, 0<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>space3d.py<\/td><td>Projection of 100 points x 5 azimuths, light2d, ring_path, mt_profile, cam_height (6.530327 H): difference 0<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>shadow3d.py<\/td><td>Body prims over 24 phases (without the project's bag prim) difference 0; ground_matrix and shade_matrix identical<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>cavity.py<\/td><td><code>still<\/code> and <code>frames<\/code> results identical to the project check (cavities, marker PNG, 15% silhouette)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"mo17","title":"Additions in Version 2.1 (05-11-2026)","html":"<p>The recipes in this chapter come from the code demos of the guide site (2D canvas, the same 3D projection as <code>space3d.py<\/code>). The numbers are accepted starting points; character size, palette and project timing still come from the brief.<\/p>\n<h4>12.1 3D human figure: proportions and volume<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Classic adult proportions, unit H (chapter 4): <strong>7.5 heads<\/strong> on a 5.78 H body; one head = 0.771 H (top to chin). Landmarks in heads from the top: chin 1, nipples 2, navel 3, crotch 3.75 (1/2 of the height), knee 5.625 (1/4 of the height above the floor). The check numbers come from <code>gait_engine.canon()<\/code> (<code>python -I gait_engine.py report<\/code> prints height, knee, elbow, wrist and foot in heads); <code>MGP.figure3d<\/code> (chapter 13) uses the same canon.<\/li><li>Shoulder width 2 heads (1.54 H) outside the deltoids (shoulder joint +-0.6 H from the centre line + sleeve volume); hips 1.5 heads, neck 0.5 heads.<\/li><li>Hip joint 2.78 H from the top, crotch 3.75 heads = 2.89 H: the legs are about 1/2 of the body height.<\/li><li>Elbow at navel height (shoulder + upper arm 0.95 H, about 2.85 heads), wrist at crotch height (about 3.75-3.8 heads), fingertips at mid thigh.<\/li><li>Foot 1.1 heads. Chest, waist and hips have real depth so the side view stays round.<\/li><li>The walking legs use the gait engine (chapter 4.3) with canon bones (thigh 1.54 H, shin 1.272 H), sample shoe x1.6, step and lift x leg length (about 1.12); angles and timing stay the same.<\/li><li>Volume: every part = a tube of elliptical rings per section (shoulder, upper arm, elbow, forearm, wrist; thigh, knee, shin, ankle); torso = 10 rings (neck, shoulders, chest, waist, hips) with a back curve.<\/li><li>Shading on top of 3 base tones, computed from the normal per point against the sun direction (lam):<ul><li>core shadow = a thin dark band right at the terminator (lam &lt; -0.38, alpha 0.32);<\/li><li>half-light = a soft light band on the lit side (lam &lt; 0.45, alpha 0.22);<\/li><li>highlight = lam &gt; 0.8;<\/li><li>rim light = the silhouette edge (fresnel factor &gt; 0.84) on the side facing away from the light;<\/li><li>bounce = warm reflected light (#ffb27a mixed at 35%) on the dark side near the silhouette.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Outline: stroke first, then fill (width 2 x lw, ink alpha 0.62); lw = clamp(0.016 x pixels per H, 0.45, 2.1) so the line thickness follows the camera distance.<\/li><li>Draw order per frame: far arm, legs (sorted by depth), hips, neck, shirt, head (back hair, ears, head, facial features, front hair + strands), near arm last.<\/li><li>Facial features are attached to the head surface (they foreshorten) and fade when the head turns away (hidden when facing &lt; 0.04, ramp x5).<\/li><li>Living idle: breathing 0.006 H with a 4.2 s period, nod 1.2 degrees with a 5.3 s period, head turn +-22 degrees with a 9.5 s period.<\/li><li>The capsule primitives of the same pose are used for the cast and contact shadows (chapter 5.3), so the shadow always follows the pose.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>12.2 One-take scene recipe: a tanker berthing at a jetty<\/h4>\n<p>One 46 s take without cuts, then a 4 s hold and repeat. The world is in metres: x along the jetty, y up (water = 0), z away from the jetty face (water z &gt; 0, land z &lt; 0). Ship frame: u forwards, v up, w to the ship's right (jetty on the left side).<\/p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sizes.<\/strong> Tanker L 176, B 28, freeboard 7.6, bow 40, stern 14. Tug L 28, B 10.<\/li><li><strong>Stages<\/strong> (u = t / 46):<\/li><\/ul>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>u<\/th><th>Stage<\/th><th>Content<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>0-0.36<\/td><td>Approach<\/td><td>Angled path, 2.6 to 1.3 knots<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.36-0.6<\/td><td>Slowing and straightening<\/td><td>1.3 to 0.35 knots<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.6-0.745<\/td><td>Pushed by tugs<\/td><td>The ship moves sideways to the fender line<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.745-0.77<\/td><td>Against the fenders<\/td><td>0 knots at u 0.75<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.765-0.9<\/td><td>Mooring lines 1/6 to 6/6<\/td><td>One line every 0.026 u (about 1.2 s)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.93-0.99<\/td><td>Loading arms connected<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.99-1<\/td><td>Safely moored<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<ul><li><strong>Path.<\/strong> Hermite keys [u, x, z, heading]; a stop key (zero tangent) where the ship touches, so it does not overshoot.<ul><li>Ship path: (-370, 128, -9.5 degrees) to (0, 22.5, 0), then sideways to B/2 + 1.7.<\/li><li>Tug in the ship frame: escorts at the bow on the sea side, turns in at -55 degrees, pushes perpendicular at -90 degrees (hold 0.61-0.77), backs off, then leaves.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><strong>Fenders and bollards.<\/strong> A fender every 22 m, compressed when the hull leans on it (thickness 1.6 to at least 1.05). A bollard every 22 m on the jetty edge.<\/li><li><strong>Six mooring lines.<\/strong> Order: head line, forward breast line, forward spring, aft spring, aft breast line, stern line. Fairleads at u +-80, +-66, +-50 to bollards at x +-121, +-77, +-11.<ul><li>A line is thrown in an arc (peak 7 m), falls slack (sag 3.2 m), then is pulled tight (sag 0.35 m within 0.05 u); its colour gets lighter when tight.<\/li><li>The line name labels are shifted up so they do not overlap.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><strong>Loading arms.<\/strong> Two arms at x +-4: riser 18.5 m, two 12 m booms with 2-bone IK, from the stowed position to the ship's manifold.<\/li><li><strong>Life.<\/strong><ul><li>Ship: heave 0.14 m, roll 0.004 rad.<\/li><li>Water: bow foam while moving (&gt; 0.12 knots), foam wakes behind the stern and the tug, a foam line at the waterline, a mirror reflection broken into glint lines, wave lines drifting slowly.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><strong>Camera.<\/strong> Presets (azimuth, elevation, distance): Air 22 / 17 / 320 m, From the sea 352 / 6 / 250 m, Jetty end 78 / 11 / 300 m, From land 205 / 13 / 240 m. The camera chases its target with ease dt x 3; drag = orbit with elevation limited to 3-55 degrees.<\/li><li><strong>Mood.<\/strong><ul><li>Day: sun azimuth 205, elevation 38.<\/li><li>Dusk: sun azimuth 258, elevation 5; jetty lamps on (screen blend) and spilling onto the water.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><strong>Draw order.<\/strong> With the camera on the sea side: land, jetty, arms, lines, ship. On the land side: the order is reversed.<\/li><li><strong>On-screen text.<\/strong> Caption \"BERTHING · stage · SPEED x KN\" + numbers (stage, speed, distance to the fenders, lines n / 6).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>12.3 Personified chibi object (example: a coffee cup)<\/h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Chibi proportions.<\/strong> A short round body; the face uses the top half of the body. Big eyes: 10 x 13 ellipses on a cup 128 wide, with 2 highlights. Short chubby arms (stroke 14, a short curve from the side of the body). Feet = two flat ellipses. Pink cheeks + blush lines.<\/li><li><strong>Moods<\/strong> (keyed states, motion-graphics-pro skill 6.7; the mood ids stay in Indonesian):<\/li><\/ul>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mood<\/th><th>Eye scale<\/th><th>Steam<\/th><th>Arm angle<\/th><th>Emote<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>normal<\/code><\/td><td>1<\/td><td>1<\/td><td>18<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>senang<\/code> (happy)<\/td><td>0.55 (smiling)<\/td><td>1.25<\/td><td>128 (waving)<\/td><td>heart<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>sedih<\/code> (sad)<\/td><td>0.85<\/td><td>0.4<\/td><td>-12 (limp)<\/td><td>drop + cloud<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>kaget<\/code> (surprised)<\/td><td>1.2<\/td><td>1.7<\/td><td>140<\/td><td>exclamation mark<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>marah<\/code> (angry)<\/td><td>0.8<\/td><td>2.4<\/td><td>70 (shaking)<\/td><td>anger mark<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<ul><li><strong>Arms per mood<\/strong> (on twos, angle rounded to 2 degrees): happy waves +-14 degrees, angry shakes fast +-7 degrees, sad hangs, normal sways slightly +-4 degrees.<\/li><li><strong>Reacting steam.<\/strong> The steam sticks to the top of the lid, which squashes along. Speed and thickness = the mood's steam factor, chased smoothly with dt x 3. Angry: reddish steam, fast, thick. Sad: bluish steam, thin, slow, drooping.<\/li><li><strong>Mood-change take.<\/strong> The eyes lead by 2 drawings (0.17 s, up 5 px). Anticipation squash -18% for 0.25 s with the arms folded (-20 degrees). The new face appears at the deepest squash (+ sparks). Settle: overshoot 15% x exp(-5 s), frequency 3.<\/li><li><strong>Jump.<\/strong> Squash 20%, an arc up 78 px with the arms raised 160 degrees, landing damped (exp(-6 s)). The squash keeps the area: sx = 1 + (1 - sy) x 0.5. The shadow shrinks and fades while rising.<\/li><li><strong>Blink<\/strong> 0.17 s at random every 2-5 s; the eye highlight disappears during a blink or when the eyes narrow (&lt; 0.7).<\/li><li><strong>Emote<\/strong> appears after the new face (pop with 35% overshoot), then floats up and down.<\/li><li>All motion on twos (12 drawings per second). Collage scene: every shape a cutout with a hard offset shadow (3, 4 px).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>12.4 Narrator face with lip-sync<\/h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Visemes from per-letter timing<\/strong> (the same as viseme_id in the motion-graphics-pro skill 6.8):<ul><li>Mapping: a to A; o to O; u, w to U; f, v to FV; i, e, é, y to E; m, b, p to MBP; other letters = E with weight 0.5.<\/li><li>Text inside [ ] is not spoken.<\/li><li>Gaps &lt; 2 drawings are bridged.<\/li><li>Each drawing picks the viseme with the largest time coverage.<\/li><li>Every m/b/p is guaranteed at least one closed-mouth drawing.<\/li><li>The last drawing = a smile.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><strong>Mouth.<\/strong><ul><li>Open shapes are filled dark (#5b1f22) and clipped: the upper teeth show on A, E, FV; the tongue shows on A, O, E.<\/li><li>The lip line is thicker when closed (7 vs 5.5).<\/li><li>Openness: A 1, O 0.8, E 0.6, U 0.45, FV 0.3, MBP and X 0.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><strong>Head.<\/strong> A nod follows the mouth openness (2.4 x open, chased with dt x 9) + a slow sway. Rotation +-2.2 degrees slowly + +-1.6 degrees while talking, pivoting at the neck. The body follows slightly. All on twos.<\/li><li><strong>Brows<\/strong> rise with open vowels (-2.5 x open) and rise 3 more when the script contains the tag [excited] / [senang] / [semangat] / [happy].<\/li><li><strong>Eyes.<\/strong> Blink 0.15 s every 2.2-5 s (y scale 0.1); slow glances +-1.4.<\/li><li><strong>Test.<\/strong> Letter time 0.07 s, punctuation 0.25 s; a viseme strip per drawing is shown for a check by ear; JSON {t, v} for use in the render.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>12.5 Motion in pixel art and origami styles<\/h4>\n<p>The character, skeleton and walk cycle stay the same; only the drawing method and the clock change (drawing the character: vector-character skill chapter 19 (<code>vector-character/references/style.md<\/code>)). Vector = 24 drawings per second, joints rotate smoothly.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Pixel art.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>The scene is drawn into a low-resolution buffer. Screen pixel size ps = max(3, round(screen height x 0.6 / 48)), so the character is about 48 pixels.<\/li><li>Draw with imageSmoothing off and integer scaling.<\/li><li>Clock: tq = floor(t x 8) / 8 (8 drawings per second, stepped); walk 6 drawings; idle 2 drawings alternating 2 per second; blink 0.13 s every 3.2 s.<\/li><li>Sprites are cached per (motion, drawing, blink).<\/li><li>Parallax in whole pixels: clouds 0.1, hills 0.4, ground 1.0 of the scroll speed.<\/li><li>Sky = checkerboard dither bands (not a gradient).<\/li><li>Sample sprite sheet: 6 walk drawings side by side, the drawing currently shown boxed.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>Origami.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Clock 12 drawings per second.<\/li><li>Unfolding: each face rotates about its first edge (hinge), staged from the feet up, a 0.025 s delay per face, 0.45 s per face (inout cubic). When cos &lt; 0, the cream back side shows (#f4ead6).<\/li><li>Eyes and cheeks appear after the unfold has finished (&gt; 1.2 s).<\/li><li>Folding crane: 8 triangles change shape from square to triangle to bird base to crane.<ul><li>Hold/change order (s): 0.7, 0.9 + 0.5, 0.9 + 0.5, 0.9 + 2.0, then back 0.9.<\/li><li>The interpolation is quantised to 12 steps.<\/li><li>Triangles whose winding flips show the back side of the paper.<\/li><li>The wings flap +-8% in the last step.<\/li><li>Step labels \"1 · square paper\" to \"4 · finished crane\".<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Kraft background (#efe3c9) with a faint triangle texture, two-tone paper mountains + snow caps, ground #c9935a with light crease lines; mountain parallax 0.25.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo18","title":"Module library (`lib/`) (Version 2.2, 05-11-2026)","html":"<p>This skill's <code>lib/<\/code> folder holds ready-to-use JavaScript motion engines that apply chapters 2-12 on a canvas (or the DOM). Every module is deterministic and driven by time: the frame at second t is always the same, however many times and from whichever direction it is seeked; no unseeded <code>Math.random<\/code>, no <code>requestAnimationFrame<\/code>, no external assets. The options of each module are in <code>lib/README.md<\/code>; one example per module is in <code>lib/examples/<\/code> (runs directly from <code>file://<\/code>).<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">13.1<\/span> <strong>Module list and when to use each.<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Module (<code>MGP.&lt;name&gt;<\/code>)<\/th><th>File<\/th><th>Use for<\/th><th>Chapter<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>core<\/code><\/td><td><code>core/mgp-core.js<\/code><\/td><td>Always loaded first: seeded rng, noise/boil, easing, closed-form springs, keyframes, Catmull-Rom, 3D camera + projection, colour, <code>setup<\/code> / <code>defineModule<\/code> / <code>timeline<\/code> / <code>expose<\/code><\/td><td>all<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>figure3d<\/code>, <code>gait<\/code><\/td><td><code>figure3d.js<\/code><\/td><td>3D human character in the 7.5-head canon, walking from gait data or standing alive; <code>gait<\/code> = side-view stride (heel, flat foot, toe, swing)<\/td><td>4.3, 12.1<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>shadow3d<\/code><\/td><td><code>shadow3d.js<\/code><\/td><td>Character shadow from the 3D pose and a moving sun, one layer + contact shadow<\/td><td>5.3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>scenePark3d<\/code><\/td><td><code>scene-park3d.js<\/code><\/td><td>3D park, one-take camera orbit, haze, DOF, light rays, grain<\/td><td>5.2, 7<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>sceneTanker<\/code><\/td><td><code>scene-tanker.js<\/code><\/td><td>One-take tanker berthing, camera presets; <code>lib.state(t)<\/code> for a data panel<\/td><td>12.2<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>characterStyles<\/code><\/td><td><code>character-styles.js<\/code><\/td><td>One character in vector, pixel art or origami style (needs <code>figure3d.js<\/code>; the \"crane\" action needs <code>origami-fold.js<\/code>)<\/td><td>12.5<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>origamiFold<\/code><\/td><td><code>origami-fold.js<\/code><\/td><td>Folding crane from a flat-fold simulation; <code>lib.foldPolygon<\/code> for your own folds<\/td><td>12.5<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>vectorKit<\/code><\/td><td><code>vector-kit.js<\/code><\/td><td>Capsule vector character kit: 4 views and turnaround, 8 expressions, 12 mouths, 8 gestures, proportion modes<\/td><td>3; vector-character 5.1 (<code>vector-character/references/rig.md<\/code>), 18<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>paperFoldTransition<\/code><\/td><td><code>paper-fold-transition.js<\/code><\/td><td>Origami/accordion fold transition between pictures, folding DOM elements, portal<\/td><td>7<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>causalTransitions<\/code><\/td><td><code>causal-transitions.js<\/code><\/td><td>Transitions by their reason: wipe, page, shut, iris, slide, bridge, split, cut<\/td><td>7<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>chibiObject<\/code><\/td><td><code>chibi-object.js<\/code><\/td><td>Objects with faces: keyed moods, mood-change takes, jumps, steam, emotes<\/td><td>12.3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>lipsync<\/code><\/td><td><code>lipsync.js<\/code><\/td><td>Indonesian text, phonemes or timestamped words into visemes at 12 drawings per second; vector presenter or your own face picture; karaoke captions<\/td><td>12.4<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>routeMap<\/code><\/td><td><code>route-map.js<\/code><\/td><td>A route growing from its tip, stopping at cities, the vehicle facing the tangent; cardboard, 3d and native styles (real geography through your own <code>land<\/code>/<code>tiles<\/code>)<\/td><td>7<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>kineticType<\/code><\/td><td><code>kinetic-type.js<\/code><\/td><td>Text from timed beats (card, rise, pop, slide, mask, cascade, typewriter, karaoke, zoom, wipe); <code>captions(words)<\/code> from per-word VO timing<\/td><td>6<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>spring<\/code><\/td><td><code>spring.js<\/code><\/td><td><code>fn<\/code>: bezier, spring, bounce, follower/chain/lag, squash, anticipate; spring graph view and principle panels<\/td><td>2, 3.1<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>particlesFlight<\/code><\/td><td><code>particles-flight.js<\/code><\/td><td>Scheduled light particles: bursts, streams, trails, depth flights<\/td><td>7.1<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>constellationLines<\/code><\/td><td><code>constellation-lines.js<\/code><\/td><td>Night sky, constellations with glowing curved lines, travelling sparks, labels<\/td><td>7.1<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>packs<\/code><\/td><td><code>pack-scenes/<\/code><\/td><td>104 motion packs (motion-graphics-pro skill <code>references/motion-packs.md<\/code>, old chapter 17.2) drawn straight from their id<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Rules of use: pick a module when its job fits the brief; the rules of chapter 00 still apply (check every frame, measure the motion, regression sheet, judge in the scene frame). Palette, fonts, character size and timing come from the brief through options (<code>pal<\/code>, <code>palette<\/code>, <code>font<\/code>, <code>colors<\/code>, ...), not from the module defaults. The modules do not replace the Python scripts of chapter 11: Python is for measuring, checking and rendering PNGs; <code>lib/<\/code> is for HTML compositions.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">13.2<\/span> <strong>Composition contract.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Load <code>lib/dist/mgp.js<\/code> (every module, one <code>&lt;script&gt;<\/code> tag, runs from <code>file://<\/code>) or <code>core/mgp-core.js<\/code> + module files; <code>lib/dist/mgp-packs.js<\/code> for the packs only. ES module: <code>import MGP from \"./lib/index.mjs\"<\/code> (needs an http server). The timeline uses mgp.motion (<code>lib/motion/motion.js<\/code>, the package's own engine; main API <code>mgp.motion<\/code>, <code>gsap<\/code> is a compatibility alias; real GSAP is not needed).<\/li><li><code>MGP.&lt;module&gt;.create(target, options)<\/code> (target = <code>&lt;canvas&gt;<\/code>, a container, or a selector) returns an instance with <code>draw(t)<\/code> / <code>seek(t)<\/code>, <code>duration<\/code>, <code>ready<\/code> (a Promise, for example waiting for fonts), and <code>timeline()<\/code> (a paused mgp.motion timeline, <code>duration<\/code> long). <code>MGP.&lt;module&gt;.render(ctx, t, options)<\/code> draws one frame into an existing canvas, to combine several modules on one canvas. <code>MGP.&lt;module&gt;.lib<\/code> = reusable inner functions.<\/li><li><code>MGP.core.expose(inst)<\/code> sets <code>window.__duration<\/code>, <code>window.__seek(t)<\/code>, <code>window.__timeline<\/code>, <code>window.__timelines[\"main\"]<\/code> and <code>window.__ready<\/code>. The renderer waits for <code>__ready<\/code>, then calls <code>__seek(t)<\/code> per frame.<\/li><li>Several modules in one composition: <code>MGP.core.expose({ duration: 12, ready: Promise.all([a.ready, b.ready]), seek(t) { a.seek(t); b.seek(t); }, timeline() { return MGP.core.timeline(this); } })<\/code>.<\/li><li>A fixed-size stage (<code>#stage<\/code> with <code>data-composition-id=\"main\"<\/code>; options <code>width<\/code>/<code>height<\/code>, default 1280 x 720; <code>dpr<\/code>, <code>fit<\/code>, <code>loop<\/code>, <code>seed<\/code>). Write <code>window.__fps = 24<\/code> (or <code>data-fps<\/code>) so the renderer does not use its default of 30.<\/li><li>New module: <code>MGP.core.defineModule(name, { size, duration, defaults, setup(S), render(ctx, t, S) })<\/code> (example <code>examples/core.html<\/code>), then <code>node lib/build.js<\/code>: it rewrites <code>dist/<\/code> and <code>index.mjs<\/code> and tests that every file loads. JS files are ASCII only (<code>\\uXXXX<\/code> for special characters).<\/li><li>No state may build up between frames: everything is computed from t (particles, springs, camera), so backward seeks and parallel renders stay correct.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">13.3<\/span> <strong>Rendering with mgp.<\/strong> mgp (motion-graphics-pro skill, folder <code>render/<\/code>; needs Node.js 22+, ffmpeg, Chrome or Edge) uses <code>window.__seek(t)<\/code> as its first driver, so <code>lib/<\/code> compositions render directly:<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code># no npm install: mgp brings its own CDP client, fonts and mgp.motion\nnode &lt;motion-graphics-pro folder&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs check &lt;motion-graphics folder&gt;/lib/examples/figure3d.html\nnode &lt;motion-graphics-pro folder&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs contact scene.html --n 12 -o review/contact.png\nnode &lt;motion-graphics-pro folder&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs render scene.html --fps 24 --workers 4 -o out/scene.mp4<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ul><li>Copy <code>lib/dist/mgp.js<\/code> into the project folder (for example <code>assets/js/<\/code>): mgp only serves files under the composition folder (<code>--root &lt;folder&gt;<\/code> changes that).<\/li><li><code>mgp check<\/code> tests determinism (three points seeked, then seeked again from elsewhere; the hashes must match). A failure = a timer, state that builds up between frames, or a fetch.<\/li><li>Together with mgp composition scenes (motion-graphics-pro T7, <code>references/template.md<\/code>): the module draws into a <code>&lt;canvas&gt;<\/code> inside the scene's <code>#root<\/code> and is called from the scene's <code>draw(t)<\/code> with local time; still one timeline per composition.<\/li><li>Motion packs from the brief: <code>MGP.core.expose(MGP.packs.create(\"#stage\", \"KIN-02\", { lines: [{ j: \"Title\", c: \"Caption\" }] }))<\/code>, or one frame on your own canvas with <code>MGP.packs.render(id, ctx, t, { width, height, ar: \"9:16\", lines: [...] })<\/code> (<code>render<\/code> accepts the <code>create<\/code> content options <code>ar<\/code>, <code>lines<\/code>, <code>pal<\/code>, <code>font<\/code>, <code>scene<\/code>/<code>variant<\/code>; reuse one options object across frames; <code>lib/README.md<\/code>, Motion packs). <code>examples/packs-gallery.html<\/code> shows all 104 packs.<\/li><li>Python renders (<code>render.py<\/code>) and mgp renders can be mixed: a scene from Python as a PNG sequence or video, then loaded in the composition as an <code>&lt;img&gt;<\/code>/<code>&lt;video&gt;<\/code> that mgp seeks per frame.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Summary (skill core)<\/h4>\n<ul><li><code>lib/dist/mgp.js<\/code> contains every <code>MGP.*<\/code> module (one <code>&lt;script&gt;<\/code>, runs from file://); the timeline uses <strong>mgp.motion<\/strong> (<code>lib/motion/motion.js<\/code>, the package's own animation engine; main API <code>mgp.motion.timeline/to/fromTo/set<\/code>, <code>gsap<\/code> is only a compatibility alias for old code). Module list, contract and examples: <code>references/lib.md<\/code> and <code>lib/README.md<\/code>.<\/li><li>New project: <code>mgp new &lt;folder&gt;<\/code> (motion-graphics-pro skill) copies <code>mgp.js<\/code>, <code>motion.js<\/code> and fonts into <code>assets/<\/code>. Without it, copy <code>lib/dist/mgp.js<\/code> into the project folder (mgp only serves files under the composition folder).<\/li><li>Full-canvas composition: <code>MGP.core.expose(inst)<\/code> sets <code>__duration<\/code>, <code>__seek(t)<\/code>, <code>__timelines[\"main\"]<\/code>, <code>__ready<\/code>; write <code>window.__fps = 24<\/code>. mgp: <code>mgp check<\/code>, <code>mgp contact --n 12<\/code>, <code>mgp curves<\/code>, <code>mgp render --fps 24 --workers 2<\/code> (no <code>npm install<\/code>).<\/li><li>New module: <code>MGP.core.defineModule(...)<\/code>, then <code>node lib/build.js<\/code> (writes <code>dist/<\/code> + <code>index.mjs<\/code> and tests that every file loads). JS files ASCII only.<\/li><li>Python rendering (<code>scripts/render.py<\/code>) and mgp can be mixed (<code>references/render.md<\/code>, <code>references/lib.md<\/code>).<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"mo19","title":"Advanced motion principles (v2.4)","html":"<p>This reference summarises the advanced motion principles already built into the <code>motion-graphics/lib/<\/code> library: physical springs, inertia, chained delay, constant-volume squash, constant stroke, rubberhose, the 2.5D head, FACS expressions, SLERP, rigid folding and the origami validator, Miura/Yoshimura/Kresling patterns, MoGraph fields, and curve QC with <code>mgp curves<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>Each section contains:<\/p>\n<ul><li>the principle in one or two sentences;<\/li><li>when to use it in a video;<\/li><li>the API with a short example;<\/li><li>QC reference numbers.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>Every function is deterministic: the value at second <code>t<\/code> is always the same from any seek direction, so it is safe to render frame by frame.<\/p>\n<p>Full examples are in <code>lib/examples/<\/code>:<\/p>\n<ul><li><code>motion-principles.html<\/code>: springs, inertia, delay, squash<\/li><li><code>rubberhose.html<\/code><\/li><li><code>face.html<\/code> and <code>facs.html<\/code><\/li><li><code>slerp-camera.html<\/code><\/li><li><code>fold-pattern.html<\/code><\/li><li><code>field.html<\/code><\/li><\/ul>\n<p>The ready-made blocks are <code>blocks/paper/miura<\/code>, <code>yoshimura<\/code> and <code>kresling<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h4>1. Physical springs: presets <code>bouncy<\/code>, <code>snappy<\/code>, <code>heavy<\/code><\/h4>\n<p>A spring is defined by mass m, stiffness k and damping c, not by a duration. The damping ratio ζ = c / (2√(mk)) sets its character. ζ &lt; 1 bounces, ζ = 1 arrives fastest without passing the target, and ζ &gt; 1 is slow and heavy. The response is computed in closed form, not by per-frame integration, so a random seek stays exact.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Preset<\/th><th>m<\/th><th>k<\/th><th>c<\/th><th>ζ<\/th><th>f₀<\/th><th>Natural duration (±0.5 %)<\/th><th>Overshoot<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>bouncy<\/code><\/td><td>1<\/td><td>180<\/td><td>12<\/td><td>0.45<\/td><td>2.1 Hz<\/td><td>0.88 s<\/td><td>21 %<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>snappy<\/code><\/td><td>1<\/td><td>180<\/td><td>26.8<\/td><td>1.00<\/td><td>2.1 Hz<\/td><td>0.56 s<\/td><td>0 %<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>heavy<\/code><\/td><td>4<\/td><td>160<\/td><td>75.9<\/td><td>1.50<\/td><td>1.0 Hz<\/td><td>2.27 s<\/td><td>0 %<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>When to use<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li><code>bouncy<\/code>: cheerful pop-ins of icons, stickers or cards.<\/li><li><code>snappy<\/code>: UI transitions, sliding panels, or typography that must feel tidy.<\/li><li><code>heavy<\/code>: large objects, corporate logos, or a camera that lands slowly.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>// mgp.motion (timeline): ease string, the curve shape depends only on zeta\ntl.to(&quot;.card&quot;, { y: 0, duration: mgp.motion.spring(&quot;bouncy&quot;).duration, ease: &quot;spring.bouncy&quot; });\ntl.to(&quot;.panel&quot;, { x: 400, duration: 0.6, ease: &quot;spring(1, 180, 26.8)&quot; });       // your own m, k, c\n// MGP modules / canvas\nconst y = MGP.core.springPhys(t, &quot;snappy&quot;, { from: 0, to: 100, v0: 0 });              // real seconds\nconst e = MGP.core.springEase(&quot;heavy&quot;);                                          // ease 0..1\nconst { freq, damping } = MGP.spring.fn.preset(&quot;bouncy&quot;);                     // for the old spring()/springState()\nMGP.core.springInfo({ mass: 2, stiffness: 300, damping: 20 });                  // -&gt; {zeta, frequency, ...}<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>ζ 0.3–0.5 for a friendly bounce, with 2–3 visible oscillations.<\/li><li>ζ &lt; 0.25 feels like a toy spring. Use it only on purpose.<\/li><li>ζ 0.9–1.1 for UI.<\/li><li>ζ 1.3–2 for heavy objects.<\/li><li>The tween duration should equal <code>mgp.motion.spring(name).duration<\/code>. When it is shorter, the spring feels cut off.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>The older presets <code>blockSpring<\/code>, <code>blockSpringSoft<\/code> and <code>blockSpringStrong<\/code> (old names <code>pegas<\/code>, <code>pegasLembut<\/code>, <code>pegasKuat<\/code>) in <code>block-core.js<\/code> remain (frequency 2.2/1.6/3.2 Hz with ζ 0.32/0.55/0.22), so old blocks do not change by a single pixel.<\/p>\n<h4>2. Inertia (inertial bounce)<\/h4>\n<p>After a keyframe, an object does not stop dead. It bounces in the direction of its arrival speed and damps out exponentially:<\/p>\n<p>y = y_k + v·A·sin(2πfΔt) / e^(γΔt)<\/p>\n<p>The arrival speed v is taken from the keyframe itself. An \"out\" ease that arrives slowly hardly bounces, while an \"in\" or linear ease bounces strongly.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to use<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Cards or icons that slam into place.<\/li><li>Text that stops suddenly.<\/li><li>Indicator needles.<\/li><li>A camera that stops.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>// mgp.motion: tween + bounce tail (duration grows by `tail`, default 1 s; the end value stays exact)\ntl.to(&quot;.icon&quot;, mgp.motion.inertia({ x: 600, duration: 0.5, ease: &quot;power2.in&quot; }, { amp: 0.05, freq: 2.5, decay: 5 }));\n// canvas: keyframes [[t, value, ease]] or a function + o.keys\nconst x = MGP.core.inertia([[0, 0], [0.5, 600, &quot;in&quot;], [3, 600]], t, { amp: 0.05, freq: 2, decay: 4 });<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li><code>amp<\/code> 0.03–0.08, <code>freq<\/code> 1.5–3 Hz, <code>decay<\/code> 3–6. The bounce should settle within 0.4–0.9 s.<\/li><li>The first deflection is roughly v·amp. For example v = 1200 px/s and amp = 0.05 give ±60 px, so the sensible limit is 5–10 % of the distance travelled.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>3. Chained delay (follow-through, overlap)<\/h4>\n<p>A child repeats the parent's motion with a delay per index: child_i(t) = parent(t − k·Δ·i). This is the same as <code>valueAtTime(time − delay)<\/code> in AE. Because no state is stored, the result is always the same when seeked.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to use<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Hair, tails, scarves, or chains of points.<\/li><li>Text lines that follow the previous line.<\/li><li>Groups of icons moving in a wave.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>const chain = MGP.core.delayChain((t) =&gt; parentPos(t), 5, { frame: 3, fps: 24 });   // -&gt; (t) =&gt; [x0..x4]\nconst xi = MGP.core.delay(parent, t, i, { delay: 0.1 });\n// DOM + mgp.motion: stagger is the same idea\ntl.to(&quot;.letter&quot;, { y: 0, duration: 0.5, ease: &quot;spring.bouncy&quot;, stagger: 3 / 24 });<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong>QC reference:<\/strong> overlap delay 2–4 frames (24 fps) per child; tip follow-through 4–8 frames after the parent stops. More than 6 frames per child feels like a separate motion.<\/p>\n<h4>4. Constant-volume squash &amp; stretch<\/h4>\n<p>When an object squashes or stretches, its area (2D) or volume (3D) stays the same. The rule is sx·sy = 1 in 2D and sx·sy·sz = 1 in 3D. When only scaleY changes, the object seems to inflate and deflate instead of feeling springy.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to use<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>A bouncing ball, a jumping object with a face, a landing character.<\/li><li>A button being pressed.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>const { sx, sy } = MGP.core.stretch(1.3);                // sy 1.3 -&gt; sx 0.77\nconst m = MGP.core.stretchDirection(1.4, motionAngle);          // stretch along the motion, det = 1\nconst s = MGP.core.stretchVelocity(speedPx, { k: 0.0008, max: 0.35 });\nMGP.core.volumeError(sx, sy);                            // |sx*sy - 1|\nMGP.spring.fn.bounceBallVolume(u);                       // constant-volume bouncing ball<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Contact for 1–2 frames with sy around 0.7–0.8.<\/li><li>Stretch at speed 1.15–1.35.<\/li><li>Volume error below 5 %. <code>mgp curves<\/code> flags errors above 10 %.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>5. Constant stroke while scaling<\/h4>\n<p>An ink line stays a set number of screen pixels thick while the character or icon is scaled up or down. This is the equivalent of <code>vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke<\/code> in SVG or the expression <code>value / (scale/100)<\/code> in AE.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to use<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Zooming on a line-style character.<\/li><li>Icons with animated scale.<\/li><li>A line style that must be uniform across one scene.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>MGP.characterStyles.create(&quot;#a&quot;, { style: &quot;vector&quot;, strokeFixed: 2 });    // 2 px screen ink\nMGP.vectorKit.create(&quot;#b&quot;, { strokeFixed: 1.5 });                         // every kit line (base 0.02 H) -&gt; 1.5 px\ng.lineWidth = MGP.core.fixedWidth(g, 2, dpr);                             // on your own canvas<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For SVG or DOM, use the attribute <code>vector-effect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"<\/code> on the scaled path.<\/p>\n<p><strong>QC reference:<\/strong> within one scene, ink thickness between characters must not differ by more than 1 px. The ink of a character close to the camera may be thicker only when that is intended.<\/p>\n<h4>6. Rubberhose (bendy arms and legs)<\/h4>\n<p>A limb is drawn as one arc from its root (shoulder/hip) to its tip (hand/ankle) with a constant arc length. When the distance shrinks, the limb bends into a curve. When the distance reaches full length, the limb is straight and never stretches. On a vector character, the trouser end is cut above the ankle with a *butt* cap, then checked so the line edge never drops below the sole.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to use:<\/strong> 1930s cartoon style, mascots, light explainers, and characters that must feel bendy.<\/p>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>MGP.characterStyles.create(&quot;#a&quot;, { style: &quot;vector&quot;, action: &quot;walk&quot;, limbs: &quot;rubber&quot; });\nconst r = MGP.core.rubber(shoulder, hand, 28.5, { dir: 1 });     // {pts, tip, straight, th}\nMGP.core.strokeBottom(MGP.core.trimEnds(r.pts, 1.3), lineWidth); // lowest y of the line, to check against the sole<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Arc length the same in every frame (difference below 0.5 %).<\/li><li>Knees bend forwards and elbows backwards.<\/li><li>The lowest point of the leg is always above the sole. Test result: −0.40 units over 200 walk poses.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>7. 2.5D head from 5 poses<\/h4>\n<p>A flat face seems to turn in three dimensions when each feature moves at a different speed (parallax). The nose, furthest forward, moves most; the eyes and mouth move a medium amount; the ears and back hair move the opposite way. Features on the far side are also compressed.<\/p>\n<p>Five extreme poses (center, left, right, top, bottom) are blended by a joystick (jx, jy):<\/p>\n<p>P = C + max(jx,0)(R−C) + max(−jx,0)(L−C) + max(jy,0)(B−C) + max(−jy,0)(T−C)<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to use:<\/strong> a narrator who turns towards a chart, a character glancing aside, or a quick look round, without drawing a separate 3/4 view.<\/p>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>MGP.face.create(&quot;#stage&quot;, { joystick: [[0, [0, 0]], [1.2, [1, 0]], [3, [-0.6, 0.3]]], expression: &quot;happy&quot; });\nconst pose = MGP.face.poseHead(MGP.face.POSE_HEAD, jx, jy);   // or 5 poses of your own (any nested number object)<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>A full turn (|jx| = 1) needs only 0.4–0.7 s, with ease in/out or <code>spring.snappy<\/code>.<\/li><li>Eyes and head move first, then the body follows 2–4 frames later.<\/li><li>Nose parallax below 35 % of the face width. Beyond that the face looks broken.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>8. FACS-lite expressions (Action Units)<\/h4>\n<p>Expressions are built from separate muscle contractions, no longer from one emotion slider. The available units:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit<\/th><th>Motion<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>AU1<\/td><td>inner brow raise<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU2<\/td><td>outer brow raise<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU4<\/td><td>brow lower<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU5<\/td><td>upper lid raise<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU6<\/td><td>cheek raise<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU7<\/td><td>lid tighten<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU12<\/td><td>lip corner pull up<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU15<\/td><td>lip corner press down<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU23<\/td><td>lip tighten<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AU26<\/td><td>jaw drop<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>blink<\/td><td>lids closed<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Emotions already used in other modules are mapped to AUs in <code>MGP.face.EMOTION<\/code>. Combining with lip-sync does not clash because the jaw is shared: jaw = max(viseme, AU26). Lip corners are still added while talking (smiling while talking), but damped when the viseme is round (O/U).<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Emotion<\/th><th>AU<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>happy / smile<\/td><td>AU6 + AU12<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>sad<\/td><td>AU1 + AU4 + AU15<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>surprised<\/td><td>AU1 + AU2 + AU5 + AU26<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>angry<\/td><td>AU4 + AU5 + AU7 + AU23<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>unsure<\/td><td>AU2 + light AU4 + AU15 + AU23<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>When to use:<\/strong> a narrator who reacts while talking, an object with a face showing mixed emotions (for example \"unsure smile\" = AU12 0.4 + AU1 0.5), and a vector kit that needs expressions beyond the eight presets.<\/p>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>MGP.lipsync.create(&quot;#a&quot;, { text: &quot;...&quot;, expression: [[0, &quot;neutral&quot;], [1.5, &quot;happy&quot;], [3, { AU1: 0.6, AU12: 0.3 }]] });\nMGP.vectorKit.create(&quot;#b&quot;, { expr: (t) =&gt; MGP.face.toVectorKit(MGP.face.auAt(keys, t)) });\nMGP.chibiObject.create(&quot;#c&quot;, { faces: { focus: MGP.face.toChibi({ AU4: 0.6, AU12: 0.3 }) }, moods: [[0, &quot;focus&quot;]] });\nconst F = MGP.face.merge(MGP.face.au(&quot;happy&quot;), MGP.lipsync.at(track, t));   // combined face parameters<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Transitions between expressions 0.2–0.35 s (option <code>transition<\/code>, default 0.25 s).<\/li><li>Blink 0.1–0.15 s every 2.5–5 s.<\/li><li>Eyes and brows change 2–3 frames before the mouth.<\/li><li>Keep AU26 from emotions below 0.4 while talking, so the visemes stay readable.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>9. Quaternion SLERP for 3D rotation<\/h4>\n<p>A rotation is represented by a unit quaternion and interpolated along the shortest arc at constant angular speed (Shoemake, 1985). Euler interpolation (azimuth and elevation lerped separately) can swing the camera a long way round the object, and near a top view it can hit gimbal lock or jumps.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to use:<\/strong> an orbit camera moving between distant angles, especially past a top view; 3D joints rotating more than 90° on two axes at once.<\/p>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>MGP.sceneTanker.create(&quot;#a&quot;, { cameraKeys: [...], cameraInterp: &quot;slerp&quot; });   // default stays &quot;euler&quot; (old pixels unchanged)\nconst q = MGP.core.slerp(MGP.core.quatEuler(0, 30), MGP.core.quatEuler(170, 60), k);\nconst direction = MGP.core.slerpDirection(az0, el0, az1, el1, k);                      // camera {az, el} without roll\nMGP.core.quatRotate(q, [0, 0, 1]);<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Camera moves of more than 120° should use <code>slerp<\/code>.<\/li><li>Elevation is limited to 84° in <code>sceneTanker<\/code>, because a straight-down view makes the projection undefined.<\/li><li>Camera angular speed below 60°/s for calm scenes.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>10. Rigid folding and the crease pattern validator<\/h4>\n<p>In rigid folding, every panel stays flat and only rotates about its hinges. Shared edges between panels must never come apart; panels that come apart look like \"broken glass\".<\/p>\n<p>A flat-foldable crease pattern must meet local conditions at every interior vertex:<\/p>\n<ul><li><strong>Maekawa:<\/strong> |M − V| = 2.<\/li><li><strong>Kawasaki:<\/strong> the alternating angles sum to 180°.<\/li><li><strong>Even degree:<\/strong> the faces can be coloured with two colours.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>Global collisions between layers (NP-hard) are not checked.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When to use:<\/strong> before animating your own origami pattern, and to check your own fold transitions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>const v = MGP.origamiFold.validatePattern({ titik: [[0,0],[1,0],[0,1],[-1,0],[0,-1]], lipatan: [[0,1,&quot;M&quot;],[0,2,&quot;M&quot;],[0,3,&quot;M&quot;],[0,4,&quot;V&quot;]] });\n// v.ok, v.peringatan (warnings): [&quot;titik 0 (0, 0): Kawasaki gagal, ...&quot;]\nMGP.paperFoldTransition.create(&quot;#a&quot;, { from, to, rigid: true });              // the second crease waits until the first is flat\nMGP.paperFoldTransition.lib.celahKaku(pose);                                 // hinge gap in px<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The input keys <code>titik<\/code> (vertices) and <code>lipatan<\/code> (creases) and the result key <code>peringatan<\/code> (warnings) keep their names.<\/p>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Hinge gap below 1 px. A z layer offset of 2–6 px to prevent z-fighting is still fine.<\/li><li>Miura edge error below 1e-12 (rigid folding). The cylinder patterns are not rigid by nature: Yoshimura about 30–45 % and Kresling about 30–85 % at fold 0.3–1.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>11. Tessellated patterns: Miura-ori, Yoshimura, Kresling<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern<\/th><th>Motion<\/th><th>Property<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Miura-ori<\/td><td>Zigzag parallelograms. One degree of freedom: one fold parameter opens the whole sheet at once and compacts it in two directions.<\/td><td>Rigid, computed in closed form<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Yoshimura<\/td><td>A diamond cylinder that compresses<\/td><td>Not rigid<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Kresling<\/td><td>A tower that twists while it sinks<\/td><td>Not rigid; a source of bistability<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>When to use<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Miura: a title or map reveal \"unfolded from the creases\".<\/li><li>Yoshimura: a compression or spring accent.<\/li><li>Kresling: a mechanical accent, loading, or a twisting transition.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>MGP.foldPattern.create(&quot;#a&quot;, { pattern: &quot;miura&quot;, motion: &quot;open&quot;, fill: (g, w, h) =&gt; { /* title card */ }, ease: &quot;spring.snappy&quot; });\nMGP.foldPattern.create(&quot;#b&quot;, { pattern: &quot;kresling&quot;, N: 6, levels: 3, rotate: -10 });\nconst m = MGP.foldPattern.miura({ kolom: 12, baris: 6, lipat: 0.5 });   // {titik, datar, panel, lipatan}\nMGP.foldPattern.validate(m); MGP.foldPattern.checkIsometry(m, MGP.foldPattern.miura({ lipat: 0 }));<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The model functions (<code>miura<\/code>, <code>yoshimura<\/code>, <code>kresling<\/code>) keep their Indonesian keys: <code>kolom<\/code> = columns, <code>baris<\/code> = rows, <code>lipat<\/code> = fold; the result <code>{titik, datar, panel, lipatan}<\/code> = vertices, flat pattern, panels, creases.<\/p>\n<p>Add the block with <code>mgp add paper/miura --into index.html --prop title=\"Chapter 2\"<\/code>; the same works for <code>paper/yoshimura<\/code> and <code>paper/kresling<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Open a Miura sheet in 1.5–2.5 s with <code>spring.snappy<\/code>.<\/li><li>Crease lines fade out as the sheet goes flat.<\/li><li>Text on the sheet at least 4 % of the screen height once open.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>12. Fields (MoGraph: cloner + effector + field)<\/h4>\n<p>Many elements are driven by one field, without keyframes per object. Clones are placed by a cloner (grid, linear, radial, text glyphs). An effector adds a change multiplied by the field weight F(x, t) ∈ [0, 1]:<\/p>\n<p>T' = T + ΔT·F(x, t)<\/p>\n<p>Effector types (the ids keep their Indonesian names):<\/p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>polos<\/code> (plain):<\/strong> T + ΔT·F.<\/li><li><strong><code>acak<\/code> (random):<\/strong> seeded random per clone.<\/li><li><strong><code>rumus<\/code> (formula):<\/strong> sin(i/n + t) and the like.<\/li><li><strong><code>tunda<\/code> (delay):<\/strong> the field is read at t − delay.<\/li><li><strong><code>pegas<\/code> (spring):<\/strong> the field is passed through the impulse response of a spring. The result is a windowed integral recomputed from t, so it stays deterministic.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>When to use:<\/strong> bar charts that grow as a sweep passes, per-letter kinetic typography, grid backgrounds that react to a focus point, and logos assembled from hundreds of elements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>API<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>const { clone, field, effect } = MGP.field;\nconst list = clone.grid({ kolom: 16, baris: 9, jarak: [60, 60], pusat: [640, 360] });   // columns, rows, spacing, centre\nconst bola = field.bola({ pusat: (t) =&gt; [200 + 300 * t, 360], r: 220 });                 // sphere field\nconst out = MGP.field.apply(list, [effect.pegas(bola, { skala: 0.8, rot: 90 }, { pegas: &quot;bouncy&quot; }), effect.acak(field.noise({}), { y: 6 })], t);\nMGP.field.draw(g, out, &quot;kotak&quot;, { ukuran: 30 });                                         // box, size\nMGP.field.create(&quot;#b&quot;, { mode: &quot;bars&quot;, data: [34, 58, 41, 77] });   // demo: grid | bars | text | free<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The inner cloner, field and effector functions and their parameters keep their Indonesian names (see <code>lib/README.md<\/code>).<\/p>\n<p><strong>QC reference<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>A linear field sweep crosses one row in 1.5–2.5 s.<\/li><li>A field spring uses <code>bouncy<\/code> for cheerful data and <code>snappy<\/code> for serious data.<\/li><li>Random offset below 15 % of the clone spacing, so the grid stays readable.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>13. Curve QC: <code>mgp curves<\/code><\/h4>\n<p>mgp seeks the composition frame by frame and records the position, scale, rotation and opacity of every moving element. The result is drawn as a value graph and a speed graph, like the Graph Editor in AE. Findings are marked with coloured bands:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Finding<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><th>Threshold<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>linear<\/code><\/td><td>Constant speed with an abrupt start/stop (robotic motion)<\/td><td>Speed variation below 6 % over ≥ 5 frames, with segment ends above 60 % of the peak<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>pop<\/code><\/td><td>A speed spike not supported by its neighbouring frames<\/td><td>Δspeed per frame above 25 % of the peak and above 2.5× the neighbours<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>abrupt<\/code><\/td><td>Rest to fast (or the reverse) within 1 frame<\/td><td>Segment end above 60 % of the peak<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>volume<\/code><\/td><td>Non-uniform scale with sx·sy deviating<\/td><td>More than 10 %<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>With <code>MGP_LANG=id<\/code> the labels read <code>linier<\/code>, <code>patah<\/code>, <code>mendadak<\/code>, <code>volume<\/code>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp curves index.html -o curves.png                 # elements picked automatically (their own transform/opacity changes)\nmgp curves index.html --selector &quot;#title, .card&quot; --json<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>A canvas composition can provide <code>window.__curves = (t) =&gt; ({ name: number })<\/code>. Those channels are analysed too.<\/p>\n<p>Notes on reading the result:<\/p>\n<ul><li>An impact (a bouncing ball) is a <code>pop<\/code> in position by nature, so ignore it when it is intended.<\/li><li><code>abrupt<\/code> at the start of an \"out\" ease is the pop-in style. Switch to <code>spring.snappy<\/code> when it feels harsh.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Reference numbers at a glance<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item<\/th><th>Number<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Anticipation<\/td><td>2–5 frames in the opposite direction before the action; scale 85–95 % before shooting off<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Overlap per child<\/td><td>2–4 frames (24 fps)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Tip follow-through<\/td><td>4–8 frames after the parent stops<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Overshoot<\/td><td>1–2 frames at the extreme pose; 5–20 % of the distance (<code>bouncy<\/code> 21 %)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Spring ζ<\/td><td>0.3–0.5 cheerful · 0.9–1.1 UI · 1.3–2 heavy<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Inertia<\/td><td>amp 0.03–0.08 · freq 1.5–3 Hz · decay 3–6<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Squash<\/td><td>contact 1–2 frames, sy 0.7–0.8; stretch 1.15–1.35; volume error &lt; 5 %<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Blink<\/td><td>0.1–0.15 s every 2.5–5 s<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Expression transition<\/td><td>0.2–0.35 s; eyes/brows 2–3 frames before the mouth<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2.5D head turn<\/td><td>0.4–0.7 s<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Rigid-fold hinge gap<\/td><td>&lt; 1 px<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"}],"title":"Motion Graphics (After Effects principles in code)","intro":"<p>General rules for any character and project. To draw the character, use the vector-character skill first. This skill governs taste and motion. Traits and parameters specific to one character/project go in the project files, not in this skill. Palette, fonts and style always follow the brief/brand of the requested project (ask when there is none), never a fixed palette.<\/p>"},"vector":{"chapters":[{"id":"ve0","title":"Main rules","html":"<ol><li><strong>Redraw, do not nudge points.<\/strong> If a shape is wrong (especially in a certain pose), redraw that shape for that pose (pose-space) or draw a new one. Nudging numbers over and over is forbidden.<\/li><li><strong>Measure, do not judge at a glance.<\/strong> Proportions are checked with numbers (arm width perpendicular to its axis at several points, landmark positions, leg reach), not by looking at the full sheet.<\/li><li><strong>One fix must not break another part.<\/strong> Every delivery must include ONE regression sheet (chapter 11.2) holding every spot ever complained about, compared with the previous sheet.<\/li><li><strong>Hidden parts must exist.<\/strong> Everything covered (under the hair, under the sleeve, the back under the hair) is drawn, because when the part in front moves, the background shows through.<\/li><li><strong>Do not send what already looks broken in your own zoom.<\/strong> Fix it first or state honestly that it has not passed.<\/li><li><strong>Per-character spec goes in the project files, not in the skill.<\/strong> Typical sizes (accessory thickness, sleeve width, hair parting side, hand length) are measured from that character's reference and written as constants in the project code/notes.<\/li><li><strong>Check in the scene, not only on the sheet.<\/strong> Every shape/rig change is checked in a scene frame at the final camera distance, at the critical angles (0, 90, 180, 270 + the hardest angle), side by side with the accepted version, BEFORE moving to the next fix. Stop long autonomous work and report as soon as one scene frame looks worse than the accepted version. (Review Results, rig v4 test project: a dozen stacked fixes checked only on the angle sheet and zooms; the front/back orbit frames were \"wrecked\": long thin legs, narrow body.)<\/li><li><strong>Copy the reference = measure the reference.<\/strong> When the user says \"copy the kit\"/\"not alike yet\", do not guess and do not ask \"what is missing\": measure the reference yourself (colour masks, width per row, hairline per x, the correct H height), overlay its outline on the character at the same H scale (<code>scripts/measure_ref.py<\/code>), then change the shape to those numbers. Always send reference / old version / new version side by side. (Review Results, kit test project: the hair was redrawn 6 times from rough numbers and stayed a \"wig\"; it was solved after measuring.)<\/li><li><strong>Reply in the user's language.<\/strong> For Indonesian users, write fully in Bahasa Indonesia, and render user-facing templates (T1 brief, gate messages, reports) in Indonesian using the ID template variants.<\/li><\/ol>"},{"id":"ve1","title":"Mistakes that must NOT be repeated","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mistake<\/th><th>Result<\/th><th>Do instead<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Assembling the body from separate parts (head, neck, torso, arms, hands, trousers, shoes) and then joining them<\/td><td>Shoes do not merge with the legs, hands do not merge with the body, it feels like \"a vector collage\"<\/td><td>ONE whole silhouette first, other parts = cut lines inside it (chapter 2); or, for a flat SVG style, layers per material with exactly the same joining curve (chapter 14)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Stuck-on geometric blocks (capsules, circles, rects) for the body<\/td><td>Stiff, like pipes<\/td><td>Pen tool: few anchors, long handles, flowing curves<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Drawing a new character from \"blind\" coordinates without a measured skeleton<\/td><td>Stiff, proportions off, rejected again and again<\/td><td>Measured pose skeleton first (chapter 1.3), hard parts designed in local coordinates, zoom check per part<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Stiff symmetric pose without gesture<\/td><td>Paper doll<\/td><td>Line of action + clear weight support; when walking the upper body leans 3-4 degrees<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Excessive contrapposto on a man (hip pushed out, hourglass waist, curved legs, tilted head)<\/td><td>Reads feminine, legs \"bent all over the place\"<\/td><td>Man: upright stance, feet hip-width and straight (knees above the feet), hips almost level, waist = hips (V taper), head upright<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Square face (live-corner jaw) or too round (jaw 0.62 H wide)<\/td><td>Not natural<\/td><td>Inverted egg: jaw angle width about 0.46 H, narrow chin (chapter 3)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Facial features misplaced / squinting eyes<\/td><td>Out of proportion, not friendly<\/td><td>Neda grid + chapter 3 numbers, eyes open enough, thin closed smile<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3/4 / side nose stuck on as a shape at the face edge<\/td><td>A pink shape \"stuck\" outside the cheek<\/td><td>The nose bump goes into the face contour; inside the face only a small shadow<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fingers pulled as dents in one path<\/td><td>\"Sloppy\"<\/td><td>Hand from palm + 4 finger capsules + thumb, United (chapter 3)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Waving palm reversed<\/td><td>Back of the hand faces front<\/td><td>Arm raised about 160 degrees without rotating the forearm → use the MIRRORED open-hand set (palm facing front, thumb toward the head)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hat/helmet: cranium outside the shell, hair not wrapping the ear (profile)<\/td><td>Hat looks stuck on<\/td><td>Occiput inside the shell, back edge lower than the front, short hair wraps around the ear<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Uniform limb width<\/td><td>Pipes<\/td><td>Anatomical profile (shoulder, biceps, forearm, thigh, knee, calf); calf side fuller than the shin side<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hard band/uniform offset shading, or computed per piece<\/td><td>Fake, seams visible<\/td><td>Shadow computed on the union of the garment group, then clipped per piece<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Rubber-hose curving along the whole limb<\/td><td>Like noodles<\/td><td>Straight bones, only the joints rounded<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Focusing on anchors, forgetting colour/shape proportions<\/td><td>Unpleasant to look at<\/td><td>Check silhouette + proportions first, detail last<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Wide neck in the middle of the head<\/td><td>Unnaturally thick neck<\/td><td>Neck about 0.45-0.47 of the head width, coming out from behind the jaw<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Armpit/shirt stretches when the arm goes up (weights pulled)<\/td><td>Armpit like webbing<\/td><td>Armpit and shoulder redrawn per pose (chapter 8)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Concave (\"picak\") or lumpy shoulder when the arm goes up<\/td><td>Shoulder does not read<\/td><td>Shoulder curve drawn per pose, tangent to the trapezius and the top edge of the arm (chapter 8)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Joint shapes switched with a toggle (if/else on the angle)<\/td><td>In animation \"first attached, then suddenly snaps off\"<\/td><td>All joint shapes change CONTINUOUSLY with the angle (chapter 8)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Side view: armhole of the outer garment (vest, etc.) is a large ellipse filled with the inner shirt<\/td><td>A \"lump\" of another colour appears on the body when the arm swings<\/td><td>Armhole the size of the sleeve, hidden when the arm is still (chapter 2)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Only checking key poses / only the part complained about<\/td><td>Other broken parts go unseen<\/td><td>Check the whole figure part by part with zoom + a sweep video of every joint (chapter 7)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Nudging numbers over and over for a shape problem<\/td><td>Never finishes (\"don't just nudge the points\")<\/td><td>Change the METHOD: draw the actual anatomical shape for that pose (chapter 8)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>A \"too long\" hand fixed by SHRINKING the hand<\/td><td>Broken fingers, small out-of-proportion hand<\/td><td>Keep the hand at its real size; shorten the ARM, hide the wrist in the cuff<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Arm-body gap on a hanging arm<\/td><td>Arm looks small/thin<\/td><td>Sleeve overlaps in front of the body edge (no gap), unless the reference shows a gap<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Arm width not compared with body width<\/td><td>Arm too small<\/td><td>Measure the ratio upper arm width : body width against the reference (chapter 11)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Left and right hands swapped / palm reversed<\/td><td>\"the view is reversed\"<\/td><td>Right hand = mirror of the left hand; thumb direction checked against the reference<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Arm stretched by pulling the body silhouette<\/td><td>A \"wing\" under the arm<\/td><td>Arm = a separate sleeve tube (fixed thickness, measured from the reference) + a small armpit fillet<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Accessories (scarf, bag, hat) thicker/larger than the reference<\/td><td>\"too thick\"<\/td><td>Measure the accessory thickness in H from the reference before drawing<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hair parting/fringe direction guessed<\/td><td>\"wrong direction\"<\/td><td>Read the parting side from the reference (viewer's left/right), write it as a constant in the character spec<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3/4 view from a 2.5D cylinder warp of the front view<\/td><td>Rejected: face/body without perspective<\/td><td>3/4 view = a new drawing, preferably TRACED from the reference (chapter 12); for orbits with many angles: loft the volume from the accepted front + side views (chapter 16), NOT a warp of one drawing<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3/4 / side view redrawn by hand without a reference<\/td><td>\"weird\", flat head, shirt and trousers do not join<\/td><td>Trace from the reference when there is one (chapter 12)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Small specks / small holes left from tracing, dents on the face edge<\/td><td>Dirty, looks amateur<\/td><td>Clean up automatically (chapter 11, chapter 12)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Waiting for the user to point out the same mistake again and again<\/td><td>\"I don't want to keep asking for the same fixes\"<\/td><td>Run the chapter 11 self-check on every render BEFORE sending<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Raised arm narrowing toward the armpit (\"funnel\")<\/td><td>Out of proportion<\/td><td>At rest the inner side of the sleeve looks narrower because the body covers it; when raised switch to an even, wide TUBE, rising smoothly with the angle (smoothstep 5-40 degrees). Measure the width perpendicular to the arm axis at 5 points<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Balloon forearm when the arm is raised<\/td><td>\"it should be straight\"<\/td><td>Raised arm: forearm edge straight from elbow to cuff; balloon only when the arm hangs<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Wide elbow bone blend (LBS)<\/td><td>Outline pulled into teeth/slivers at the elbow<\/td><td>Sleeve split at the elbow hinge into 2 rigid pieces + a joint circle (racket-ball) the width of the tube<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Nothing under the hair/sleeve<\/td><td>Hair \"wrecked\", background shows through when moving<\/td><td>Continue the sleeve under the hair; body panel down to the body side under the arm; back under the hair (side); nape in hair colour under the collar<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Dark cover layer follows the chest bone but covers the head<\/td><td>Dark stroke peeks out when the head tilts<\/td><td>The gap cover layer only on the body<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Winding (made-up) arm-body cut line<\/td><td>Wavy underside of the arm when raised<\/td><td>Straight cut line, descending smoothly to the inner edge of the cuff<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Armpit patch using a wide-zone vector closing<\/td><td>Loose shirt piece floating between arm and body<\/td><td>Patch = a simple pose-space shape (deltoid + armpit wedge)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Traced Catmull-Rom contour from sparse anchors<\/td><td>Thin needles/spikes on the cuff and body<\/td><td>Dense anchors (approxPolyDP eps 0.6 px) + remove spikes (angle &lt; 40 degrees)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Traced face used raw<\/td><td>Eyes become blobs (reference about 90 px per H)<\/td><td>Vector face: outline, eyes, brows, nose, lips at positions measured from the reference<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3/4 face profile guessed<\/td><td>Hair covers the face, deformed face<\/td><td>Far-side profile edge = leftmost face skin per reference row; hairline = measured fringe curve from the parting to above the ear<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Pathfinder (PathOps) results trusted blindly<\/td><td>Holes without an error (union area smaller than the parts), or RuntimeError on dense polygons (skia engine)<\/td><td>Check the garment union area (<code>vkit.area<\/code>), retry with simplified/shifted input; on the skia engine wrap <code>brush.union<\/code>/<code>brush.subtract<\/code> in try/except + <code>brush.simplify<\/code>; the pure engine computes booleans exactly per sample (no error), still check the area<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Waving hand: thumb pointing away from the head, wrist sunk into the cuff<\/td><td>Only the thumb stub is visible<\/td><td>Thumb toward the head, wrist out about 0.12 H from the cuff<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Flat style given too much detail (ribs, strands, dense fringe, heavy grain, jagged contour)<\/td><td>\"not simple\", less elegant than the reference<\/td><td>Chapter 10.1: little purposeful detail, smoothed contours<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3/4 torso narrower than the front view<\/td><td>\"body shape too thin\"<\/td><td>3/4 chest, waist, hip width at least equal to the front view, because a body turned 45 degrees shows part of its width plus part of its depth (chapter 14.5). In the loft: boxy body cross-section (dual exponent m 1.25), not an ellipse<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3/4 far arm comes out below the shoulder line of the shirt panel; near shoulder sloped<\/td><td>\"the arms dropped\", arm root looks like it comes from the side of the chest<\/td><td>Body panel and arm share one shoulder curve (trapezius to deltoid); near shoulder upright and somewhat square (chapter 14.5)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fist with two symmetric bumps underneath + a centre line<\/td><td>Does not read as a hand<\/td><td>Side-view fist: finger side with small steps + 2 short strokes, bottom smoothly rounded (chapter 14.7)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3/4 view facing the other way made by mirroring the finished drawing<\/td><td>Shadow moves to the light side; buttons, hair parting, bag, arms swap sides<\/td><td>Mirror only the leg, body, face skeleton; redraw hair, arms, bag, buttons, shadows (chapter 14.6)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>T-shirt tucked in but drawn after the trousers<\/td><td>T-shirt covers the buttons and waistband of the trousers<\/td><td>T-shirt drawn before the trousers in all views<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Relaxed hand placed exactly at the side of the thigh<\/td><td>Hair-thin background line and 1-4 px holes between hand and thigh<\/td><td>Hand overlaps in front of the thigh side; drawn after the shirt body, then cut by the sleeve (<code>diff(hand, sleeve)<\/code>)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hand drawn before the outer shirt<\/td><td>Shirt hem covers the hand root; hand seems to come out from behind the body<\/td><td>A hand in front of the body is drawn after the shirt, cut by its own sleeve<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Straight bottom edge of the trouser pelvis piece<\/td><td>Far thigh looks cut square<\/td><td>Pelvis bottom edge = crotch curve merging into the root of the far thigh<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Neck shadow as a horizontal band<\/td><td>Reads as a necklace<\/td><td>Neck shadow = the face shape shifted down about 0.075 H, clipped to the neck<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3/4 outer hair contour starts inside the face line<\/td><td>Forehead pierces the head top (skin notch)<\/td><td>Outer hair contour always outside the skull; the hairline sits inside the face<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Box-shaped / flat-topped shadows<\/td><td>Notches and bands that do not follow the form<\/td><td>Shadow shape follows the material contour; if needed clip to <code>diff(body, sleeve)<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Smooth dome hair; back hair a dark block<\/td><td>\"helmet\"<\/td><td>Small dip at the parting, more volume on the long side, round occiput tapering to the nape, flicked ends; back hair in base colour + shadow only at the bottom<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Side/back view freely guessed when the reference lacks that view<\/td><td>Inconsistent with other views<\/td><td>Check all references first; if none, tell the user, then build from the character's own measurements (landmarks, bone lengths, widths of other views) + chapter 15 rules<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Flat object (tote bag) in side view drawn fully facing without computing<\/td><td>Inconsistent with front/3/4<\/td><td>Compute from the other views (3/4 width = front width x cos 45); in side view the object is seen edge-on; offer the option of turning it 45 degrees<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fringe and profile hair as two separate paths<\/td><td>Hooks/spikes on the forehead, jagged temple hairline<\/td><td>One outer hair contour (fringe, top, occiput) shared by both side directions; the fringe joins the same contour<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Form shadow on the hair<\/td><td>Hairline reads as a headband<\/td><td>Hair without form shade; just strands + a shadow under the nape (same as front and 3/4)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Long-side hair on the far side given its own silhouette<\/td><td>Reads as a ponytail/mullet<\/td><td>Back hair silhouette exactly the same as the view facing the other way; the far side only differs in tone<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Profile ear the same colour as the cheek with no border<\/td><td>Ear does not read<\/td><td>Back edge of the ear overlaps the hair + inner stroke + thin tragus stroke (alpha 0.3)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Chin shadow on the neck from the face contour extended under the hair<\/td><td>Wavy band under the ear<\/td><td>Face skin may extend under the hair, but the neck shadow uses the original face contour<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>1-20 px cavities under the ear, at the strap-fist join, at the end of the arm-body gap<\/td><td>Background specks<\/td><td>Skin and neck continue under the hair; the strap goes 0.05 H into the fist (fist drawn on top); close the end of the gap with a small ellipse<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Union of garment panels whose edges almost coincide<\/td><td>Thin background line showing through on the back<\/td><td>Bridge piece goes 0.06 H into the panel; check deviating pixels in the garment area<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Mirrored view shadow shifted by hand<\/td><td>Wrong shadow side<\/td><td>form_shade in screen space (chapter 15.3): automatically right in the mirrored view<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Pointed side sneaker toe<\/td><td>Reads as flat shoes<\/td><td>Rounded toe box, same as the 3/4 view<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Contact pose stride not measured<\/td><td>Stride is only half a realistic stride without noticing<\/td><td>Compute the stride from the skeleton and compare with gait data (motion-graphics chapter 4.3 (<code>motion-graphics/references/walk.md<\/code>)) before locking the pose<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>A still view drawing used for orbit angles and then slid along the path<\/td><td>\"It moves like it's sliding\"<\/td><td>Multi-angle rig from one 3D skeleton (chapter 16)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Loft: the nose follows the face volume<\/td><td>Wide snout at 3/4 angles<\/td><td>Smooth the front depth of the face at the nose rows; nose = a separate 3D feature, merged into the silhouette only in profile (sin v &gt; 0.5) (chapter 16)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Loft: hairline closed in the wrong direction<\/td><td>Hair covers the face (left profile) or a bald spot at the top (67.5 degrees)<\/td><td>Hairline table per ring angle, closed at the silhouette edge (chapter 16.5)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Bag turned 45 degrees the wrong way<\/td><td>Bag seen edge-on in front 3/4<\/td><td>Bag yaw chosen so that the bag face faces the camera in the reference 3/4 view; edge-on only in back 3/4<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Loft: box face (bag) = the corner pair with the largest screen span<\/td><td>In orthographic projection the front and back faces are ALWAYS equally wide: the choice is decided by float rounding (bag mirrored between Linux and Windows), and the \"second side\" is always the opposite face, so the hatched face and light gusset (reversed from the reference) at every angle<\/td><td>Pick face/gusset from the normal facing the camera (<code>facing &gt;= -1e-9<\/code>, eps so an exact side-on view stays deterministic) (chapter 16.4)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Loft: hairline = front-view points + per-case end-closing rules<\/td><td>Square ends (square sideburns) in front of the ear at 45-90 degrees, fringe flat like a visor<\/td><td>Hairline = a y table per ring angle that wraps around the head, closed at the silhouette edge (chapter 16.5)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Loft: skin = face minus hair cover<\/td><td>Hair-thin skin slivers at the face silhouette edge (a \"helmet\" line at the head top), because the cover edge coincides with the face edge<\/td><td>Skin = face minus the hairline POLYGON (jutting out of the silhouette); the cover is filled with hair colour + the same hair shadow<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Loft: neck drawn on top of the back hair<\/td><td>Light neck box behind the jaw (nape)<\/td><td>The hair cover also covers the neck root down to the nape (test character example y 0.95)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Loft: mouth only from front surface points<\/td><td>Mouth disappears in profile (centre point side-on to the camera)<\/td><td>One stroke whose position shifts smoothly from the front point to the reference profile stroke (smoothstep abs(sin v) 0.80-0.97), not a crossfade of two strokes<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Front-view sole band = rectangle with a top edge sloping from heel to ball<\/td><td>When the heel lifts (toe-off) the shoe becomes a tall gray block<\/td><td>Sole band = full 3D silhouette minus the silhouette of the part above the midsole; toe cap, laces, heel stitching = curves on the 3D shoe surface (chapter 16.2)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>One hand shape (side) at every rig angle<\/td><td>Hand in the wrong view at front/back, thumb backward on the left side<\/td><td>Shape keys per angle + contour morph (chapter 16.2)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Orthographic billboard in a perspective scene, then only the legs perspective-corrected<\/td><td>Foot slip gone (57 px → 0.2 px), but legs get long and thin when the camera is in front/behind, body stays orthographic: \"wrecked\"<\/td><td>Correct the whole figure consistently, or stay orthographic and fix slip with a stance-foot anchor; measure slip (px) AND look at scene frames before use (chapter 16.1)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Rig parts never checked in the scene<\/td><td>Pass on the angle sheet, broken in orbit frames<\/td><td>Rule 00.7: scene frames at critical angles side by side with the accepted version at every step<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Kit style: front view drawn as one finished painting<\/td><td>Arms stuck to the body, not riggable, other views inconsistent (\"the workflow is not right\")<\/td><td>Piece kit first, views = assemblies (chapter 1 step 9, chapter 5.1)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Female body = neutral body with long hair<\/td><td>\"the female body shape is a bit different\"<\/td><td>Female body parameters + type comparison sheet (chapter 3)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Kit proportions set with a screen warp (y x KY, x x KX) on top of the pieces<\/td><td>Joint circles become ellipses, capsule sides are no longer tangent lines: knee/hip joint ends stick out of the trousers (\"the leg rig is outside the trousers\"); pieces not rigid when rotated<\/td><td>Draw the pieces directly at final size; joints = circles of equal radius at the pivot + outer tangent sides (chapter 5.1); limb bulge zero at the ends<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Kit side view from the neutral body depth<\/td><td>\"thin as paper\" (knee 0.48 H, waist 0.71 vs kit 0.74 / 0.95)<\/td><td>Measure the kit side depth per row and use 85-100%: chest 0.87, waist 0.76, hem 0.86, thigh 0.80, knee 0.63, calf 0.64 H; buttocks in the pelvis piece<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Wavy hair from many narrow strands unioned<\/td><td>Reads as rope/noodles or dreadlocks<\/td><td>Large masses (\"curtains\") with two wavy edges IN PHASE, 2-4 flicked curl ends, strands follow the wave; crown + curtain unioned into one path; close the nape gap in side/back views<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Kit hand = one blob (bulb) + strokes, or long straight fingers<\/td><td>\"make the fingers more detailed\"; straight fingers read as a paddle/claw<\/td><td>Palm + 4 fingers as 3-segment chains + thumb; kit relaxed pose = a loose C-shaped grip (fingers bent at the middle joint, stepped base, thumb in front with a gap + dark contact), length 0.55-0.60 H<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\"Hand-made\" style imitated with noise (wobbly edges, shaky lines, grain) and small procedural details (nails, segments, knuckles, parallel hair strands, repeating sine waves)<\/td><td>\"stiff\", \"it all looks so AI\"<\/td><td>Design shapes like an illustrator: hand silhouette with a manual pen (C grip, stepped finger dips, 2-3 lines), hair waves from key points that grow downward + varied pointed strand ends + separation lines from notches; smooth pressure lines; saturation on par with the reference; check palette choices (e.g. \"sage\" must show)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hair redrawn many times from rough coordinates (light lines, layered strands, pulling tighter)<\/td><td>\"like a hat or a wig stuck on\", \"even worse\"<\/td><td>MEASURE the reference head first (hair/skin mask, the correct H = hair top to chin): top width, hairline per x, width at cheek and jaw; overlay the reference outline at the same H scale. Wig = wide flat top + forehead open to the temples + straight sides without jaw volume + 3/4 without far-side hair + back shaped like a cloak<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hand holding an object from separate finger capsules<\/td><td>\"the way it grips, ugh.. the fingers\"<\/td><td>One hand shape: palm behind the object joined to the wrist + one jagged fingertip blob at the front edge of the object (tone wedges in the notches) + thick thumb lobe; object upright in the world<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Rig explode sheet with spacing smaller than the joint overlap<\/td><td>Pieces still touch; does not read as a kit<\/td><td>Explode spacing = 1 joint circle diameter + a 0.05-0.06 H gap<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"ve2","title":"Work order","html":"<ol><li>Read the brief + previous notes (HANDOVER, PROGRESS, character spec) word by word. If there is a character reference image: trace (chapter 12). If there is a video reference, look at its key frames (Chrome: seek <code>video.currentTime<\/code>, zoom the player area).<\/li><li>Gesture: line of action, pose, weight support. Contrapposto only subtle; for a male worker character use a neutral upright stance (safer for the rig).<\/li><li>Human proportion grid (\"our proportions\", 7.5-head canon; the numbers = <code>loft.CANON<\/code>, the same as <code>gait_engine.canon()<\/code> in the motion-graphics skill): chin 1 H, nipples 2 H, navel 3 H, crotch 3.75 H (1/2 of the height), knee 5.625 H (1/4 of the height from the floor), elbow at navel height (3 H), wrist at crotch height (3.75 H), fingertips 4.7 H (mid-thigh), ankle 7.15 H, sole 7.5 H; shoulder width 2 H outside the deltoids (+-1.0 H from the centre line), hips 1.5 H, waist 1.1 H, neck 0.5 H, foot 1.1 H. Comparison style Audrey/Cosmin: small head, long thin neck, wide square shoulders, long legs.<ul><li>Character without a reference: first write the pose skeleton as measured points in H (hip, knee, ankle, shoulder, elbow, wrist, head). Thigh and shin each about 1.75 H, upper arm about 1.35-1.45 H, forearm about 1.15-1.2 H (CANON from the shoulder top: upper arm 1.5 H, forearm + hand 2.0 H); check the lengths with numbers before drawing.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>GRAYSCALE SILHOUETTE first (Jess). Three-panel review sheet: (1) gesture + grid + bones, (2) one silhouette path with anchors/handles + cut lines, (3) grayscale per region. Plus a close-up sheet of face + hands. Send to the user BEFORE colour.<ul><li>First ask the user the form of the first delivery: grayscale silhouette or straight to full flat after passing the self-check (the test project chose full flat directly).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Cut lines + colour (5-6 colour palette) → colour sheet (full body + close-up of face/hands).<\/li><li>Shadows + little, purposeful detail.<ul><li>Self-check the risky spots (chapter 11) in every delivery.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Rig + sweep test of every joint (chapters 5, 8). Shoulder/joint check sheet for all poses.<\/li><li>Side / 3/4 view = NEW DRAWING (not a rotation/warp of the front view); if there is a reference, trace (chapter 12). Review the silhouette again. If the reference does not contain that view (side, back): chapter 15.<\/li><li>Character kit style (character constructor kit): the order differs from steps 4-8 (the flow proven in the test project): (1) ALL pieces first, following the kit sheet layout (head per angle + expressions, front/side/back/3/4 rig, hand-gesture set, mouth set); (2) set proportions in PROPORTION MODE (same geometry, gray per material, paint layer off) + a proportion check block (temporary assembly of all rigs with the same landmark lines); (3) only then apply the skill style (palette, lines, contact) as the paint layer; (4) only then assemble the turnaround and poses. Detail of stage (1): build a PIECE KIT per view (front, then side and 3/4) from body parameters (chapter 5.1): head, body + neck + collar, pelvis, upper arm, forearm + cuff, hand, thigh, shin + hem, ankle, shoe, back hair. Send an explode sheet (pivot points) + assembly. Turnaround, poses, gestures = ASSEMBLIES of those pieces, not separate drawings. (Review Results, kit test project: a front view drawn as one painting was rejected as a workflow: arms stuck to the body, not riggable.)<\/li><li>Motion (motion-graphics skill). For orbit/many angles: offer the method options (new drawing per angle, full 3D rig, hybrid) in a table, then test the concept at 3 new angles before production (chapter 16).<\/li><\/ol>"},{"id":"ve3","title":"One silhouette + cut lines method (core)","html":"<p>Cosmin (jlxdo9iUyjA 3:22): \"create one big shape and then mask it whatever we need\".<\/p>\n<ul><li>Geometry in H units (head height; skull top y 0, chin y 1, sole about 7.5), x = 0 centre line, y downward. <code>Space(ox, oy, H)<\/code> maps H to pixels, so one drawing can render at any scale.<\/li><li>Draw ONE closed path for the whole figure (head to sole), clockwise: smooth anchors <code>s(x, y)<\/code> (automatic Catmull-Rom handles) + corner anchors <code>c(x, y, r)<\/code> with live radius. Few anchors (about 110-120 for a front body, 70 for a profile). Flowing curves: shoulder to arm to wrist to hand; thigh to shin to ankle to shoe.<\/li><li>Hands are built with Shape Builder and then United into the silhouette (still one silhouette; the body path only has a \"wrist stub\" hidden in the palm).<\/li><li>Colour parts = pieces of the silhouette: silhouette intersect a knife shape (hair/hat, face, neck, inner shirt, collar, outer garment, belt, trousers, shoes, sole, hands). Knife = a large polygon (BIG = 3-8 H) with one side being the cut line. Because they come from the same shape, edges meet exactly.<\/li><li>Anti-alias seams are closed with a thin stroke (0.9 px) of the same colour on every region.<\/li><li>Shadows are also pieces of the silhouette (side away from the light, under the chin, under the cuff, under the trouser hem on the shoe).<\/li><li>Hidden roots: the forearm comes out from INSIDE a wider cuff; the trouser hem covers the shoe collar with a cast shadow.<\/li><li>Side view:<ul><li>body = one path (head, face profile, neck, chest, belly, near leg, shoe, calf, buttocks, back, nape);<\/li><li>far leg + far arm = a layer behind (about 20% darker), near arm in front, because the limbs swing past the body when walking;<\/li><li>the leg profile piece is shared by the near and far leg, so both are identical.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Side view, arm: the sleeve root = a circle (r about 0.25 H) at the shoulder pivot, staying round at every swing angle. Sleeveless outer garment (vest): armhole = an ellipse the size of the sleeve, filled with the inner shirt; the shoulder strap only covers the shoulder top.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve4","title":"Body parts (general numbers, chin = 1.0 H)","html":"<ul><li>Head/face (Neda EWE8HXT0330):<ul><li>Face box in 3 parts (hairline-brows, brows-nose bottom, nose-chin).<\/li><li>Inverted egg shape: temples +-0.338, jaw angle +-0.32 at y 0.72, chin +-0.10 at y 1.0.<\/li><li>Brows 0.40-0.45 (thick at the root, tapering at the tail), eye line 0.51, eye width 0.15 = distance between the eyes.<\/li><li>Nose base 0.69 (width = distance between the eyes), mouth 0.79 (1/3 of the nose-chin distance), mouth corners below the inner iris.<\/li><li>Ears from the brows to the nose base.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Natural, friendly eyes (animation style):<ul><li>Almond open enough (height about 0.06 H); upper lid = a tapering crescent covering the top of the iris; lid crease; thin lower lid.<\/li><li>Iris r 0.030 (3 layers: iris, pupil, highlight) + lid shadow.<\/li><li>Nose: shadow under the tip, nostril wings, small nostrils, side shadow.<\/li><li>Mouth: tapering line + soft corner dips (closed smile), shadow under the lower lip. Thin radial-gradient blush.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Minimal flat-style face:<ul><li>Small dark oval eyes (rx about 0.035 H, ry 0.05 H) + one highlight dot; brows = short tapering strokes.<\/li><li>Nose = a small shadow in dark skin colour. Open smile: D shape, white upper teeth, lighter tongue/lower lip. Soft blush.<\/li><li>3/4 view: far eye narrower; nose goes into the face contour. Side view: mouth a closed line.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Face profile: forehead, brow ridge, eye socket, nose, lips, chin in one body path; profile eye = a small almond + oval iris.<\/li><li>Hat/helmet: the occiput (back of the head) goes into the shell, back edge lower than the front; sideburns below the edge; short hair wraps around the ear.<\/li><li>Neck + shoulders one skin outline (Neda), jaw shadow on the neck.<\/li><li>\"Racket ball\" joints (Audrey/Jess): the ends of the parts meeting at a joint are rounded; the lower part tucks under the upper part. No detail in the joint area. For shoulders/armpits see chapter 8.<\/li><li>Hands (Shape Builder):<ul><li>Palm + 4 tapering finger capsules (relative length middle 1.00, ring 0.94, index 0.92, little 0.75; knuckles form an arc; fingers touching and slightly converging) + thumb with a large thenar root.<\/li><li>Hand length 0.64-0.76 H (measure from the reference). Finger separation lines from the tip to the knuckle; the thumb edge only visible where it overlaps the palm.<\/li><li>Hanging arm: back of the hand facing slightly forward.<\/li><li>Swap set: relaxed, open (waving, mirrored; fingers spread 14-16 degrees, thumb away about 1.9x the spread angle, no finger separation lines), fist (fingers folded, thumb across), on the belt, holding an object.<\/li><li>Relaxed hand in side view (flat style): one silhouette of the back of the hand with closed fingers whose tips converge + a tube thumb in front; finger gaps = soft lines.<\/li><li>Hand holding an object = ONE hand shape (chapter 18.4): palm behind the object joined to the wrist, one jagged fingertip blob at the front edge of the object with a tone wedge in each notch, thumb one thick lobe. No separate finger capsules.<\/li><li>Tapered capsule = union of two circles + an outer tangent quad.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Shoes:<ul><li>Feet turned slightly outward (front view).<\/li><li>Sneaker profile (local coordinates heel → toe, height scaled by foot length): round heel counter, collar dipping down, tongue, vamp sloping to the toe, round toe box, white cup sole with toe spring, laces = short cross bars, thin stitching.<\/li><li>Work shoe/boot: thick sole, domed toe cap, tongue + laces, heel; boot profile about 1.15 H.<\/li><li>Trouser hem curves down at the front.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Leg raised in front view: thigh looks short (foreshortening), shoe facing the camera.<\/li><li>Body profile: chest about 1.0 H deep, thigh 0.7 H; ear, shoulder, hip, knee, ankle on one vertical line.<\/li><li>Wavy hair: not a repeating sine offset (reads as stiff curls/a wig). Use CURTAINS (<code>shapes.curtain2<\/code>): two edges with 1-2 large S curves in the same direction on both edges, starting at the jaw, varied strand ends; volume at the jaw/shoulders. Crown shape and hairline from measuring the reference (chapter 18.3). Side view: round cranium and the hair as one mass.<\/li><li>Fringe (scarf, etc.) = separate strands (background visible in between).<\/li><li>Ears in 3/4 and side views in front of the hair.<\/li><li>Clothing:<ul><li>Outer layer over the inner layer (straps/shoulders of the outer garment cover the sleeve root of the inner shirt).<\/li><li>A few folds at knees/elbows; cuffs, buckles, zips, buttons, pockets.<\/li><li>Objects at the side of the body are only visible from the correct side for the view.<\/li><li>Patterns clipped to that garment; inner detail uses thin lines, not outlines.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Wide-leg trousers: the fabric does not bend sharply at the knee (thigh-calf width blend); front-view leg = a straight line from hip to ankle.<\/li><li>Female body (Review Results, kit test project: \"the female body shape is a bit different and worth varying\"): write the body as half-width parameters (shoulders, chest, waist, hips, thighs, knees, ankles, arms, neck) + clothing ease, then offer 2-3 types on a comparison sheet (e.g. slim, hourglass, curvy). Front-view traits: shoulders narrower and more sloped than a man's; the chest-waist-hip curve still reads even in loose clothes (hem band hugs the hips); hips wider than the shoulders except the slim type; upper thighs full on the outside; knees closer together than the hips; elbows at the side of the waist and forearms angled outward (carrying angle) so the waist gap is visible; smaller hands and feet.<\/li><li>Kit-style clothing detail catalogue (research on 5 character constructor kit sheets, test project research): neck rib 2 parallel lines; short armhole seam running 0.3-0.5 H down from the armpit; cuff/hem rib 1 line; elbow fold 1-2 strokes that appear smoothly with the elbow angle; pocket = an open line; rolled sleeve = a 2-3 line band covering the elbow; rolled trouser cuff = a lighter band. Lines: material colour dL* -5..-8, thickness 0.015-0.025 H, tapered and open ends (not closed). Fabric 1 base tone; shadows only for contact (band under the chin about 0.07 H, inside the collar, inner hair layer, sole).<\/li><li>Kit-style gesture set = WHOLE ARMS (upper + forearm already bent + hand + object), about 35-40 assets per character: thumbs up, pointing, stop, raised fist, OK, crossed arms, phone, tablet, cup, paper, book, card, bag, laptop with two hands. The top end of the arm is round and goes under the body's shoulder.<\/li><li>Kit-style face + mouths: 12 visemes (A E I, O, C D N S T X Y Z, R, L, B M P, F V, Ee, Th, Ch J Sh, U, Q W) + a head without a mouth (front and 3/4). Expression through brows (angle, height), lids (^ arc laughing, half closed) and mouth (D with teeth, O, clenched teeth, frown); eyes stay dots. Mouth size 0.15 H closed up to 0.26 H open; upright oval eyes 0.03 x 0.07 H, centre distance 0.27 H; nose = a C-shaped dark skin line about 0.16 H.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve5","title":"Shading","html":"<ul><li>Light from the top left. Soft linear gradient per garment (Fd4IOI): light on the light side, dark on the far side (stops 0, 0.42, 1).<\/li><li>Form shadow = region minus a copy shifted toward the light, blurred, clipped to the region. Thin rim light on the light side.<\/li><li>Accent-colour rim light (Myth style): a copy of the silhouette in the accent colour BEHIND the silhouette, shifted (+3, -2) px (estimate), so only a thin edge shows.<\/li><li>Parts of one garment (chest V, shoulder, upper arm, forearm) use gradient bounds and shadows from the UNION of their group, then clipped per part → no seams.<\/li><li>Cast shadow = (caster shifted away from the light) intersect receiver, blur, clip. Examples: hat brim onto the forehead, chin onto the neck, collar onto the body, cuff onto the hand, belt onto the trousers, shirt hem onto the trousers, trouser hem onto the shoe, arm onto the body.<\/li><li>Ground shadow = a radial-gradient oval under the feet, following the foot positions. In a 3D scene with a sun: shadows from the 3D pose (motion-graphics chapter 5.3 (<code>motion-graphics/references/backgrounds.md<\/code>)).<\/li><li>Grain 3-3.5% (Ben Marriott 3-8%); simple flat style 0.6-1.2%. For video use a fixed grain seed. Texture clipped per shape; dry-brush edges = 1-2 px path point jitter.<\/li><li>Orbit with a world sun: the character's light direction per angle is computed from the sun (test project decision); screen form_shade uses the projected light vector, not a fixed top left.<ul><li>Offer the user two styles before doing it: (a) kit style, no form shadows, only contact shadows that do not depend on the light direction (under the chin, inside the collar, under the hair, sole); safest for orbits. (b) Isometric-style far-side plane: one dark plane dL* -9..-13 (about 30% of the area), its boundary from the loft normal against the sun, continuous with the angle.<\/li><li>Tested method (rig v4 test project): world sun → body frame (X forward = (sin facing, cos facing), L right = (cos facing, -sin facing)); per angle v: hx = sX sin v - sL cos v (screen-right component), hz = sX cos v + sL sin v (toward the camera), normalised from the horizontal light. Shadow band = region minus a copy shifted k x hx (k the same as the old form_shade, e.g. 0.05-0.075 H). When hz &lt; 0 (sun behind the character): the whole shape is shaded 0.28 x (-hz) except a 0.03 H edge on the light side (rim). This shade is required on ALL parts (skin, hair, shoes, bag), otherwise a light face sits on a dark body. Character sheets without a sun keep the screen top left so they can be regressed against the reference drawing.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve6","title":"Rig (so it stays one shape while moving)","html":"<ul><li>The FINISHED artwork is cut once in the rest pose (high resolution, 200 px/H), every outline is sampled at 0.008 H into a polygon (puppet mesh, Adobe Puppet Warp M01), points are LBS-skinned to bones: head, neck, chest, pelvis, upper/fore/hand L-R, thigh/shin/foot (+ toe for foot roll) L-R. Rotation anchors at the centre of the joint circles.<\/li><li>Every layer has a weight <code>hint<\/code>: 'auto' (computed from position), 'torso' (never to the arm), a bone name (rigid), 'arm+-1' (arm chain only).<\/li><li>Weights must be CONTINUOUS across shared edges (e.g. the body | sleeve edge), otherwise the edge tears. Weight transitions use smoothstep along the bone.<\/li><li>Body layers (outer garment, collar, belt) are never weighted to the arm. Sleeve = arm chain only, cut diagonally below the shoulder, rotating rigidly; shoulder + armpit from chapter 8.<\/li><li>Shoulder pivot at the centre of the deltoid circle; shoulder elevation when the arm goes up = a small offset of the upper arm bone (about (0.02, -0.08) H).<\/li><li>Straight bones, rounded joints. 2-bone IK for the legs (front: knee outward; side: knee forward) and arms (elbow backward). Foot/toe angles are given in world angles, so the stance foot stays flat.<\/li><li>Hands = a swap set, rigid on the hand bone; layers cached per hand set combination.<\/li><li>Shadows and cast shadows are RECOMPUTED on the posed shape. Pathfinder via <code>brush.union<\/code>/<code>brush.subtract<\/code>: on the skia engine with try/except + <code>brush.simplify<\/code> (skinning can make self-intersecting polygons); the pure engine computes booleans exactly per sample at draw time, so no fallback.<\/li><li>The style can change without changing the rig: the paint layer (colour, outline, detail, face) is a separate module that replaces the painter functions; geometry and weights stay.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>5.1 Kit-style 2D piece rig (character constructor kit research)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Pivot at the centre of the half circle at the piece end (inset = half the width). Two round ends with equal radius at one pivot → joint overlap = 1 diameter, outer silhouette whole at every angle.<\/li><li>Pieces: head; body + collar + neck (skin capsule, round top end rising into the head, head pivot at its centre); pelvis (straight top edge at the belt, bottom edge two curves centred on the hip pivots); thigh capsule; shin (round top, flat bottom = hem + cuff band); ankle/sock its own small capsule; shoe its own piece; upper arm capsule; forearm (round top, bottom = cuff); separate hand with a wrist stub; rolled sleeve band = its own piece covering the elbow.<\/li><li>Side rig: upper thigh widens backward (buttocks) about 1.27x; each character has 3 rigs (side, 3/4, front) at the same turnaround scale.<\/li><li>Assembly check: hip pivot = crotch +-0.07 H; the reassembled pieces must cover the turnaround silhouette without gaps.<\/li><li>Piece shape = TANGENT capsule (<code>scripts/shapes.py<\/code> <code>cap<\/code>): circles of equal radius at the pivot on both meeting pieces, sides = outer tangent lines, thigh/calf/arm bulge zero at the ends; hem/cuff = <code>cut<\/code> perpendicular to the axis + a cuff band (<code>band<\/code>). Pieces drawn directly at final size (a screen warp makes the joint ends stick out of the trousers).<\/li><li>Explode sheet: spacing between pieces = 1 joint circle diameter + a 0.05-0.06 H gap, white pivot points. Add a joint test block (knee 22-30, hip 14-22, shoulder 24-28, elbow 38-48 degrees) and a proportion check block (assembly of all views + landmark lines).<\/li><li>Relaxed turnaround pose: shoulders -1.5..+1, elbow 6-7 degrees, head tilted 2 degrees, one leg slightly open; head rotation at the neck pivot. An arm holding an object is drawn last (in front of the other arm and the hair) + a contact line along the sleeve edge over a body of the same colour (<code>edge_on<\/code>).<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve7","title":"Code tools","html":"<ul><li>Python 3.10+ with numpy + Pillow only: <code>pip install -r scripts/requirements.txt<\/code> (Linux workspace: add <code>--break-system-packages<\/code> if needed). Draw through <code>brush<\/code> (chapter 17): skia engine automatically when <code>skia-python<\/code> is installed (<code>pip install skia-python<\/code>, optional, faster), otherwise the pure numpy engine; force it with <code>MGP_BRUSH_ENGINE=skia<\/code> or <code>MGP_BRUSH_ENGINE=pure<\/code>, pure-engine supersampling <code>MGP_BRUSH_SUPERSAMPLE<\/code> (default 4; 6-8 is smoother). Images through <code>image_ops<\/code>; OpenCV is not needed. Fonts: Poppins (titles), Inter (Aptos is not in the workspace); the pure engine renders text with Pillow.<\/li><li>Windows (Claude Code on Windows): <code>python -m pip install -r scripts/requirements.txt<\/code>; command <code>python -I<\/code>, not <code>python3<\/code>; ffmpeg via <code>winget install Gyan.FFmpeg<\/code>; engine variable in PowerShell: <code>$env:MGP_BRUSH_ENGINE=\"pure\"<\/code>. Per-user fonts (HKCU registry + <code>AddFontResourceW<\/code> + WM_FONTCHANGE broadcast) are only seen by skia after the font cache is refreshed; on the skia engine check with <code>skia.Typeface(name).getFamilyName()<\/code> (silent fallback to Segoe UI). The pure engine (<code>brush.Font<\/code>) looks up the font file by name in the system and per-user font folders, then silently falls back to Arial/Segoe UI; check with a test text sheet.<\/li><li>Regression \"pixel difference 0\" only holds on the same engine: Linux vs Windows differ in label text (font rasterisation) and max 2/255 in scenes. Changing engine = make a new reference once, after checking that this is the only difference. Code that picks from tied float values (same width, same angle) can differ between engines: use comparisons with an eps.<\/li><li>Pathfinder (skia engine): <code>subtract(a, intersect(b, a))<\/code> leaves hair-thin slivers on coinciding edges. Subtract with a shape that juts outward (the original polygon), not with the intersect result.<\/li><li>Proportion mode without touching the drawing code: wrap <code>Fig.fill/shade/ink<\/code> (base colour → gray per material via a table, lines and contact shadows skipped), so the proportion sheet and the style sheet always come from the same geometry.<\/li><li>Contour morph: render the path to a mask (2000 px/H, <code>Surface.array()<\/code>), <code>image_ops.outer_contours<\/code> (equivalent of findContours RETR_EXTERNAL), force clockwise (signed area), start at the top-centre point, resample N points (e.g. 72) by arc length, then lerp between keys. Mirror = reverse the index order (<code>o[-i]<\/code>) then negate x, so it stays clockwise from the same start point.<\/li><li>Anchors S (smooth) and C (corner + live radius); pathfinder via <code>brush.union<\/code> / <code>brush.subtract<\/code> / <code>brush.intersect<\/code> (<code>vkit.union<\/code> / <code>diff<\/code> / <code>inter<\/code>); mirror for symmetric shapes; tapered capsules; <code>brush.measure(path)<\/code> (contours with <code>length<\/code> and <code>pos_tan(d)<\/code>) to sample an outline; stroke → path (vector morphological closing): on the skia engine <code>Paint.getFillPath<\/code> through the skia object (<code>brush.Paint(...).to_skia()<\/code>), on the pure engine the thick stroke is drawn to a mask, then <code>image_ops.outer_contours<\/code>.<\/li><li>Limb tube: Catmull centre line + SEPARATE left and right half-width profiles (calf/biceps fuller on one side), ends rounded or cut.<\/li><li>Removing outlines without changing the painter code: a canvas proxy that drops strokes in the outline colour.<\/li><li>Pixels are read with <code>Surface.array()<\/code> (RGBA uint8 on both engines; <code>vkit.to_array<\/code>). Old code that uses skia directly still MUST use <code>toarray(colorType=skia.kRGBA_8888_ColorType)<\/code> (default BGRA → swapped colours).<\/li><li>Project modules take a <code>brush.Canvas<\/code> (<code>cv<\/code>) and build shapes with <code>brush.Path<\/code> (via <code>Fig.pen<\/code> / <code>poly<\/code> / <code>ellipse<\/code>), not <code>skia.Path<\/code>. On the skia engine the canvas still forwards the original skia methods (<code>cv.drawPath<\/code>) for old code, but new code uses the brush API so it runs on both engines.<\/li><li>For video/HTML compositions the character kit and character styles also exist as JS modules in the motion-graphics skill: <code>lib/vector-kit.js<\/code> (<code>MGP.vectorKit<\/code>: views and turnaround, 8 expressions, 12 lip-sync mouths, 8 gestures, proportion mode, flat/cut/gray styles) and <code>lib/character-styles.js<\/code> (<code>MGP.characterStyles<\/code>: vector, pixel art, origami from the same gait engine), rendered with mgp (motion-graphics chapter 13 (<code>motion-graphics/references/lib.md<\/code>)). The chapter 00 rules and chapter 11 checks still apply to the result.<\/li><li>Render review PNGs; video via ffmpeg H.264 CRF 18 preset veryfast, 24 fps.<\/li><li>Helper scripts kept per project: review sheets (silhouette, colour, detail), regression sheet, gapcheck (holes + loose pieces), arm width measurement (armw), zoom crop per part.<\/li><li>General tools (drawing kit, multi-angle loft, gapcheck, zoom sheet, SVG measurement) are in this skill's <code>scripts/<\/code> (chapter 17).<\/li><li>Measure shapes from a finished drawing: render one path to a mask (200 px/H), take xmin/xmax per row (<code>extents<\/code>) for the loft (chapter 16).<\/li><li>A Fig with a dummy canvas (8 x 8 surface) is used to get paths from a painter function without drawing into the delivery.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve8","title":"Review with the user","html":"<ul><li>Send the grayscale silhouette first + face/hand close-ups, then one large colour pose, only then other poses.<\/li><li>Before sending, check the WHOLE figure part by part with zoom (render 1.5-6x): head/hat, face, neck, left-right shoulders, armpits, elbows, hands, waist, hips, crotch, knees, shoes. Do not only fix the part that was complained about (\"are you even looking at the whole thing?\").<\/li><li>Compare side by side with the style reference at the same scale before sending.<\/li><li>For a rig: render a sweep video per joint (e.g. arm 0-120-0 degrees) + a strip every 10-20 degrees, not only key poses. For a multi-angle rig: angle sheet (0, 22.5, 45, 67.5, 90, 135 + references 45 and 90 side by side), walk strip per angle, 360-degree turn video while walking.<\/li><li>Measure before fixing: find the cause with numbers (position, distance, jump per frame), write the cause down, only then change.<\/li><li>User comments are short; translate them into shapes:<ul><li>\"picak\" = concave/flat;<\/li><li>\"banci\" = feminine gesture;<\/li><li>\"asal-asalan\" (sloppy) = wrong construction, not lack of detail;<\/li><li>\"tidak simple\" (not simple) = too much detail/texture/lines, unclean contour;<\/li><li>\"kurusan\" / \"terlalu kurus\" (too thin) = body or limb width smaller than in other views or than the reference (measure first, compare across views);<\/li><li>\"lengannya turun\" / \"nggak seimbang\" (arms dropped / unbalanced) = arm root below the shoulder line or shoulders too sloped;<\/li><li>\"meluncur\" (sliding) = still drawing shifted, feet not planted (needs a rig at that angle);<\/li><li>\"bayangannya tidak berubah\" (the shadow does not change) = shadow does not follow the pose (needs shadows from the 3D pose).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>If the user asks for autonomous iteration: repeat render → zoom → fix until it passes on its own, then send.<\/li><li>Write the weaknesses that are still visible honestly in every delivery. Ask in chat + interactive options.<\/li><li>Write progress in PROGRESS.md; handover in HANDOVER.md + code archive.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve9","title":"Corrective joints: areas that are \"filled\" while moving","html":"<p>Review Results: the joint areas that fill while moving (shoulder, armpit, elbow, hip, crotch, knee) must have an actual anatomical shape and stay PROPORTIONAL at every angle. Nudging points/pulling the mesh is not enough.<\/p>\n<ul><li>Shoulder + armpit are REDRAWN every pose from the posed geometry (pose-space corrective), drawn between the sleeve and body layers:<ul><li>Top: trapezius from the neck (about 0.50, 1.25 H; same slope as the silhouette, rising slightly with abduction), then one cubic curve to the top edge of the upper arm, tangent to both (handles 0.42 x distance) → the deltoid is one round arc. Above horizontal (more than 60 degrees) the shoulder tangent is aimed along the arm.<\/li><li>Armpit: the underside of the upper arm is intersected with the chest side line; the corner is filleted. When the arm is down the corner is hidden. A hollow shadow + 2 fabric folds appear smoothly with the angle (starting at 35 degrees).<\/li><li>The upper sleeve is cut diagonally below the shoulder and rotates rigidly; its top is replaced by this shoulder curve.<\/li><li>Loose clothing: shoulder = cubic from the trapezius to the outer edge of the arm + an armpit wedge from the tube root to the body side, without a line across the armpit.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>MUST be continuous: no if/else that turns shapes on/off. The fillet is always computed; radius (0 → 0.075 H, smoothstep 5-45 degrees), hollow depth, shadow and folds rise smoothly from 0 with the angle. (The version with an angle toggle was rejected: \"the armpit first stays attached, then suddenly snaps off\".)<\/li><li>Proportions kept at every angle: upper arm width fixed (measured from the reference), deltoid mass does not shrink/grow, trapezius length to the shoulder tip fixed. Measure the width perpendicular to the arm axis at 0, 30, 45, 60, 76, 108 degrees.<\/li><li>Raised arm: upper arm = an even tube; elbow = 2 rigid pieces + a joint circle; straight forearm.<\/li><li>The same principle for the elbow (round outer side, fold on the inner side), knee, hip/crotch during a kick.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve10","title":"Contact with the environment","html":"<ul><li>Every contact (feet on the ground, buttocks on a chair/crate, hand on the knee/an object) is COMPUTED as a constraint, not guessed. Sitting example: trouser points in the buttock/back-of-thigh area are skinned, the root is raised/lowered iteratively (12 times, factor 0.8) until the lowest point sits exactly on the surface; when approaching/standing up it is only pushed out (must not penetrate). Check: maximum penetration 0.000 H in all frames.<\/li><li>Object size follows the body: chair/crate a little below knee height (surface about 1.7 H from the floor) so the thigh is almost level and the shin upright; the object is placed BEHIND the standing position (the person steps back, then sits), not under the body.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve11","title":"Alternative styles from taste references (use when the brief asks)","html":"<ul><li>Maximalist flat (Glam, Dan Stein): 8-9 colours are fine as long as all are flat with a uniform dark outline of 1.5-2 px at 1080p, strokes not scaled.<\/li><li>3-colour sequence (Myth): light background, dark silhouette, warm accent only for the rim + details; background as line art only, character fully coloured.<\/li><li>Spot illustration: character in front of a pale organic blob.<\/li><li>Noodle limbs (short, boneless): curved centre line + thick round-cap stroke. 3-shape shading (light, base, dark) for a cartoon style (Ben Marriott).<\/li><li>Collage style: torn edges, jagged starbursts, a different font per word.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>10.1 Simple flat vector (Review Results: \"simple, elegant, sells\")<\/h4>\n<ul><li>No outline. Flat colours + ONE crisp low-contrast shadow on the side away from the light (region minus a copy shifted toward the light, no blur).<\/li><li>Clean contours: few anchors, long curves; traced contours smoothed strongly (hair more than the body) until there are no small waves.<\/li><li>Little but purposeful detail:<ul><li>seams/folds = thin LIGHT lines (the line polarity is chosen per project and written in the spec: the character kit and the test project use DARK lines dL* -5..-8, about 7% of the area);<\/li><li>cuff/hem rib bands slightly darker;<\/li><li>buttons, pockets, shoelaces;<\/li><li>hair 1 shine line.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Drop: knit texture, dense ribs, many hair strands, dense fringe (use fewer, wider strands), heavy grain.<\/li><li>Limited palette (3-5 colours + 1 accent); main garment colour contrasting with the background (e.g. cool on a warm background).<\/li><li>Lively pose: 3/4 walking (front heel planted, back heel lifted), upper body leaning 3-4 degrees, arms swinging opposite, one small prop that tells a story.<\/li><li>Slim cropped trousers + visible socks make a light silhouette; long wide trousers make a heavy silhouette (choose per the character spec).<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve12","title":"Self-check of risky spots (REQUIRED before every delivery)","html":"<p>Review Results: the user does not want to repeat the same fix request. Every render is checked and fixed by yourself first; send only when every item passes, or state honestly which items have not passed.<\/p>\n<p>Flow: render → numeric check script → zoom crop 3-6x per part → fix → repeat until it passes → send with the check results table.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>No<\/th><th>Risky spot<\/th><th>How to check<\/th><th>Pass criterion<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>1<\/td><td>Proportions across views<\/td><td>Horizontal guide lines on the sheet: head top, eyes, chin, shoulders, elbows, shirt hem, wrists, knees, sole<\/td><td>Landmarks at the same height in all views (max 0.02 H difference) and the same as the reference<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2<\/td><td>Hand + arm length<\/td><td>Measure fingertips and wrist in H<\/td><td>Fingertips about mid-thigh / per reference; hand 0.64-0.76 H; do not shrink the hand<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3<\/td><td>Arm width vs body<\/td><td>Ratio upper arm width : body width, compared with the reference<\/td><td>Max 10% from the reference; no arm-body gap that makes the arm look small<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>4<\/td><td>Fingers<\/td><td>Zoom 6x<\/td><td>4 fingers + thumb read clearly, relative length 1.00/0.94/0.92/0.75, no merged/cut fingers<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>5<\/td><td>Hand direction<\/td><td>Compare left and right hands<\/td><td>Mirror each other; thumb and palm direction per reference/pose<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>6<\/td><td>Shoulders + armpits<\/td><td>Zoom + joint sweep (chapters 7, 8)<\/td><td>Shoulders round, not flat/lumpy; armpit without webbing; changes continuously<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>7<\/td><td>Head<\/td><td>Zoom the head in each view<\/td><td>Round cranium (not flat in profile); hair covers the skull and wraps around the ear; hat wraps the occiput<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>8<\/td><td>Face<\/td><td>Zoom 4x<\/td><td>Eyes on the 0.51 H line and symmetric (front); eye spacing = eye width; mouth centred between the eyes (front); face profile edge without dents other than the designed nose-lips-chin<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>9<\/td><td>Garment joins<\/td><td>Silhouette minus the union of all regions<\/td><td>Remaining area 0 (no background showing through); shirt hem overlaps the trousers; shirt and trousers join<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>10<\/td><td>Specks + small holes<\/td><td>Count connected components per region and the holes inside<\/td><td>0 components and 0 holes smaller than the threshold (initial estimate 0.003 H², adjust) except intended details (buttons, etc.)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>11<\/td><td>Wrong-class colour<\/td><td>Look for blobs of another colour inside the hair/shirt mass<\/td><td>0 blobs (e.g. a light hair strand read as trousers, a floor shadow read as body)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>12<\/td><td>Shoes<\/td><td>Zoom 6x<\/td><td>Clearly visible below the trouser hem; sole, toe, laces/tongue per shoe type; sole without notches<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>13<\/td><td>Accessories (scarf, bag, hat)<\/td><td>Measure thickness and position in H vs reference<\/td><td>Max 10% difference; wrap/parting/end direction per reference<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>14<\/td><td>Outline (if the style uses outlines)<\/td><td>Zoom 4x on edges<\/td><td>Uniform thickness, not jagged, not broken, not doubled<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>15<\/td><td>3/4 perspective<\/td><td>Compare with the reference<\/td><td>Far side of face/body foreshortened; far eye narrower; body centre line shifted; far leg slightly smaller<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>16<\/td><td>Arm width while moving<\/td><td>Width perpendicular to the axis at 5 points, several angles<\/td><td>Upper arm even (max 0.02 H difference), not narrowing toward the armpit<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>17<\/td><td>Holes + loose pieces in EVERY animation frame<\/td><td>gapcheck<\/td><td>0 holes &gt; 6 px (except reference design), 0 loose pieces<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>18<\/td><td>Simplicity (flat style)<\/td><td>View at small scale + zoom<\/td><td>No purposeless texture/lines; contour without small waves<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>19<\/td><td>Width across views<\/td><td>Measure chest, waist, hip, thigh width in front and 3/4 views<\/td><td>3/4 not narrower than the front<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>20<\/td><td>Shoulders and arm roots (3/4)<\/td><td>Zoom near and far shoulder<\/td><td>Both arms hang from the shoulder top; shoulder curve continues from the panel into the arm<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>21<\/td><td>Light direction across views<\/td><td>Compare the shadow side in all views<\/td><td>Shadow always on the side away from the same light (default top left), also on mirrored views; in scenes with a world sun: per the sun at each angle<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>22<\/td><td>All-views sheet<\/td><td>Turn-order sheet (front, 3/4, side, back, mirrored side, mirrored 3/4) + landmark lines; measure top and sole of each view with a mask<\/td><td>Top and sole equal (max 0.006 H difference)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>23<\/td><td>Union joins<\/td><td>Look for pixels deviating from the garment colour in the garment area<\/td><td>0 background lines showing through<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>24<\/td><td>Regression of accepted views<\/td><td>Re-render the old sheet and compare pixels (<code>tools/regresi.py &lt;ref&gt; &lt;new&gt; diff.png<\/code>)<\/td><td>Pixel difference 0 on the same engine (reference per engine, chapter 6)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>25<\/td><td>Legitimate cavities<\/td><td>List the remaining cavities with their reasons<\/td><td>Only the gap between the legs, the bag strap triangle, the arm-body gap, the bag-leg gap<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>26<\/td><td>Multi-angle rig<\/td><td>Angle sheet + references, 360-degree turn, gapcheck per angle<\/td><td>At the reference angles (0, 90) the silhouette equals the reference drawing; no bald spots, hair covering the face, or features on the unseen side<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>27<\/td><td>Kit style 5-view sheet<\/td><td>Side, 3/4, front, back 3/4, back + lines for top, shoulder, crotch, knee, ankle, sole; automatic mask per view<\/td><td>Top/sole/shoulder max 0.035 H difference; 3/4 width including arms may be 5-7% narrower than the front (item 19 is measured without arms)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>28<\/td><td>Rig in the scene (rule 00.7)<\/td><td>Scene frame at the final camera distance, angles 0/90/180/270 + the hardest, side by side with the accepted version, actual size and 2x zoom<\/td><td>No part worse than the accepted version: limb/body proportions equal to the reference drawing at that angle (measure crotch height and body width in H in the frame)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>29<\/td><td>Foot slip in a perspective scene<\/td><td><code>tools/selip.py<\/code> per beat: screen position difference of the stance foot against the projection of its world point during one stance phase<\/td><td>Max 1 px, AND item 28 passes (a correction that removes slip must not change proportions)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>30<\/td><td>Hair vs style reference<\/td><td><code>measure_ref.py<\/code>: top width, hairline per x, cheek/jaw width; overlay the reference outline at the same H scale; side-by-side comparison of 4 views<\/td><td>Top, hairline and jaw volume within +-0.03 H of the reference; 3/4 has far-side hair; back symmetric without diagonal edges<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>31<\/td><td>Grips<\/td><td>Zoom 3-4x on every hand holding an object<\/td><td>One hand shape (not separate finger capsules), wedges in the finger notches, thick thumb; object upright in the world and facing per the pose<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>32<\/td><td>Hand size<\/td><td>Skin mask of the relaxed front-view hand, width x height in H<\/td><td>Equal to the reference (kit: 0.29 x 0.58 H)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Note: items 1-5, 9-11, 16-17 can be computed automatically (numpy on region masks). The other items must be seen through zoom crops, not from the full sheet.<\/p>\n<h4>11.2 Regression sheet (rule 00.3)<\/h4>\n<p>One PNG holding: full body in several frames of each clip; zoom of every spot ever complained about (e.g. waving hand 4 frames, armpit 2 angles, hair while waving, face front/3/4/side, raised forearm). Compare with the previous delivery sheet; parts that were not changed must be identical.<\/p>"},{"id":"ve13","title":"Tracing from a reference image (turnaround / character sheet)","html":"<p>Review Results: when the user provides a reference image, the 3/4, side and back views are TRACED from the reference (direction agreed), not redrawn by hand or warped. The front view is also traced so all views share one style.<\/p>\n<ul><li>Tools: numpy + <code>image_ops<\/code> (HSV <code>cvt_color<\/code>, cubic <code>resize<\/code>, <code>gaussian_blur<\/code>, <code>connected_components<\/code>, <code>outer_contours<\/code>), then <code>brush.Path<\/code>. Steps that use OpenCV functions outside <code>image_ops<\/code> (distanceTransformWithLabels, RETR_CCOMP, approxPolyDP) are written with numpy (line pixels to the nearest part via repeated dilation per label, holes from the outer contours of enclosed background components, Douglas-Peucker), or use OpenCV when it happens to be installed (optional).<\/li><li>Steps:<ul><li>Cut the reference per view (columns x0-x1), one head-top line and the same sole line for all views, same H scale.<\/li><li>HSV segmentation per part (hair, skin, shirt, accessories, trousers, shoes) + dark line pixels. Line pixels are assigned to the nearest part (<code>distanceTransformWithLabels<\/code>).<\/li><li>Masks upsampled 4x (cubic + Gaussian), then thresholded; contours (RETR_CCOMP, even-odd fill) smoothed with a Gaussian on the points, approxPolyDP, then Catmull-Rom → smooth vector paths.<\/li><li>Colours, gradients, shadows, uniform outline from the paint system (chapter 4); reference lines are not used raw except for thin elongated strokes (hair strands, folds).<\/li><li>Face = a new vector face placed at the eye/mouth positions found by detecting dark blobs inside the face; 3/4 eye perspective (far eye narrower), small profile eye at the front. Back view without a face.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Traps already found (automatic checks):<ul><li>The reference's floor shadow is read as body → drop low-saturation pixels near the sole.<\/li><li>Lighter hair strands read as trousers (similar colour) → similar colour above the shirt hem = hair; components touching the hair mass = hair.<\/li><li>Cheek shading read as hair → face = the largest head skin, closed, then holes filled (hole fill).<\/li><li>Nose/mouth lines make dents on the face profile edge → large closing limited to the non-hair foreground; if the dent remains (3/4), replace the face profile edge with the vector face curve (forehead, nose, lips, chin) over the trace mask.<\/li><li>Specks/small holes → fill holes &lt; 60 px, drop components &lt; 40 px (reference scale) for clothing and shoes.<\/li><li>White shoes become blobs → redraw the sole, toe, laces detail inside the traced shoe region.<\/li><li>Rough traced hands → replace with Shape Builder hands (chapter 3) at the traced position and size.<\/li><li>The hue of the dark part of one garment reads as another garment → hue ranges per body height.<\/li><li>Shirt details (hem band, cuff rib, seams, folds, accessory lines) = a measured spec on the H grid; automatic line detection (black-hat) proved to catch outline edges, not folds.<\/li><li>Traced face too rough → measured vector face (chapter 0).<\/li><li>Hair: strands = isolines parallel to the hair edge (several depths, dashed, dark + light); drop small loose hair pieces.<\/li><li>Flat style: traced contours look jagged without an outline → smooth the contour points more strongly (hair about 2x the body).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Poses that are not in the reference (e.g. outstretched arms): build from the traced front view + arm tubes, same style.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve14","title":"Rig from traced artwork (DeformSpace)","html":"<ul><li>All paths are built through <code>Space<\/code>; <code>DeformSpace<\/code> skins EVERY point (LBS) to the posed bones, weights by part (layer hint) + rest position. Traced contours are cached in H, so posing only moves anchors. The rest pose must be identical to the sheet.<\/li><li>Garments split for the rig: body + sleeves (measured seams, 2 px overlap) + hidden parts (chapter 0). When the arm goes up/bends: tube, rigid pieces, joint circle, pose-space shoulder.<\/li><li>Details and textures are drawn per part with that part's weights.<\/li><li>Change style (e.g. outline → flat) through a separate paint module; rig and geometry untouched, all motion clips take on the new style.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve15","title":"Flat vector layered per material + 3/4 walking view","html":"<p>Used when the user chooses a flat SVG style (people set, reading) and \"a fully new drawing with the pen tool, the SVG only for measuring\". The character spec stays in the project files.<\/p>\n<h4>14.1 Measure the SVG reference<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Render: <code>brush.draw_svg<\/code> (<code>scripts/svgref.py<\/code>): <code>skia.SVGDOM<\/code> on the skia engine, a numpy SVG interpreter on the pure engine (path, rect, circle, ellipse, line, polyline, polygon, g, use, transform, style, fill/stroke/opacity; gradients become flat colours). Three traps already found (svgref already handles them):<ul><li>CSS classes (<code>.st0{fill:..}<\/code>) must be inlined as style attributes;<\/li><li>colours <code>rgb(77.9%, 64.0%, 66.2%)<\/code> must be converted to hex, because without that SVGDOM fails silently (None).<\/li><li>(skia engine) <code>skia.MemoryStream(src.encode())<\/code> without a copy can read memory that was already freed, so it fails at random; keep the bytes in a variable and use <code>skia.MemoryStream(data, True)<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Measure with a width profile per row (mask of the background colour and the skin colour), not by guessing. Unit H_ref = hair top to chin.<\/li><li>People set style numbers (standing adult figure):<\/li><\/ul>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Landmark<\/th><th>H_ref<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Total height<\/td><td>5.3-5.8<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Shoulder top<\/td><td>1.25-1.30<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Crotch<\/td><td>about 3.38 (58.5% of the height from the top; realistic 50%)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Neck<\/td><td>0.17-0.18 wide<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Thigh<\/td><td>0.32-0.36 wide<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Ankle<\/td><td>0.12-0.18 wide<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fingertips<\/td><td>about 0.624 of the total height from the top<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Relaxed hand<\/td><td>about 0.5 long<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<ul><li>Always make a comparison board: reference figure and new figure at the same H scale, with landmark lines.<\/li><li>Large SVGs (stock illustrations of 3-6 MB) can make <code>brush.draw_svg<\/code> crash or return None (skia engine) or be very slow (pure engine); render them with headless Chrome to look at, parse the XML with Python for numbers (path count, solid vs gradient fill, stroke, opacity).<\/li><li>Character constructor kit style numbers (5 stock sheets, front view, unit H hair top to chin), as a comparison for an alternative style:<\/li><\/ul>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Landmark<\/th><th>Kit style<\/th><th>test character (people set)<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Total height<\/td><td>6.1-6.5 (6.8 with heels)<\/td><td>5.78<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Shoulders<\/td><td>1.26-1.31<\/td><td>1.27<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Crotch<\/td><td>3.10-3.45 (51-53%)<\/td><td>3.42 (59%)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fingertips<\/td><td>3.5-3.8<\/td><td>3.57<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Knee (rig pivot)<\/td><td>4.4-4.7<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Trouser hem<\/td><td>5.7-6.15<\/td><td>5.64<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Across 5 views<\/td><td>top, sole, shoulders max 0.05 H difference<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<h4>14.2 Tools (kit)<\/h4>\n<ul><li><code>Fig(cv, ox, oy, hp)<\/code> with a local matrix:<ul><li><code>rotated(px, py, deg, dx, dy)<\/code> for a tilted head, hands and a leaning upper body;<\/li><li><code>mirrored(cx)<\/code> for the view facing the other way.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><code>pen(\"M .. C .. Q .. L .. Z\")<\/code> makes an absolute SVG path in H. <code>ellipse()<\/code> only for eyes, cheeks, buttons, ground shadows.<\/li><li><code>shade(base, shape)<\/code> = fill shape clipped to base (shadows, cuff bands, soles).<\/li><li><code>ink(pts, w, color, alpha, clip)<\/code> = tapered Catmull-Rom stroke (profile sin^0.6), always clipped to its material.<\/li><li><code>union<\/code> / <code>diff<\/code> via <code>brush.union<\/code> / <code>brush.subtract<\/code> (<code>vkit.union<\/code>, <code>vkit.diff<\/code>). Parts cut by a sleeve: <code>diff(hand, sleeve)<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>14.3 Layer order (back to front)<\/h4>\n<ol><li>Ground shadow<\/li><li>Back hair (base colour, bottom part shaded)<\/li><li>Far leg + its shoe (3/4)<\/li><li>Inner collar of the outer shirt behind the neck, then the neck (+ shifted face shadow)<\/li><li>T-shirt (before the trousers when tucked in)<\/li><li>Bag and strap (behind the near leg when the bag is on the far side)<\/li><li>Trousers: pelvis + near leg, near shoe<\/li><li>Outer shirt: far panel + far arm (union, shared shoulder), then near panel + near arm (union, shared shoulder), cuffs, ink, buttons<\/li><li>Hands and fists, cut by their sleeves<\/li><li>Face, then front hair, fringe, visible ears<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>14.4 Relaxed standing front view<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Stance foot right under the neck root (check C with a vertical line).<\/li><li>Hip on the stance side up about 0.06 H; shoulder on that side down about 0.05 H; head tilted about 4 degrees.<\/li><li>Knee of the relaxed leg slightly inward; do not push the shin too far out, or the trousers read as flares.<\/li><li>Relaxed arms: elbow slightly bent, forearm pointing to the thigh, hand overlapping in front of the thigh side. This breaks two stiffly parallel arms.<\/li><li>Arm carrying a bag: almost straight, slightly out from the hip so the bag hangs free. Strap from the fist widening to the top corners of the bag.<\/li><li>The arm-body gap is a legitimate cavity. It holds background, not T-shirt or trousers. Make the T-shirt and the trouser pelvis narrower than the sides of the outer shirt body.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>14.5 3/4 walking view<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Draw from its own 3/4 skeleton (not a warp). Thigh and shin length equal to the front view (check the numbers).<\/li><li>Legs:<ul><li>near leg in front, straight, heel planted, shoe tip slightly raised;<\/li><li>back leg bent at the knee, heel lifted, resting on the toes;<\/li><li>the two legs cross visually;<\/li><li>far leg about 30% darker.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Arms opposite to the legs. The free arm swings; the arm carrying a bag only swings a little.<\/li><li>Upper body leans about 2 degrees in the walking direction: frame U rotated around the pelvis, the legs stay in frame F.<\/li><li>Width: 3/4 chest, waist, hips must not be narrower than the front view.<\/li><li>Shoulders:<ul><li>far panel and far arm share one shoulder curve (trapezius to deltoid);<\/li><li>near shoulder upright and somewhat square;<\/li><li>a far arm swinging forward is unioned with the far panel, then given a darker tone on <code>diff(arm, panel)<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>3/4 face (facing right, chin 1.0):<ul><li>far contour with forehead, cheek and chin pointing in the facing direction;<\/li><li>near eye rx about 0.028, far eye about 0.022-0.023, distance between eye centres about 0.21;<\/li><li>nose = a small hook stroke inside the face, near the far contour, not on its edge;<\/li><li>mouth shifted in the facing direction;<\/li><li>far cheek blush small and not cut by the hair;<\/li><li>near ear behind the jaw.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>3/4 neck comes out from behind the chin, slightly toward the nape, not right under the chin.<\/li><li>3/4 hair:<ul><li>round occiput tapering to the nape;<\/li><li>outer hair contour outside the skull, hairline inside the face;<\/li><li>long hair on the far side may cover the edge of the far cheek, but must not cover the far eye.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Motion folds of the outer shirt: 1-2 thin strokes (alpha 0.3-0.35) pulled toward the stepping leg.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>14.6 View facing the other way (mirror)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Design in the same frame (face toward +x), then <code>F.mirrored(0)<\/code>.<\/li><li>Shared: legs, pelvis, body, collar, neck, T-shirt, face.<\/li><li>Redrawn because the character is not symmetric:<ul><li>hair: parting, fringe direction, long side;<\/li><li>both arms and their swing;<\/li><li>bag;<\/li><li>button placement (stays on the same side of the body);<\/li><li>all shadows, so they stay on the side away from the top-left light. Example: the near-leg shadow moves to the back edge. The <code>shade_front<\/code> parameter is used for this.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>14.7 Hands, fists, shoes (people set style)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Relaxed hand:<ul><li>one shape widening smoothly from the forearm, convex back of the hand, tapering to a rounded tip;<\/li><li>one finger dip stroke;<\/li><li>length from the cuff about 0.45-0.5 H_ref.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Side-view fist:<ul><li>rounded back of the hand;<\/li><li>finger side with small 2-step stairs and 2 short strokes;<\/li><li>one thumb stroke across the top;<\/li><li>rounded bottom without a split;<\/li><li>finger side facing the thigh (front) or the walking direction (3/4).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Shoes:<ul><li>front = a dome with a flat sole + sole band + one toe cap line;<\/li><li>side = a local function (heel 0 to toe 0.43, sole y 0) with heel counter, collar dip, toe cap, laces, sole band, then rotated at the heel point (back foot about 33 degrees, front foot about -6 degrees).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>14.8 Special checks<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Enclosed cavities:<ul><li>render the figure on a pure green background, then <code>image_ops.connected_components(bg_mask)<\/code> (equivalent of connectedComponentsWithStats) on the background pixels; <code>cavity.py still<\/code> already does this;<\/li><li>components that do not touch the canvas edge = cavities;<\/li><li>legitimate cavities: gap between the legs, arm-body gap, bag strap triangle, space between bag and leg;<\/li><li>the rest (1-4 px) = defects and must be fixed.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Zoom every risky spot: head, near and far shoulder, fist, hand, waist (T-shirt-trousers, buttons), crotch, shoes.<\/li><li>Three-view sheet with landmark lines (hair top, chin, shoulders, crotch, sole): all landmarks aligned.<\/li><li>Package test: zip the project, extract to a new folder, render again, compare pixels with the delivery. The workspace does not carry over between sessions, so hand the zip to the user at the end of the session.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve16","title":"Side and back views","html":"<h4>15.1 When the reference does not contain that view<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Render all references and look for a pure view. If there is none, tell the user with options: continue from the character's own measurements, or the user sends a reference.<\/li><li>Build from the character's landmarks (top, chin, shoulders, crotch, sole), thigh/shin/arm lengths from the other views, and the widths of the other views.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>15.2 Side walking view (contact)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Skeleton:<ul><li>x 0 = hip joint, face toward +x;<\/li><li>2-bone IK, knee forward;<\/li><li>near leg in front landing on the heel, far leg behind resting on the toes;<\/li><li>sneaker rotated at the heel (front foot) or at the ball point (back foot);<\/li><li>bone lengths equal to the front view (check with the <code>skel<\/code> command).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Measure the stride length (chapter 0).<\/li><li>People set style profile depth (test character example): chest 0.57 H, cardigan body at the waist 0.54 H, hem 0.68 H. Naturally slimmer than the front-view width.<\/li><li>Trousers:<ul><li>leg built from bone stations (point, normal, front width, back width): root 0.23/0.24, thigh 0.185/0.19, above knee 0.155/0.16, knee 0.135, calf 0.128/0.148, bottom 0.132/0.142 H;<\/li><li>hem sits on the sneaker, back side lower than the front;<\/li><li>pelvis = its own piece unioned with the near leg, its bottom edge the buttock curve.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Open cardigan:<ul><li>front edge falls straight from the chest; the T-shirt only shows as a slice at the chest;<\/li><li>back hem widens to cover the buttocks, so the trouser pelvis does not peek out;<\/li><li>buttons are only drawn in views where the buttoned panel is on the near side.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Arms:<ul><li>tube from the shoulder-elbow-wrist bones (width 0.128/0.122/0.118 H) + a round deltoid cap over the shoulder pivot;<\/li><li>thin join line on the back side of the arm, over the body;<\/li><li>far arm darkened; cuff = a band perpendicular to the forearm.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Hands:<ul><li>relaxed side = back of the hand, fingers closed and converging, tube thumb in front, one finger gap + one stroke at the thumb root, rotated 0.6 x the forearm angle;<\/li><li>fist = the 3/4 fist shape, finger side toward the walking direction.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Side sneaker (length 0.56 H; 3/4 foreshortened to 0.43 H): heel counter, collar dipping down, tongue, vamp, round toe box, sole band, 2 laces, toe cap edge, heel stitching.<\/li><li>Profile head (chin 1.0):<ul><li>one contour: forehead, brow, eye socket, small button nose, lips, round chin;<\/li><li>small oval eye 0.08 H behind the front edge; short brow; blush; smile-line mouth;<\/li><li>ear at the middle of the head depth (x 0), y 0.51-0.77;<\/li><li>neck comes out from behind the jaw, its front line sloping from under the chin.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Hair:<ul><li>one shared outer contour (fringe, top, occiput about -0.50 H);<\/li><li>tucked side: hair curves over the ear then tight behind it, nape slightly flicked;<\/li><li>long side: covers the ear down to the shoulder, its front edge covers the back of the cheek, ends flicked backward; fringe sweeps toward the long side;<\/li><li>a long side on the far side shows as a dark layer behind the nape, with the same silhouette.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Bag turned 45 degrees: face + gusset, gusset on the side away from the light.<\/li><li>View facing the other way: skeleton mirrored. What is shared and what is redrawn follows chapter 14.6.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>15.3 Extra tools<\/h4>\n<ul><li><code>spline(pts, corners)<\/code>: Catmull-Rom to cubic, with zero handles at corner anchors, for contours from measured points.<\/li><li><code>form_shade(F, region, ...)<\/code>: shadow = region minus a copy shifted toward the light in screen space (px). The result stays correct in mirrored views.<\/li><li><code>leg_path<\/code> (bone stations) and <code>sleeve_path<\/code> (tube + deltoid): shapes computed from the skeleton, so they are ready to animate right away (motion-graphics chapter 4.3 (<code>motion-graphics/references/walk.md<\/code>)).<\/li><li>6-view turn-order sheet + landmark lines; labelled zoom sheet (image, H box, label, scale).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>15.4 Standing back view<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Back silhouette = geometric mirror of the front silhouette. Copy the front-view path, draw it through <code>mirrored(0)<\/code>; the character's right moves to the viewer's right.<\/li><li>Content that is redrawn:<ul><li>back hair covers the head; tucked side tight so the ear edge shows; bottom edge smoothed without hooks; shadow only a band at the bottom edge;<\/li><li>cardigan back = union of panel + bridge + hem band; thin rib collar;<\/li><li>fist on the little-finger side without knuckles; relaxed hand with finger dips on the inner side;<\/li><li>shoes: heel counter stitching + tab; trousers: short centre seam under the hem + fold behind the knee;<\/li><li>cast shadow of the hair onto the shoulders; nape hollow.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve17","title":"Hybrid multi-angle rig for orbits","html":"<p>Used when the camera circles the character (16 angles or continuous). Options offered to the user: A new drawing per angle (slowest), B full 3D rig (risk of geometric shapes), C hybrid (chosen). The rig is used for ALL angles, including angles that have a hand drawing, so the style does not jump; the hand drawings become loft references and comparisons.<\/p>\n<h4>16.1 Skeleton and projection<\/h4>\n<ul><li>One 3D skeleton in the body frame: X forward (face +X), L lateral (+ = character's right), y downward from the hair top. Leg and arm joints from the side-view gait engine (motion-graphics chapter 4.3 (<code>motion-graphics/references/walk.md<\/code>)) + measured lateral offsets (test character example: pelvis +-0.16, knee x 0.92, ankle x 0.85; shoulders +-0.40, elbow x 1.10, wrist x 1.18-1.30).<\/li><li>Camera angle relative to the character v (0 front, 90 = 90R, 180 back, 270 = 90L). Local screen: u = X sin v - L cos v. Depth toward the camera = X cos v + L sin v. A normal (nX, nL) faces the camera when nX cos v + nL sin v &gt; 0.<\/li><li>Projected body lean: screen rotation = lean x sin v.<\/li><li>Body projection orthographic; in a perspective scene measure foot slip during integration.<ul><li>Test project measurements (camera 14 H, height 6.53 H, f 1500): side view (sideways walking beat) slip 1.15 px; orbit with the camera in front/behind 57 px. The reason: a ground point moving forward/backward by Δd H in depth should shift vertically by about f x h_camera x Δd / distance^2 (about 48 px per H), while the billboard draws everything at pelvis depth.<\/li><li>Perspective correction ONLY on the legs (weighted 0 at the pelvis to 1 at the sole) removes slip (0.2 px) but lengthens the legs by 0.3-0.5 H when the camera is in front/behind, because the body stays orthographic and the leg width is not scaled. Rejected (\"wrecked\"). To test next: correct the whole figure (position AND scale per point from its depth), or stay orthographic with a billboard anchor that follows the stance foot (smooth blend during double support). Always compare scene frames with the accepted version (chapter 11 items 28-29).<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>16.2 Legs, arms, shoes<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Leg = a 2-bone tube from the projected joints, same stations as leg_path. Projected width of each station: sqrt((w_side sin v)^2 + (w_front cos v)^2); front and back swap on the left side.<\/li><li>Arm = sleeve_path from the projected joints; arm-body join line on the back side of the near arm (alpha follows |sin v|, gone in the front view).<\/li><li>Hands per angle (tested in the rig v4 test project):<ul><li>shape keys from the reference drawings at v 0 / 90 / 180 / 270; the back and left-side views in the reference drawings are usually mirrors (F.mirrored), so their keys = mirrors of the front/side contour;<\/li><li>relaxed hand: front → side → mirrored front → mirrored side; fist: the same fist silhouette in front and side (only the knuckle strokes), mirrored at the back and the left side, with a little-finger-side stroke at the back;<\/li><li>between keys: the contour is morphed (resample N points, chapter 6, smoothstep per 90 degrees), strokes are crossfaded (alpha x weight), no toggles;<\/li><li>the kit style uses 5 drawings by the facing direction of the HAND (0 thumb side, 45, 90 back of the hand, 135, 180 palm side) + mirrors; with a morph within +-11 degrees around the boundary when there is no in-between drawing.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Shoe = the side sneaker profile x lateral width per point, rotated by the gait cfg, projected convex hull, then smoothed. The sole underside shows fully when the bottom normal of the shoe faces the camera (heel lifted, camera behind); otherwise the sole band = full silhouette minus the silhouette of the part above the midsole (sole points raised to local y -0.032, a copy shifted 0.04 H up unioned so the top edge leaves no slivers).<ul><li>Details = curves on the 3D shoe surface (px, py profile, s lateral -1..1), shown per segment when facing the camera: U-shaped toe cap edge (top centre px 0.37, sides px 0.50 near the sole), 2 laces across the instep (shown when the instep tilts toward the camera as the heel rises, or in side view), heel counter stitching (back view).<\/li><li>Kit style for the front view and toe-off: a two-part shoe, the toe dome stays on the floor (wider than the ankle, height/width about 0.6, angled outward 10-15 degrees) + heel/collar rotating behind it; sole band only under the dome, toe cap shine + 2 lace strokes. From behind: heel counter, shoe tip peeking out to the side. Not yet tested on the test character rig.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>16.3 Loft volume from two accepted drawings<\/h4>\n<ul><li>For face, hair, outer shirt body, T-shirt, neck, pelvis: render the front-view and side-view paths to a mask (200 px/H), take xmin/xmax per row (step 0.005 H). Reference frame without head tilt/lean.<\/li><li>Per row: centre and half depth from the side view (Xc, d), centre and half width from the front view (Lc = -(xmin + xmax)/2, w). Superellipse cross-section; projected edge u = (Xc sin v - Lc cos v) +- (|d sin v|^m + |w cos v|^m)^(1/m), m = dual exponent. Test character example: face/hair/neck 2.0, body 1.25 (so 3/4 is not narrower than the front), pelvis 1.3, T-shirt 1.6. At v 0 and 90 the result equals the reference drawing.<\/li><li>The silhouette is subsampled every 4 rows, then splined. On the skia engine PathOps on dense silhouettes can fail: try/except + <code>brush.simplify<\/code>; the pure engine (exact pathfinder per sample) needs no fallback.<\/li><li>The nose is not part of the loft (front depth smoothed at the nose rows); nose = a projected profile polygon unioned into the skin only when sin v &gt; 0.5; below that = a small hook stroke.<\/li><li>Asymmetric hair (long side vs tucked side) comes automatically from the left/right widths of the front view and the side profile (crown + long side).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>16.4 Surface features<\/h4>\n<ul><li>3D feature points on the front surface of the volume (X from the superellipse at that row and lateral): eyes (rx = rx_front x (0.55 + 0.45 x facing), hidden when facing &lt;= 0), brows, cheeks, mouth (3 points), ears (profile ear points with L 0.30-0.38; shown when sin v &gt; 0.3; shaded from behind), edges of the outer shirt opening + buttons (shown when facing &gt; 0.15).<\/li><li>Open shirt opening = a polygon between the two projected panel edges, intersected with the body silhouette; filled with the T-shirt above the waist, trousers below.<\/li><li>Bag = a 3D box (face x thickness x height), swinging on a spring at the fist. The face and gusset shown = the normals facing the camera (not the screen span, chapter 0). Light face (base colour), hatched gusset (same as the reference drawing); the gusset hatch covers the silhouette bulge on the outer side and widens smoothly as the face turns almost side-on. Strap ends at the face edge, shifting smoothly to the gusset edge when the gusset dominates (smoothstep gusset ratio 0.35-0.65), otherwise both straps merge into one line. Face folds fade as the face narrows. The yaw is picked by the user from a comparison sheet per angle (<code>tools/tascek.py<\/code>): the hand reference drawings cannot all be satisfied by one 3D box (test project: -30 chosen).<\/li><li>Strokes on the surface (hair strands, fabric folds, rib collar, shoe details) = 3D points (ring angle, y) on the loft volume; only parts facing the camera (facing &gt; 0.12) are drawn, alpha fading <code>min(1, (mean facing - 0.12) / 0.33)<\/code> so they do not pop in/out during the orbit.<\/li><li>Hair strands: convert the strokes of the reference drawings (front, right side, left side, back) to (hair ring angle, y); drop points below the row where the ring narrows toward the hair ends. The kit style adds a shine band across the crown (dL* +7..+15, depth 0.15-0.5 H, thickness 0.12-0.17 H), also visible from behind, so the back hair does not read as a flat block. Shadow under the hair = silhouette minus its copy shifted 0.05 H up.<\/li><li>Mouth: see chapter 0 (smooth shift from the front point to the profile stroke). Neck: chin shadow from the face loft shifted 0.075 H, clipped to the neck.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>16.5 Hairline (front hair covering the face)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Hairline = a TABLE of y per face ring angle th (0 front, 90 character's right, 180 back, -90 left) wrapping around the head, not a list of front-view points. Surface point: X = xc + d cos th, L = lc + w sin th from the ring row (interpolated between rows), normal (cos th / d, sin th / w).<\/li><li>Calibrate from each reference drawing: side view X → th = acos((X - xc)/d) (sign = side), front/back view L → th = asin((L - lc)/w) (back 180 - asin). When two references conflict (hand drawings are not 3D-consistent), write down the compromise and which angle gives way. Test character example: a step at the fringe end near the parting (th 31-34, forehead open below the parting); tucked side drops behind the ear to the nape (th 135-141); long side covers the back of the cheek (th -64 to -120).<\/li><li>Cover polygon: points on the arc facing the camera (the longest continuous run containing th = v), in ascending order; both ends (at the silhouette edge) closed horizontally outward, then upward. Points whose y is below the face use the last ring that is still as wide as the head (test character 0.85), because below it the rings narrow toward the chin and the back points get pulled forward (a hair blade under the jaw).<\/li><li>Cover = polygon intersect (face skin union neck root down to the nape). The skin drawn = face minus the POLYGON (chapter 0). The cover is filled with hair, then given the same hair shadow (bottom shadow, shade against the light), so there is no plain patch over shaded hair. The hair still has no form shade (chapter 0).<\/li><li>Back view (cos v &lt; -0.17): the scalp is drawn, then the full hair silhouette on top; cheeks/ears sticking out of the hair silhouette stay visible.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>16.6 Layer order per angle<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Back hair (not the back view) → far-side legs + arms (darker) → neck (+ chin shadow) → bag when on the far side or front 3/4 → near leg + pelvis → bag (side view) → body + hem band + folds + opening → near arm + join line + hand → head (hair cover over the face and nape, skin, hair strands, features, ears).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>16.7 Concept test (required before producing all angles)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Angle sheet 0, 22.5, 45, 67.5, 90, 135 + reference drawings 45 and 90 side by side.<\/li><li>Walk strip of 1/8 cycle at 3 new angles.<\/li><li>360-degree turn video while walking in place (continuous angle) to find broken angles.<\/li><li>Gapcheck every frame of each angle (<code>scripts/cavity.py frames module:draw_angle n<\/code>, project example: <code>tools/rigcheck.py v n threshold<\/code>); classify legitimate cavities (bag strap triangle, gap between the legs, bag-leg gap, arm-body gap).<\/li><li>Write down the weaknesses: hands per angle, hair strands, shirt details, toe-off shoes, light per angle.<\/li><li>The scene test (chapter 11 item 28) is part of the concept test: a rig that passes the angle sheet has not necessarily passed in orbit frames.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>16.8 Method status (test project Review Results)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Concept test v1 accepted; bag v3 accepted (face from normals, yaw -30).<\/li><li>The full v4 overhaul (hairline table, hair strands, 3D shoes, hands per angle, cardigan details, world light, standing pose + 0.3 s blend between actions, leg perspective correction) was stopped by the user after seeing the orbit frames: \"wrecked\". Proven cause: perspective correction only on the legs lengthens the legs in the front/back views. Also visible in both versions: the loft body from front/back is narrow and stiff compared with the hand drawings. The angle sheet and zooms looked better, the scene frames did not; that is the reason for rule 00.7.<\/li><li>Before continuing with this method, measure the loft proportions in scene frames (body width, leg length in H) at 0/180 against the hand drawings; if the difference is large, offer the user option A (new drawing per key angle + morph/swap) or a kit-style 2D piece rig (chapter 5.1) per view.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve18","title":"Bundled scripts (`scripts/`)","html":"<p>General tools from the test projects, cleaned up so they do not depend on a specific character. Character drawings, sizes, palettes and special rigs stay in the project.<\/p>\n<p>How to use them in a new project:<\/p>\n<ol><li>Copy this skill's <code>scripts/<\/code> folder into the project folder (e.g. <code>tools/vc/<\/code>). The copy in the skill is not changed; tool fixes are noted and brought into the skill through a skill update.<\/li><li>Install once: <code>pip install -r tools/vc/requirements.txt<\/code> (numpy + Pillow); <code>skia-python<\/code> optional.<\/li><li>In the project module: <code>sys.path.insert(0, \"tools/vc\")<\/code>, then <code>import brush<\/code>, <code>from vkit import Fig<\/code>, <code>import vkit<\/code>, <code>import loft<\/code>.<\/li><li>Project drawing functions are written as <code>draw(cv, ox, oy, hp)<\/code> (still) or <code>draw(cv, ox, oy, hp, p)<\/code> (animation) with <code>cv<\/code> = <code>brush.Canvas<\/code> and shapes as <code>brush.Path<\/code>, so they can be checked with <code>cavity.py<\/code> right away.<\/li><li>Run each script's demo once in the work folder with <code>python -I<\/code> (isolated mode; the scripts find their sibling modules themselves).<\/li><li><code>brush.py<\/code>, <code>image_ops.py<\/code>, <code>cavity.py<\/code> and <code>NOTICE<\/code> are copies of one shared source with the motion-graphics skill; do not edit the copies (sync flow in <code>scripts/README.md<\/code>).<\/li><\/ol>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Script<\/th><th>Contents<\/th><th>Check/demo<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>vkit.py<\/td><td><code>Fig<\/code> (<code>rotated<\/code>, <code>mirrored<\/code>, <code>pen<\/code>, <code>poly<\/code>, <code>ellipse<\/code>, <code>fill<\/code>, <code>shade<\/code>, <code>ink<\/code>), <code>union<\/code>/<code>inter<\/code>/<code>diff<\/code>/<code>sop<\/code>/<code>union_all<\/code>, <code>area<\/code> (check for holes in unions), <code>spline<\/code> (corner anchors), <code>form_shade(light=)<\/code>, <code>tube_stations<\/code>/<code>tube_path<\/code>, <code>ik2<\/code>, <code>mix<\/code>, <code>to_array<\/code><\/td><td><code>python -I vkit.py demo out.png<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>loft.py<\/td><td>Hybrid multi-angle rig (chapter 16): <code>extents<\/code>, <code>rings_from(front, side)<\/code>, <code>flatten_front<\/code> (nose), <code>outline(rings, v, m)<\/code>, <code>outline_path<\/code>, <code>surf_point<\/code>, <code>proj<\/code>, <code>depth<\/code>, <code>facing<\/code>, <code>proj_widths<\/code>, <code>hull<\/code>; <code>CANON<\/code> (7.5-head canon)<\/td><td><code>python -I loft.py demo out.png<\/code> (prints the edge difference v 0 vs front and v 90 vs side)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>cavity.py<\/td><td>Gapcheck <code>still<\/code> (enclosed cavities + loose pieces + 15% silhouette + marked PNG) and <code>frames<\/code> (all animation frames); one source with motion-graphics<\/td><td><code>python -I cavity.py still module:draw out<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>zoomsheet.py<\/td><td>Labelled zoom sheet of risky spots from one or more images (old vs new regression); boxes in H (<code>--origin<\/code>, <code>--hp<\/code>)<\/td><td>`python -I zoomsheet.py out.png 1600 \"img<\/td><td>x0,y0,x1,y1<\/td><td>label<\/td><td>3\"`<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>svgref.py<\/td><td>Render a reference SVG (inline CSS, percent colours, copied stream), <code>palette<\/code>, <code>profile<\/code> per row<\/td><td><code>python -I svgref.py render in.svg out.png 1600<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>shapes.py<\/td><td>Kit-style shapes: <code>cap<\/code> (tangent capsule + <code>cut<\/code>), <code>seg_caps<\/code> (finger chains), <code>curtain2<\/code> (hair curtain: designed dip profile + strand ends + separation lines), <code>curtain<\/code>, <code>lock<\/code>/<code>wave_spine<\/code>/<code>offset_line<\/code><\/td><td>import + example in the docstring<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>styles.py<\/td><td>\"Hand\" style layer: <code>organic<\/code> (subtly wobbling edges based on arc length, stable when pieces rotate), <code>hand_stroke<\/code> (pressure strokes), <code>texture<\/code> (grain + colour mottling, only when asked)<\/td><td>installed by wrapping <code>Fig.fill/shade/ink<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>measure_ref.py<\/td><td>MEASURE references (chapter 18.3): <code>masks<\/code> (hair/skin by colour), <code>largest<\/code>, <code>profile<\/code> (width per row in H), <code>hairline<\/code> (hairline per x), <code>overlay<\/code> (reference outline over the character render)<\/td><td><code>python -I measure_ref.py mask ref.png x0 y0 x1 y1 4 out<\/code>, then <code>profile out 304<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>brush.py<\/td><td>Vector drawing layer: <code>Surface<\/code> (<code>canvas<\/code>, <code>array()<\/code>, <code>save<\/code>), <code>Canvas<\/code>, <code>Path<\/code> (+ <code>from_svg<\/code>), <code>Paint<\/code> (<code>style<\/code>, <code>cap<\/code>, <code>join<\/code>, <code>blend<\/code>), gradients, <code>Matrix<\/code>, <code>Font<\/code>, <code>Image<\/code>, pathfinder <code>union<\/code> / <code>intersect<\/code> / <code>subtract<\/code> / <code>exclude<\/code> / <code>union_all<\/code> / <code>simplify<\/code>, <code>measure<\/code> (PathMeasure), <code>draw_svg<\/code>; skia or pure engine (<code>MGP_BRUSH_ENGINE<\/code>, <code>MGP_BRUSH_SUPERSAMPLE<\/code>)<\/td><td>examples in <code>scripts/README.md<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>image_ops.py<\/td><td>numpy + Pillow image tools replacing OpenCV: <code>read<\/code> / <code>write<\/code>, <code>cvt_color<\/code> (HSV), <code>resize<\/code>, <code>gaussian_blur<\/code>, <code>warp_affine<\/code>, <code>connected_components<\/code> (connectedComponentsWithStats), <code>outer_contours<\/code> (outer findContours), <code>draw_contours<\/code>, <code>convex_hull<\/code>, <code>line<\/code>, <code>rectangle<\/code><\/td><td>used by <code>cavity.py<\/code>, <code>measure_ref.py<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>requirements.txt, README.md<\/td><td>Required packages (numpy, Pillow) and optional ones (skia-python); command list, engine choice, pure engine notes<\/td><td><code>pip install -r scripts/requirements.txt<\/code><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Regression test against the test character pipeline (2026-10-30):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Script<\/th><th>Result<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>loft.py<\/td><td>6 volumes (face, hair, body, T-shirt, neck, pelvis): silhouettes at 8 angles identical to the project rig; one face row (y 0.83) differs by 0.005 H (1 px rasterisation)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>loft.py demo<\/td><td>Loft silhouette edge difference vs reference drawing: 95th percentile 0.005-0.010 H, max 0.015-0.050 H (4-row subsampling + spline)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>cavity.py<\/td><td><code>still<\/code> and <code>frames<\/code> identical to the project check (cavities, marked PNG, 15% silhouette)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>svgref.py<\/td><td>Renders of 5 reference SVGs identical to the project tool (3 runs)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>shapes.py, styles.py<\/td><td>Copies of the kit test project tools (kit v3-v15); import + capsule, curtain, organic, hand_stroke examples run with the package vkit (2026-10-30)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>measure_ref.py<\/td><td>Kit turnaround front head (4x, H 304): top +-0.22, cheeks +-0.47, hairline dropping steeply at the temples, same as the project measurement<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"ve19","title":"End-to-end character kit","html":"<p>One female character in character constructor kit style from scratch to a full kit sheet. The character spec (palette, body) is in the project; here are the method and the kit style numbers.<\/p>\n<h4>18.1 The order that was finally accepted<\/h4>\n<ol><li>Brief (AskUserQuestion): style, persona, set contents, outfit, hair, palette, skin; then the body type from the comparison sheet (chapter 3).<\/li><li>Front/side/back piece kit (chapter 5.1) → proportion mode → measure the kit (total height, fingertips, hem, side depth) → pieces at final size.<\/li><li>Style layer (18.2), kit-style face, hands (18.4), hair (18.3).<\/li><li>Expressions + mouths (18.5), gestures (18.6), 3/4 rig + turnaround (18.7), full kit sheet (18.8).<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>Every step is sent with a before/after comparison and (for style) a side-by-side comparison with the reference at the same H scale.<\/p>\n<h4>18.2 Style layer (without changing geometry)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Wrap <code>Fig.fill/shade/ink<\/code>: small organic edges (long wave 0.003 H, cached per path so the fill, line clip and shadow use the same edge), pressure strokes without centre-line wobble, no grain (review decision), saturation on par with the reference (take the median reference colour per material).<\/li><li>Proportion mode from the same geometry: base colour → gray per material, lines and contact turned off.<\/li><li>Arm contact line over a body of the same colour: the sleeve edge is lined in the material line colour, only inside the body (an arm crossing the body does not disappear).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>18.3 Hair (the longest problem: \"like a hat/wig\")<\/h4>\n<ul><li>MEASURE first (<code>measure_ref.py<\/code>). Female kit head numbers (H = hair top to chin): hair top +-0.23 at y 0.08 (round, narrow); hairline peaks at the parting (y about 0.18-0.21) then drops steeply to cover the temples (x +-0.20 → y 0.31-0.38, x +-0.24 → y 0.38-0.54); cheek sides +-0.45-0.48; volume at the jaw/shoulders +-0.55-0.66 with a large S curve; ears in front of the hair.<\/li><li>Proven causes of the \"wig\": wide flat top, forehead open to the temples (hairline following the top of the face), straight sides without jaw volume, 3/4 without far-side hair, back shaped like a diagonal cloak, small repeating waves.<\/li><li>What failed (do not repeat): LIGHT strand lines on dark hair (reads as wire); a shine equally large on both sides without a light direction; pulling the hair tight to the head (becomes a helmet); layered strands from narrow capsules (become ropes/dreadlocks); wrong H when measuring (chin detected automatically from the skin width → all numbers off by 20%; measure H by hand).<\/li><li>One light direction (top left) for all views: shine = a wide light band across the crown, cut by DARK strand lines (only inside the band); light side wider/lighter; form shadow = mass minus a copy shifted toward the light (screen space). Dark strand lines dL* -7, shine dL* +15 from the base (kit: L* 28 / 21 / 43).<\/li><li>Curtain (<code>shapes.curtain2<\/code>): dip profile with 2 large S curves starting at the jaw (amplitude 0.05-0.06 H), 3-4 strand ends with different width/drop/flick, separation lines from notches; back view two symmetric curtains overlapping in the middle; 3/4 far-side hair frames the far cheek; side: volume at the nape (not tucked into the nape), then lying on the backpack/back.<\/li><li>Hair side tucked behind the shoulder: the front part ends in a dip above the shoulder, the join behind the shoulder (drawn before the body) wide and jutting toward the neck so a head tilt does not open a gap.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>18.4 Hands and grips<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Kit relaxed hand: one pen-silhouette shape (loose C grip, base with 3 stepped finger dips, thumb lobe) of 0.29 x 0.58 H; finger separation = dark skin TONE WEDGES (wide at the notch, thinning toward the root), short curves following the contour. Rejected: 3-segment capsules + nails + knuckles (\"AI\"), straight slanted lines (\"cat scratches\"), lines too long, hand too large/narrow.<\/li><li>Object grip: palm (a capsule from the wrist computed back from the hand frame) behind the object + a jagged fingertip blob at the front edge of the object (a wedge in each notch) + thick thumb lobe; all in a world-UPRIGHT frame at the object centre (the object does not tilt with the arm; for a mirrored frame the rotation sign is flipped). Phone: screen visible as in the kit, palm behind, fingertips on one edge, thumb on the other edge; 3/4 = narrower + thickness side; side = thin edge, slanted. Glass: fingers wrapping in front, thumb on top. Carried book: fingers bent in front of the top edge.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>18.5 Face, expressions, mouths<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Kit face: upright oval eyes without shine (0.021 x 0.039 H), short thick capsule brows (not tapered lines), nose one C line from the brow root to the nostril wing, full lips + an upper lip line, cheek blush.<\/li><li>12 expressions through brows (raised, worried, angry, flat, one-sided), lids (^ arc, closed, half), side glance, mouth. 3/4: far eye x0.76, front-set mouth shortened 0.80 on the 3/4 face centre line.<\/li><li>12 visemes + a head without a mouth. Open mouth = dark cavity + teeth + tongue + lower lip ATTACHED (not a floating line); O/U/QW = a whole oval (not a heart shape).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>18.6 Gestures<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Whole arm = the same rig pieces rotated at the shoulder + elbow, the gesture hand attached through a hook (HAND_HOOK) without changing other pieces; 3/4 and side views use the same hook (shoulder/elbow pose per view; side mirrored → the angle sign and the upright rotation are flipped).<\/li><li>Thumbs up: forearm upright, thumb short and thick (a long one reads as an index finger). Thick index finger (not a needle).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>18.7 3/4 rig and turnaround<\/h4>\n<ul><li>3D skeleton from the front + side rig joints; body/pelvis = loft (<code>loft.rings_from<\/code>) superellipse m 1.25-1.3; legs/arms = tangent capsules at the projected joints (radius sqrt(w^2 + d^2)/sqrt2); 3/4 shoes are DRAWN (the convex hull of 3D points reads as a box); backpack = a 3D box with dark side panels; front details (collar, straps, pockets) projected onto the volume surface and clipped to the body.<\/li><li>Turnaround: side, front 3/4, front, back 3/4, back, same landmark lines (top, chin, shoulders, hem, crotch, fingertips, knees, trouser hem, sole).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>18.8 Full kit sheet<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Components rendered fresh from code (turnaround, piece kit + joint test, 3/4 rig + heads at 4 angles + hands, expressions + visemes + 3/4 expressions + head without a mouth, front + 3/4/side gestures), laid out with a title, character summary, hex palette; the components are also saved separately.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>18.9 Working with the user at the style stage<\/h4>\n<ul><li>The user's style comments are short and get harsher when repeated (\"AI banget\" (so AI), \"kaku\" (stiff), \"wig\", \"aduh.. jari2\" (ugh.. the fingers), \"LO RISET NDIRI\" (do your own research)). Meaning: stop fiddling with numbers, measure the reference, find the cause of the shape, fix it at once in all views, send a comparison. Do not offer options that were already rejected (e.g. a hair colour that is already locked).<\/li><li>When the user says \"stop\", stop the experiment, do not put it into the kit, report that the last accepted delivery is unchanged.<\/li><\/ul>"},{"id":"ve20","title":"Character style variants: vector, pixel art, origami (Version 2.1, 05-11-2026)","html":"<p>One character, one skeleton, three ways of drawing. The skeleton and pose do not change between styles; only the drawing module changes (the same principle as the style layer in chapters 5 and 18.2). The examples below come from the guide site's code demo, side view facing right, units 100 = body height, y downward, feet at y 0. Motion per style: motion-graphics skill chapter 12.5 (<code>motion-graphics/references/scene-recipes.md<\/code>); production rules: motion-graphics-pro skill T14, T15.<\/p>\n<h4>19.1 Shared skeleton<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Side-view joints:<ul><li>pelvis (y -46 when standing);<\/li><li>neck 24 from the pelvis, shoulder 21 from the pelvis;<\/li><li>thigh 21, shin 20, foot 10;<\/li><li>upper arm 15, forearm 14;<\/li><li>head centre 13 above the neck.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>The stance foot is always pinned to the ground after the pose is computed.<\/li><li>Palette per material, each with a shadow pair: skin, hair (+ light hair), top, trousers, shoes, plus ink, cheeks and mouth.<\/li><li>Far-side legs and arms use the shadow colour.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>19.2 Vector (reference)<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Legs and arms = round-cap strokes over an ink stroke that is 2.4 thicker.<\/li><li>Body = a thick stroke.<\/li><li>Head a circle r 11.5 + hair cap and fringe from quadratic curves.<\/li><li>Face: ear, elliptical eyes (blink = height x 0.18), brows, cheeks alpha 0.5, smile.<\/li><li>Flat colour per material.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>19.3 Pixel art (derived from the same vector drawing)<\/h4>\n<ol><li>Draw the vector character small without ink (scale 0.48, about 48 pixels tall). The face is simplified to one eye block (blink = flattened block); cheeks and mouth are not drawn.<\/li><li>Alpha threshold: &lt; 120 becomes fully transparent, the rest fully opaque. No semi-transparent pixels.<\/li><li>Every pixel is locked to a fixed palette of 12 colours (skin, dark skin, hair, light hair, top, dark top, trousers, dark trousers, shoes, dark shoes, cheeks, mouth). Colour distance weighted by luminance 0.3 / 0.59 / 0.11.<\/li><li>A 1-pixel edge line in the ink colour on every empty pixel that neighbours (4 directions) a filled pixel.<\/li><li>Enlarge by an integer scale, nearest neighbour; positions on whole pixels.<\/li><li>Check: the palette does not grow, no mixed pixels, edge lines closed, feet on the same ground row in every drawing.<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>19.4 Origami (folded paper planes)<\/h4>\n<ol><li><strong>A kite per segment.<\/strong> Every leg and arm segment is made from joint points A and B + side points at 42% of the length (width w).<ul><li>Split along the fold line A-B: a light half (base colour) and a dark half (shadow colour).<\/li><li>Far legs/arms mixed 22% toward dark.<\/li><li>Widths: thigh 11, shin 9.5, upper arm 8.5, forearm 6.5.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><strong>Body and head.<\/strong><ul><li>Body = a fan of 6 triangles around the body centre, tones alternating light/base/dark.<\/li><li>Head = 6 triangles around the centre (one lightest plane facing the light).<\/li><li>Hair = 3 triangles; neck, hands, shoes = one triangle.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li><strong>Fold lines.<\/strong> Thin light fold lines (0.5 px, cream alpha 0.38), no thick ink lines.<\/li><li><strong>Face.<\/strong> Small triangular eyes (blink = flattened triangle) and triangular cheeks; they appear after the planes have finished unfolding.<\/li><li><strong>Back side.<\/strong> Cream (#f4ead6) with brownish fold lines; visible when a plane flips over.<\/li><li><strong>Check.<\/strong> Every plane has a light-dark pair, the number of planes stays the same across drawings, the silhouette still reads as the same character as the vector version.<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>19.5 When to choose<\/h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Vector:<\/strong> the default, most flexible for subtle acting.<\/li><li><strong>Pixel art:<\/strong> gaming/retro/cozy content, small and cheap assets.<\/li><li><strong>Origami:<\/strong> meaningful messages, event openers, process education, fold transitions.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>The style choice is confirmed by the user at the taste stage. The style can change without touching the rig; after changing style, re-render the clips and repeat the self-check (chapter 11).<\/p>"}],"title":"Vector Character (drawing characters in code)","intro":"<p>General rules for any character (people, workers, mascots, illustration characters, animated characters). Distilled from 27 videos (Cosmin Serban, Jess/Envato, Audrey Stemen/School of Motion, Neda Draws, Mark Rise/Gigantic, Fd4IOI-82CQ, Ben Marriott, Puppet Warp Adobe) and the Review Results of several character projects. Features, sizes and colours specific to one character are NOT written in this skill; they are measured from that project's brief/references and recorded in the project files (character spec). To animate the character, continue with the motion-graphics skill.<\/p>"}});
